import{J as e,n as t,ot as n,q as r}from"./chunks/framework.NDkVE8yV.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Entity and tick re-architecture: one entity model, one tick pipeline","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Entity and tick re-architecture: one entity model, one tick pipeline"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.dev/plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.dev/plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture.md","filePath":"plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture.md","lastUpdated":1794458512000}`),a={name:`plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="entity-and-tick-re-architecture-one-entity-model-one-tick-pipeline" tabindex="-1">Entity and tick re-architecture: one entity model, one tick pipeline <a class="header-anchor" href="#entity-and-tick-re-architecture-one-entity-model-one-tick-pipeline" aria-label="Permalink to “Entity and tick re-architecture: one entity model, one tick pipeline”">​</a></h1><p>Status: in progress. The decisions were taken on 2026-11-10 and this plan was written the same day. Counts in this page are from that day. Slices 0 to 2 have landed on the program branch (<code>t3/subagent-architecture-review</code>, pull request #2948), and slices 3 to 5 were in progress when work moved to another machine on 2026-11-10. <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/plans/entity-simulation-rearchitecture.handoff.html">The handoff</a> says where each package stands and how to pick it up.</p><p>The server runs two simulations of the same world and reconciles them every tick. Every data-driven mob lives once in <code>EntityRuntime</code> and a second time as a legacy body in the world&#39;s entity map, and health crosses between the two by comparison. Two entity indexes answer the same question under opposite rules. A tick awaits at 233 points, so a packet handler can change a player in the middle of it. This plan replaces all of that with one entity store, one index, a synchronous tick, and replication that reads a change log.</p><p>The decisions are in ADRs 0006 to 0018. This page holds what does not fit an ADR: what the review changed, the order of the work, how each slice is judged, and what is still unknown.</p><h2 id="priorities" tabindex="-1">Priorities <a class="header-anchor" href="#priorities" aria-label="Permalink to “Priorities”">​</a></h2><ol><li>The server feels like Minecraft and mostly is Minecraft, and it keeps up with Mojang&#39;s releases. That is why behavior comes from data and not from reimplementation.</li><li>Performance, a close second.</li><li>The program finishes. No slice ends with mobs on one model and players on another, and the slices that create a temporary mix are planned as one commitment.</li></ol><h2 id="decisions" tabindex="-1">Decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#decisions" aria-label="Permalink to “Decisions”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0006-replay-determinism-contract.html">ADR 0006</a>: a replay gives identical digests at any host speed.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0007-one-entity-index-visible-on-write.html">ADR 0007</a>: one entity index, and a write is visible to the next query.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0008-one-entity-model.html">ADR 0008</a>: one entity model, with players and items in the same store.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0009-external-inputs-enter-once-per-tick.html">ADR 0009</a>: external inputs enter once per tick and are recorded, and a handler&#39;s lane decides whether its packet is an input.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0010-no-barrel-files.html">ADR 0010</a>: no barrel files, and explicit package exports.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0011-a-world-exchanges-only-plain-data.html">ADR 0011</a>: a world exchanges only plain data with the outside.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0012-the-change-log-is-the-history.html">ADR 0012</a>: the change log is the history, complete and invertible.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0013-vetoable-actions-are-staged.html">ADR 0013</a>: vetoable actions run as intent, guard, compute and commit.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0014-handlers-share-a-shape-and-run-in-the-input-step.html">ADR 0014</a>: handlers share one shape, and input-lane handlers run in the input step.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0015-a-tick-is-a-fixed-sequence-of-synchronous-phases.html">ADR 0015</a>: a tick is a fixed sequence of synchronous phases.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0016-replication-reads-the-change-log.html">ADR 0016</a>: replication reads the change log after the tick and keeps a replica.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0017-one-lifecycle-event-bus.html">ADR 0017</a>: one lifecycle event bus.</li><li><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0018-a-save-is-a-snapshot-of-one-tick-boundary.html">ADR 0018</a>, proposed: a save is a snapshot of one tick boundary.</li></ul><h2 id="what-the-review-changed" tabindex="-1">What the review changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-review-changed" aria-label="Permalink to “What the review changed”">​</a></h2><p>The first version of this plan left ten gaps. Each one is closed below, with the evidence that closed it. Seven of them changed an ADR.</p><h3 id="replication-needs-more-than-the-current-tick-s-log" tabindex="-1">Replication needs more than the current tick&#39;s log <a class="header-anchor" href="#replication-needs-more-than-the-current-tick-s-log" aria-label="Permalink to “Replication needs more than the current tick&#39;s log”">​</a></h3><p>A player who walks into range of an entity that has not changed needs its full state, and the current tick&#39;s log has no entry for it. Today <code>spawnTo</code> reads position, rotation, metadata, attributes, equipment and effects from the live entity object and encodes a spawn packet for each viewer. Nothing caches replicated state.</p><p>Replication now keeps a replica, built only from the log. A membership entry carries an entity&#39;s full replicated state, and later entries update it. A first sight is answered from the replica and encoded once for each codec group. Replication still reads nothing from the simulation except the log, which keeps it movable to a worker. The cost is a second copy of the client-visible fields of each loaded entity, counted as replica entries and bytes. ADR 0016 records this.</p><h3 id="invertibility-needed-a-scope-and-a-use" tabindex="-1">Invertibility needed a scope and a use <a class="header-anchor" href="#invertibility-needed-a-scope-and-a-use" aria-label="Permalink to “Invertibility needed a scope and a use”">​</a></h3><p>ADR 0012 priced the before-values at three numbers for a moving entity, and authoritative state also includes blocks, inventories and block entities. Nothing in the program read a before-value.</p><p>Three things close this. First, the log has three entry kinds with stated inverses, and a chunk that enters the world is named in the log and not carried by it, because it arrived as an input. Second, a unit of work that throws is undone by inverting its span, which gives invertibility a use and a test inside the program and also closes the failure gap below. Third, this program keeps a tick&#39;s log only until replication and the save cut have read it. The before-values then cost memory for one tick and nothing on disk. Slice 2 reports deltas and bytes per tick on the replay scenarios as a non-gating count, so the retention work that comes later starts from real figures.</p><h3 id="the-tick-was-audited-for-awaits" tabindex="-1">The tick was audited for awaits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-tick-was-audited-for-awaits" aria-label="Permalink to “The tick was audited for awaits”">​</a></h3><p>The handlers had been audited and the simulation had not. An audit of everything reachable from <code>World.update</code> found 233 awaits and about 20 promises the tick starts without awaiting.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Awaits</th><th>What removes them</th><th>Slice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Packet sends</td><td>77</td><td>Sends become entries in the change log</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Functions that are async only to pass a wait on</td><td>120</td><td>They become synchronous once the waits below them are gone</td><td>3 to 9</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk or block reads that may load a chunk</td><td>21</td><td>Loaded-chunk accessors, chunk tickets and waiting states</td><td>3, 5, 9</td></tr><tr><td>Disk reads and writes</td><td>4</td><td>Chunk loads as inputs, saves under ADR 0018</td><td>8, 9</td></tr><tr><td>Awaited events</td><td>3</td><td>Guards under ADR 0013</td><td>4, 9</td></tr><tr><td>Other waits</td><td>8</td><td>Worlds built outside ticks, completion results as inputs</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most of the 233 are mechanical. The simulation never reads the result of a send, and <code>WorldSystems.tick</code>, <code>RaidManager.tick</code>, cooking stations, spawners, <code>EntityRuntime.tick</code> and the block-behaviors, engine-systems, lighting, spawning, entities, entity-goals and entity-brains packages contain no asynchronous code today.</p><p>Three sites are hard, and each needs a mechanism this plan now names.</p><ul><li>Portal transfer runs inside an entity&#39;s update (<code>Entity.ts:526</code>). It can build a dimension, generate chunks, raise a cancellable event and save player data. It becomes the message of ADR 0011: the source world commits the departure in its tick, and the destination admits the arrival as an input once its chunks are ready.</li><li>Respawn search runs inside <code>onDeath</code>, which runs inside <code>damage</code> (<code>Player.ts:988</code>). It is why every caller of <code>damage</code> is asynchronous, and the runtime starts <code>damage</code> without awaiting it. Death commits in the tick. The respawn point becomes a request to a world service, and its answer returns as an input.</li><li>The dragon fight loads its arena, gateway and pillar chunks inline (<code>DragonFight.ts:390</code>, <code>:512</code>, <code>:565</code>). It takes chunk tickets and gets explicit waiting states.</li></ul><p>Unloaded chunks are already handled the right way by most of the simulation. Block updates skip them, fluids and scheduled ticks read air and have their writes refused, and explosions and data-driven mobs read only loaded chunks. The code that loads a chunk from inside a tick is the legacy item entity, the player breath check, the dragon fight, portal travel and <code>World.setBlockRuntimeId</code>.</p><p>Each slice removes the awaits in the code it rewrites, and the count is reported from slice 1 on. The test that a tick completes without yielding can only pass at slice 9.</p><h3 id="the-temporary-mix-was-too-long" tabindex="-1">The temporary mix was too long <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-temporary-mix-was-too-long" aria-label="Permalink to “The temporary mix was too long”">​</a></h3><p>The first version kept a transitional adapter over legacy entities from slice 2 to slice 8 and deleted the legacy classes at the end. The legacy classes now go in slice 5, when players move, so the adapter lives for slices 2 to 5. Those four slices are one commitment. Slice 2 does not start until the work through slice 5 is planned.</p><p>Slice 5 is where the program is most likely to stall, because <code>Player</code> carries the entity, the connection, the saved record and server-wide chat and permissions. A spike before slice 2 puts one player-shaped entity into a prototype store, with an inventory, a network session that the entity references and a save and load round trip. The spike is thrown away. If it shows that a player cannot be expressed in the shape of ADR 0008, that ADR is reopened before any store code lands.</p><h3 id="one-input-step-would-have-throttled-chunk-streaming" tabindex="-1">One input step would have throttled chunk streaming <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-input-step-would-have-throttled-chunk-streaming" aria-label="Permalink to “One input step would have throttled chunk streaming”">​</a></h3><p>Of the 72 handler classes, 19 write authoritative state, 15 read it without writing, and 38 touch only their own connection. A sub-chunk request is answered in handler context today. Under one input step for every packet it would wait for the next tick and slow down with the tick rate.</p><p>ADR 0009 now defines three lanes. The connection lane and the read lane run when the packet arrives, and only input-lane packets are inputs. Four of the 15 readers relay something other players see (<code>ActorEvent</code>, <code>Animate</code>, <code>Emote</code>, <code>LevelSoundEvent</code>), so they belong in the input lane, where the relay becomes a log entry. The read lane is for handlers that reply only to the sender. <code>LevelChunk</code> delivery already runs from its own scheduler outside the tick and stays there.</p><p>The read lane narrows the outbound digest in ADR 0006. The digest covers what replication produces from the log, counted toward the tick that produced it. Read-lane replies and chunk streaming are outside it, because no tick decides when they leave. Today&#39;s recorder stamps a packet with the tick in which the transport framed it, which slice 7 changes.</p><h3 id="guards-have-to-replace-parameters" tabindex="-1">Guards have to replace parameters <a class="header-anchor" href="#guards-have-to-replace-parameters" aria-label="Permalink to “Guards have to replace parameters”">​</a></h3><p>The first version of ADR 0013 said a scripting before-hook could only pass or veto. In <code>@minecraft/server</code> 2.11.0, ten of the seventeen stable before-events let a script rewrite a parameter, such as the damage of <code>EntityHurtBeforeEvent</code> or the block list of <code>ExplosionBeforeEvent</code>. Bedrock add-ons are the primary way to extend the server, so a pass-or-veto rule would have ruled out a faithful host.</p><p>A guard now returns pass, veto or replacement values for the parameters its pipeline declares replaceable, and still writes no state. That matches the documented restricted execution of before-event callbacks. ADR 0017 changed for the same reason. Bedrock runs a <code>system.run</code> callback at the end of the same tick, so the ADR no longer places it in the next input step and leaves the point to the Script API host design.</p><h3 id="saves-had-no-rule" tabindex="-1">Saves had no rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#saves-had-no-rule" aria-label="Permalink to “Saves had no rule”">​</a></h3><p>A save pass mixes ticks today. Autosave starts in the middle of a tick, captures every player at that instant and then serializes chunks eight at a time across later ticks. A crash can leave an item in a saved inventory and also in a chest saved later. An entity that changes chunk during the pass can be written twice or not at all.</p><p>ADR 0018 proposes that a pass writes the state of one tick boundary and commits it as one atomic write. It is proposed and not accepted, because the commit is a synchronous LevelDB append on the server thread and its size is unknown. Slice 8 measures the bytes in a pass before the ADR is accepted.</p><h3 id="a-failed-update-left-partial-writes" tabindex="-1">A failed update left partial writes <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-failed-update-left-partial-writes" aria-label="Permalink to “A failed update left partial writes”">​</a></h3><p>ADR 0015 skipped an entity that throws and assumed the store stays consistent. An update that throws halfway has already written. The store now undoes the span of the unit that threw before it skips the entity, as described under invertibility above.</p><h3 id="slice-1-promised-too-much-and-proxies-had-no-slice" tabindex="-1">Slice 1 promised too much, and proxies had no slice <a class="header-anchor" href="#slice-1-promised-too-much-and-proxies-had-no-slice" aria-label="Permalink to “Slice 1 promised too much, and proxies had no slice”">​</a></h3><p>Slice 1 was named &quot;trustworthy replay&quot;, and replay is not fully trustworthy until the tick is synchronous. The slice list below says what replay guarantees after each step, and the target table names the slice at which each proxy starts to gate.</p><h2 id="slice-order" tabindex="-1">Slice order <a class="header-anchor" href="#slice-order" aria-label="Permalink to “Slice order”">​</a></h2><p>Every slice lands on <code>master</code>, and a slice deletes what it replaces. The one exception is the adapter over legacy entities, which slice 2 adds and slice 5 removes.</p><ol start="0"><li><strong>Barrel ban.</strong> Biome&#39;s <code>noBarrelFile</code> and <code>noReExportAll</code> with a baseline that only shrinks. No code changes. This slice is independent of the others and can land at any time.</li><li><strong>Tick boundaries.</strong> Every handler declares a lane. The dispatcher holds input-lane packets, and the ticker applies them at the start of the next tick in arrival order, which is what the replay harness already does when it injects. Capture stamps a packet with the tick that applied it. Chunk completions are recorded as inputs. Counts replace the 20 ms and 25 ms budgets. Worlds tick one after another in a fixed order, and a failing world does not skip the next. Handlers are still asynchronous here, so a tick can still yield, but no handler runs in the middle of one. After this slice, live play and its replay apply the same inputs at the same ticks.</li><li><strong>The entity store.</strong> The store, setters that append entries with before-values, spans and their inverses, the one index, and the adapter over legacy entities. The player spike comes first.</li><li><strong>Items and the eye of ender</strong> move into the store with built-in definitions. The item ground probe stops loading chunks.</li><li><strong>Staged damage and death.</strong> The store owns damage and death under ADR 0013, with replaceable parameters. The mirror and the health comparison are deleted. <code>damage</code> becomes synchronous, which removes the promises the runtime starts without awaiting. Respawn search becomes a world service request.</li><li><strong>Players</strong> move into the store, split into components, a network session and services for chat, permissions and persistence. <code>Entity</code>, <code>GenericEntity</code>, <code>Human</code>, the <code>Player</code> class and the adapter are deleted. No legacy entity remains after this slice.</li><li><strong>Handlers.</strong> The shared shape, a narrow context and synchronous input-lane handlers. The client package adopts the shape.</li><li><strong>Replication reads the log.</strong> Block and block entity writes go through logged setters, so the log is complete. Replication builds its replica, computes each audience once and encodes once for each codec group. Sends leave the simulation, which removes the largest class of awaits. Outbound packets count toward the tick that produced them.</li><li><strong>Saves at a tick boundary</strong>, if ADR 0018 is accepted after its measurement.</li><li><strong>The synchronous tick.</strong> Dimension travel as messages, chunk tickets and waiting states for the dragon fight, worlds built outside ticks, and tick functions admitted as inputs. After this slice a tick completes without yielding, and ADR 0006 holds in full.</li><li><strong>One lifecycle event bus.</strong></li><li><strong>Explicit exports.</strong> Prismarine&#39;s barrels and its <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> export are replaced.</li></ol><p>Players come before replication so the log is complete when replication reads it. Handlers come before replication so they have somewhere to put their output. The cost of that order is that the encode-once change lands at slice 7.</p><h2 id="progress" tabindex="-1">Progress <a class="header-anchor" href="#progress" aria-label="Permalink to “Progress”">​</a></h2><p>As of 2026-11-11. The rows through C4f were checked at program head <code>7265972882</code>, rebased on <code>master</code> <code>878604e167</code>, where every CI check passed, Client E2E included. Each C4h step records its own checks below.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A1 barrel ban, A2 count budgets and world order, A3 handler lanes, A4 change log module, A5 player spike</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>B1 input admission, with its two follow-up fixes (a held input blocked its connection; a stale move undid a teleport)</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>B2 chunk completions as recorded inputs, with the follow-up fix for the tick that stalled after the dragon died</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>B3a one live player per account (#2951) and the persistence service</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>B3b chat fan-out and respawn search as services</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>B4 save pass measurement, B5 re-keyed maps</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>C1a component state on the instance, C1b one entity index, C1c the entity store with logged setters, spans and undo</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>Replay determinism fixes (settle gaps, wall-clock chunk budget in the harness)</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>C2 items and the eye of ender in the store</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>C3 staged damage and death, the mirror deleted</td><td>Landed. Damage, death and client display are the store&#39;s; the legacy bodies remained for non-network callers until C3b</td></tr><tr><td>C3b the legacy mob bodies deleted, which meets slice 4&#39;s &quot;no mirror class&quot; target</td><td>Landed, see below</td></tr><tr><td>C4c player components with logged setters, and an inventory that logs one entry per slot</td><td>Landed</td></tr><tr><td>C4b the player overlay, client-owned movement and a death that keeps the entity</td><td>Landed. The store&#39;s rules are ready for a store player; the live player is still legacy, see below</td></tr><tr><td>C4e player persistence from the store entity, the saved record unchanged</td><td>Landed, see below</td></tr><tr><td>C4g store players shown to clients through the current replicator</td><td>Landed. The store path is built and tested; the live player is still legacy, see below</td></tr><tr><td>C4d player systems: hunger, breath, falls, meals and mining, reading loaded chunks only</td><td>Landed. The systems run over store players; the live player calls the same rules, see below</td></tr><tr><td>C4f the session holds the player&#39;s entity id, and the conversions split into four groups</td><td>Landed with its four groups, F1 to F4, by program head <code>7265972882</code>. F3 and F4 left their notes in commit messages, which the C4h plan below folds in</td></tr><tr><td>C4h live players in the store, and the legacy classes deleted</td><td>Done with H-S0 to H-D. No legacy entity remains, which meets slice 5&#39;s target, and <code>noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code> gates it; see below</td></tr><tr><td>Slice 5, players in the store</td><td>Done with C4b to C4h</td></tr><tr><td>D1 the shared handler shape, the narrow context and the proxy test</td><td>Landed on the program branch, with four handlers converted; see the D1 notes under phase D</td></tr><tr><td>D3 block and block entity writes through logged setters</td><td>Landed on the program branch, see the D3 notes under phase D</td></tr><tr><td>D2 the remaining handlers on the context, with its close</td><td>Landed on the program branch, with D2a to D2g and the close; see the D2 notes under phase D</td></tr><tr><td>D4 replication reads the log</td><td>Landed on the program branch with D4a to D4f; see the D4 section under phase D</td></tr><tr><td>Slice 7, replication reads the log</td><td>Done with D3 and D4a to D4f. Its three targets gate; see the D4f notes</td></tr><tr><td>Phases E and F</td><td>Not started</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="phases-and-work-packages" tabindex="-1">Phases and work packages <a class="header-anchor" href="#phases-and-work-packages" aria-label="Permalink to “Phases and work packages”">​</a></h2><p>A slice is too large to hand to one person or agent, and several slices have parts that do not depend on each other. The phases below cut the slices into work packages. Packages in one phase run side by side, each in its own worktree, and a phase starts when the packages it depends on have landed. Each package commits in small steps and passes the gate below before it is rebased onto the program branch.</p><h3 id="phase-a-foundations" tabindex="-1">Phase A: foundations <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-a-foundations" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase A: foundations”">​</a></h3><p>Nothing here depends on another package, and no two packages edit the same files.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Work</th><th>Main files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A1</td><td>0</td><td>Ban barrels with a baseline that only shrinks</td><td><code>biome.json</code>, <code>scripts/</code></td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td>1</td><td>Counts replace the 20 ms and 25 ms budgets. Worlds tick one after another in a fixed order, and a failing world does not skip the next</td><td><code>World.ts</code>, <code>WorldSystems.ts</code>, <code>modules/ticker.ts</code></td></tr><tr><td>A3</td><td>1</td><td>Every handler declares a lane, and a test checks the registry. No handler changes when it runs yet</td><td><code>network/handler/</code>, <code>PacketHandlerRegistry.ts</code></td></tr><tr><td>A4</td><td>2</td><td>The change log as a module of its own: the three entry kinds, spans, inverses and the one-tick lifetime, with nothing wired to it</td><td>new module beside <code>world/</code></td></tr><tr><td>A5</td><td>2</td><td>The player spike. It is thrown away, and its findings are kept as a research note</td><td><code>docs/research/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="phase-b-admission-and-preparation" tabindex="-1">Phase B: admission and preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-b-admission-and-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase B: admission and preparation”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>B1</td><td>1</td><td>A2, A3</td><td>The dispatcher holds input-lane packets and the ticker applies them at tick start. Capture stamps the applying tick. The replay test compares two runs tick by tick</td></tr><tr><td>B2</td><td>1</td><td>A2</td><td>Chunk completions are recorded as inputs</td></tr><tr><td>B3</td><td>5</td><td>none</td><td>Player persistence, chat fan-out and respawn search move out of <code>Player</code> into services keyed by player identity, while <code>Player</code> is still a legacy entity</td></tr><tr><td>B4</td><td>8</td><td>none</td><td>Measure the bytes in a save pass and confirm what a saved player record copies, so ADR 0018 can be accepted or changed</td></tr><tr><td>B5</td><td>5</td><td>none</td><td>Re-key the seven maps keyed by the <code>Player</code> object by player identity or entity id</td></tr></tbody></table><p>B1 and B2 both edit the ticker, so B2 follows B1. B3, B4 and B5 run beside them.</p><p>The lane declarations of A3 put 35 handlers in the connection lane, 9 in the read lane and 28 in the input lane, and left B1 these rules.</p><ul><li>An input-lane packet is applied in the input step of the world its sender&#39;s entity is in. An effect on another world leaves as a message under ADR 0011 and is admitted at that world&#39;s next input step.</li><li>Server-wide state that is not a world&#39;s authoritative state, such as the operator list, the default game mode and chat fan-out, changes in memory when the handler runs and persists off the tick.</li><li>A player&#39;s removal is an input, whether a <code>Disconnect</code> packet or a closed transport caused it.</li><li><code>SubChunkRequest</code> can load a chunk the world evicted. The handler may wait for it, and the loaded chunk is admitted through the completion queue like any other.</li><li><code>Login</code> and <code>ClientToServerHandshake</code> stay in the connection lane although they read the default world&#39;s spawn and build the player. That work moves in front of the join in slice 6.</li></ul><p>Slice 6 inherits a list of what stops an input-lane handler from being synchronous. Eight handlers load chunks or search for a position, among them <code>PlayerAction</code>, <code>InventoryTransaction</code>, <code>ItemStackRequest</code>, <code>Respawn</code> and <code>RequestChunkRadius</code>. Six reach the disk through <code>savePlayerData</code>, the operator file or the config. <code>Text</code>, <code>CommandRequest</code> and <code>SettingsCommand</code> call into commands and chat, which can do anything. Six read-lane handlers are write requests the server drops today, and each moves to the input lane when its write is implemented.</p><p>B2 left three things for later slices to remove.</p><ul><li><code>world/TickContext.ts</code> uses <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> to tell code inside a tick from code between ticks, because the tick still awaits. Slice 9 makes the tick synchronous, and a plain flag set by the ticker replaces it then.</li><li>Eight code paths still load a chunk from inside a tick. Each is admitted inline, recorded as <code>inline</code> and listed in <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code>, and a test fails when one is removed without updating the list. Slices 3, 5, 6 and 9 remove them.</li><li><code>World.disable</code> waits for an unload parked on admission, so calling it from inside a tick would never finish. Nothing does today. Slice 9 has to keep it that way or drain admissions first.</li><li>A tick that awaits work started outside it, such as from an event or a command between ticks, cannot be told apart from one that waits for its own chunks, and a chunk that work loads waits for the next input step. The ticker therefore admits every held completion inline, recorded as <code>inline</code>, and logs a <code>TickStalledError</code> each <code>TICK_STALL_MS</code> a tick runs with completions held. Slice 9&#39;s synchronous tick removes the wait and the watch with it.</li></ul><p>C1a and C1b left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>C1a moved component state the entities package hid in <code>WeakMap</code>s onto <code>EntityInstance.componentState</code>, with one write path that reports each write&#39;s before and after values to <code>EntityEnvironment.componentStateWritten</code>. <code>target_nearby_sensor</code> keeps a runtime id there, and runtime ids are drawn again each session, so C1c marks that field as not saved until the store has ids that survive a reload.</li><li>C1b left one index per dimension (<code>EntityIndex</code>, <code>World.getEntityIndex()</code>), and chunk membership as a second structure written only by <code>World.entityRelocated</code>. Membership follows the client body and the index follows the runtime&#39;s position, so the two agree again once C3 deletes the mirror. Until then legacy explosions search four blocks beyond their reach.</li><li>Viewer tracking still passes every entity in the world to <code>EntityTracker.plan</code>. D4 replaces that with audiences computed from the index.</li><li>Inside the tick, <code>World.getEntities()</code> is still copied for spawner counts, the attribute flush and tracking, and <code>EntityRuntime.all()</code> is copied for the entity pass, the despawn cycle and the mirror&#39;s <code>sync</code>. Slice 5&#39;s target removes the copies.</li></ul><p>C1c left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>The store is <code>EntityRuntime.store</code>, which took over the runtime&#39;s entity map, and it holds the world&#39;s change log until block writes join it in D3. A log tick is numbered by the store, not by the level clock, which <code>/time set</code> moves backwards.</li><li>A player&#39;s inventory was not logged: a slot changed by mutating the <code>Item</code> in place and <code>Inventory</code> did not know its owner. C4c closed this, as the notes below say. Legacy position, health and game mode are logged through <code>entity/legacyAdapter.ts</code>.</li><li>A merchant&#39;s offers are saved and hashed but not logged, because a trade changes the <code>Merchant</code> in place.</li><li>An undo restores only what a save or the digest reads. Goal progress, pending deaths and other runtime bookkeeping stay as the throw left them.</li><li>A chunk admitted inline inside a unit pins the unit, which then keeps its writes if it throws, until slices 3 to 9 remove the inline loads.</li><li>Writes between ticks, such as tick functions, which run after the worlds seal, and connection-lane handlers, are counted as unlogged and not logged.</li><li>A player whose update throws is not contained yet: legacy updates do not run as units. When players move into the store, the store&#39;s failure count disconnects the session instead of removing the entity.</li></ul><p>B3b left these for C3 and C4.</p><ul><li>Chat fan-out is <code>ChatManager</code>. A player joins it as a <code>ChatRecipient</code>, keyed by <code>keyOfPlayer</code>, with a <code>receive</code> callback, and the manager decides the audience and builds the line. Each recipient is still its own <code>chat</code> listener, because a listener that cancels the event stops only the players who joined after it. One listener with one audience pass is the shape D4 wants, and it changes which players such a listener stops, so it belongs with the guards of ADR 0013 or the event bus of slice 10.</li><li>Respawn search is <code>RespawnSearch</code> in <code>world/RespawnSearch.ts</code>, one for each level (<code>Level.getRespawnSearch</code>). <code>find(request)</code> takes the player&#39;s key, saved spawn point and body as plain data. It answers with <code>spawn</code> (<code>own</code> or <code>world</code>), the dimension, the three exchange positions and <code>spawnPointLost</code>, and writes nothing. Every world read is behind its <code>RespawnWorlds</code> port: building the bed&#39;s dimension, the bed and feet checks, and the world spawn, which can still run the search for dry land.</li><li><code>Player.getRespawnPoint</code> is the adapter. It builds the request, applies <code>spawnPointLost</code> by clearing the point and sending <code>tile.bed.notValid</code>, and resolves the answer&#39;s world. <code>onDeath</code>, <code>respawn</code>, <code>RespawnHandler</code> and <code>DimensionTravel.leaveEnd</code> call it. For C3, <code>onDeath</code> sends the request instead of awaiting the adapter, and the input that admits the answer applies <code>spawnPointLost</code> and sends the <code>SearchingForSpawn</code>. The in-tick chunk load entry for the search names <code>RespawnSearch.ts</code> and slice 4.</li><li>The request does not carry the world spawn. Reading it before the search would run the search for dry land even when the player&#39;s own point holds, so the service reads it only when it falls back to it.</li><li>For C4, the request&#39;s spawn point comes from the store entity&#39;s component, and the chat recipient&#39;s <code>receive</code> finds the session through the session registry by identity.</li></ul><p>C2 left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>Dropped stacks reach clients through <code>entity/data/itemBodies.ts</code>, an <code>ItemActorBody</code> per stack that reads the store entity and goes on the wire as an <code>AddItemActor</code>. It sits apart from the mirror, so whatever replaces the mirror for the legacy tracker can take it over too. The eye of ender has no display code of its own and shows through the mirror like any store mob.</li><li>The item bodies still run <code>Entity.update</code>, which is how a stack reaches a portal. Portal travel adopts a stack that arrives in another world into that world&#39;s store from the body&#39;s saved form. Slice 9 turns travel into messages and removes this.</li><li>A stack&#39;s age is component state, so each stack appends one component entry every tick it lies in a ticking chunk, besides its position and speed while it moves. An idle world with stacks on the ground is not an idle log.</li><li>The eye&#39;s shatter roll draws on the dimension&#39;s random, where the legacy eye drew on the server&#39;s.</li><li>A drop now goes straight into the store, so it no longer waits on <code>World.addEntity</code>. <code>World.ITEM_ENTITY_LIMIT</code> counts the item bodies.</li></ul><p>C4c left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>Every write to a slot of a player&#39;s main inventory, armor, off hand, ender chest, cursor or crafting grid appends one <code>inventory-slot</code> entry. Its value is the stack&#39;s saved form (<code>inventory/slotLog.ts</code>), the same form the player record keeps, so an undo restores what a save and the digest read. The hand slot is the entity field <code>inventory.handSlot</code>. <code>Inventory.observe</code>, <code>HumanInventory.observeCarried</code> and <code>CraftingInput.observe</code> report the writes, and <code>Human</code> wires them to the adapter.</li><li><code>PlayerComponents</code> (<code>entity/playerComponents.ts</code>) holds vitals (food, saturation, exhaustion, drowning ticks, fall distance), experience, flying, the movement flags (sneaking, sleeping, on the ground), the spawn point, the credits flag and the tick an item use began. Each field is logged as <code>&lt;component&gt;.&lt;field&gt;</code>. Food, saturation, exhaustion and experience stay in the attributes and the component reads them there. <code>Player</code>&#39;s private fields are accessors over it, so the death, respawn and damage paths were not edited and are logged too. C4e and C4f can move this object onto the store entity unchanged.</li><li>The replay digest hashes <code>Player.componentSnapshot</code>, so digests of a world with a player changed once, in a commit of their own. The replay suite still finds two replays identical.</li><li>Not logged yet, for C4d: air (<code>metadata.air</code>, whose owner C4d decides), sprinting, the block being mined, the meal in progress, which still holds a closure, and every attribute other than the ones above, such as movement speed and luck. Absorption is on the damage path, which C3 owns.</li><li>A stack changed in place is not a write. The inventory keeps each slot&#39;s last logged value, so the next write to that slot still carries the right before-value, but the change lands in the tick of that write. Two paths did this and were converted: wearing a tool, now <code>Item.withDurability</code>, and a partial <code>/clear</code>. <code>ItemStackRequestHandler.itemOrNull</code> still sets an item&#39;s count from its entry in place, which changes no saved form. A slot whose entry count and item count disagree logs the entry&#39;s count only.</li><li>A stack network id is session state and is not in a slot&#39;s value, so an undone write gives the stack a new id. Replication has to send the slot again (slice 7).</li><li>A workstation&#39;s input slots and a chest&#39;s slots are block entity state, which D3 logs.</li><li><code>Entity.restoreLoggedField</code> and <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code>, which <code>Human</code> and <code>Player</code> override, are how the adapter&#39;s inverse writer reaches a legacy player. C4h deletes them with the adapter. Test doubles built from <code>Player.prototype</code> without the constructor get their components from <code>withPlayerComponents</code> in <code>testing/playerDouble.test.helper.ts</code>, which C4h deletes with them.</li><li><code>Player.getExperience</code> replaced six hand-built experience holders, so every experience write goes through the component.</li><li>The saved player record is unchanged: <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> passes byte for byte.</li></ul><p>C3 left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>Every blow a store entity takes is a <code>DamageIntent</code> run by <code>DamagePipeline</code> (<code>entity/data/damagePipeline.ts</code>, <code>damageStages.ts</code>): a mob&#39;s bite, an area attack, a projectile, a blast, a player&#39;s swing, a status effect, a command, an item action, a component hazard and a dropped stack&#39;s fire, lava and cactus. The guards are <code>present</code>, <code>invulnerable</code>, <code>damage_resistant</code> (a dropped stack&#39;s item), <code>hit_grace</code>, <code>cannot_be_attacked</code>, <code>dragon</code>, <code>damage_sensor</code>, the <code>fireDamage</code> game rule and the before-hooks a host adds. The modifiers are the grace difference, armor, protection, resistance, absorption, the dragon&#39;s scaling and the sensor&#39;s multiplier, in that priority order, so the sensor still scales what armor left, as it did when the mirror handed the runtime the body&#39;s loss.</li><li>A blow from the legacy code keeps the rules the legacy pipeline gave a mob through <code>DamageSourceFacts</code>: the grace period, the mitigation and the shove. A mob&#39;s own attacks keep none. <code>legacyDamage.ts</code> turns a <code>DamageSource</code> into an intent and maps the legacy causes to Bedrock names, so a sensor sees a player&#39;s swing as <code>entity_attack</code>.</li><li>A veto appends one <code>action-rejected</code> occurrence and nothing else. The grace period is a runtime map, not logged and not saved, as the legacy body&#39;s was not. The sensing is memoized for one run in <code>DamageSensingSlot</code>, which the sensor guard, the multiplier and the commit share.</li><li>A death runs inside the commit of the blow, queued <code>minecraft:on_death</code> events first, where it waited for the entity&#39;s next tick before.</li><li>The plugin <code>entityDamage</code> event is a before-hook that can cancel a blow or change its amount, and <code>entityDeath</code> and the advancement triggers are raised from <code>EntityHostFacts.damageCommitted</code>, inside the blow, before a death removes the victim. The <code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code> parity family checks that timing. Slice 7 has to keep it when observers read the log.</li><li>Health is the store&#39;s alone. The comparison that handed a body&#39;s loss back to the runtime is gone, and a body reads its health from the store and heals through it.</li><li>Clients are shown store entities through <code>StoreActor</code>s (<code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>), one per store entity, which answer the network layer from store state through the structural <code>ShownActor</code> (<code>world/ShownActor.ts</code>). <code>StoreActors.sync</code> sends what changed since it last sent each entity, which is replication&#39;s work until slice 7 reads the log instead. Nothing in it is mob-specific; the eye of ender shows through it. Dropped stacks still show through C2&#39;s <code>ItemActorBody</code>, which the actors can take over.</li><li>The legacy bodies (<code>LiveBodies</code>) stayed in each world&#39;s entity map, written silently and never announced, because the legacy code still reached a mob through them: commands and selectors, legacy blasts and swings (<code>damageEntity</code>), interactions and trades, chunk saves and the replay digest (body NBT and attributes), portal travel, spawner counts, the dragon fight and riding. C3b moved those to the store actors and deleted the bodies.</li><li><code>damage</code> is synchronous for store entities. A legacy player&#39;s <code>Entity.damage</code> is still async, because its sends are awaited, and <code>live.ts</code> still starts it without awaiting for a mob&#39;s blow on a player. That goes with C4b and slice 7.</li><li>A player&#39;s death no longer awaits the respawn search. <code>onDeath</code> files it with <code>RespawnRequests</code> (<code>world/RespawnRequests.ts</code>), and the next input step awaits every search filed before it, in order, then applies <code>spawnPointLost</code> and sends <code>Respawn</code> and <code>DeathInfo</code>. Awaiting in the input step keeps the applying tick independent of host speed, but the search still loads chunks inside the tick, so its <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code> entry moved to slice 9, where such loads become recorded inputs. <code>RespawnHandler</code> and <code>respawn</code> still search as they run.</li></ul><p>C3b, which deleted the legacy mob bodies, left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>Every store entity but a dropped stack reaches the legacy world code through its <code>StoreActor</code> (<code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>), the same object that shows it to clients. <code>World.getActors</code> and <code>World.getActor</code> list and find a world&#39;s entities, a store entity as its actor; <code>World.getEntities</code> and <code>World.getEntity</code> hold only legacy entities, which are players and the bodies of dropped stacks. <code>WorldActor</code> (<code>world/WorldActor.ts</code>) is the union of the two, and it is what <code>DamageSource</code>&#39;s attacker and projectile, the <code>entityDamage</code>, <code>entityDeath</code> and <code>entityPortalEnter</code> plugin events, the advancement triggers, the death messages, the trade window, the save context and the providers take. Slice 5 narrows it to the actor.</li><li>The actor answers from the store and keeps no copy of it. A blow on it runs <code>DamagePipeline</code>, a shove and a teleport write the body, and a tag or a status effect goes into the entity&#39;s state, filtered by its <code>minecraft:mob_effect_immunity</code>. It keeps three things the store does not model yet: the actor record tags a save carries forward (<code>persistentNbt</code>, which a save writes the record into), the portal cooldown and contact, and the clock of the status effects. Slice 9&#39;s travel as messages takes the portal state; the other two belong with replication&#39;s replica (slice 7) or with a status effect component.</li><li>The world updates each actor in its tick, after the legacy entities, which runs the portal clock, a trip once its contact is ready, and the status effects, whose harm and healing go through the store. The bodies did this in <code>Entity.update</code>, interleaved with the legacy entities in the order the entity map held them. <code>DimensionTravel</code> moves a store entity by restoring its record in the destination store, with its actor record tags, tags and status effects.</li><li>Chunk membership of a store entity is the index. Its cells are sixteen blocks wide and aligned to chunks, so <code>EntityIndex.chunkChanged</code> reports the chunk each move is in and the chunk a crossing left, and the world marks them for the next save, as the body&#39;s position writes did. A chunk that leaves the world takes the store entities <code>EntityIndex.storeInChunk</code> files in it. C1b&#39;s two structures no longer disagree, and a legacy blast no longer searches four blocks past its reach: it asks for the legacy entities (<code>legacyWithin</code>) and the store&#39;s (<code>storeWithin</code>) apart.</li><li>The replay digests did not change: an actor carries the tags, attributes and metadata the body carried, and the digest hashes the actors in the order it hashed the bodies.</li><li>The in-tick copy of <code>EntityRuntime.all()</code> for the mirror&#39;s <code>sync</code> is gone, and spawner caps walk the entity map and the actors in place. <code>World.getActors</code> copies and sorts for selectors, saves, the digest and the content adapter&#39;s <code>at</code>, <code>near</code> and <code>countOfType</code>, which slice 5&#39;s target removes. No <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code> entry went through a body, so the list is unchanged.</li><li>The proxy target &quot;no mirror class and no health comparison&quot; gates now. <code>entity/data/noMirror.test.ts</code> fails if <code>liveBodies.ts</code> returns or another legacy class wraps a store entity, and <code>live.test.ts</code> checks a running world: a store entity shows through its actor with nothing in the entity map, and a health written onto an actor is not handed back to the store.</li><li>C2&#39;s <code>ItemActorBody</code> stays. A dropped stack still reaches a portal through <code>Entity.update</code>, goes on the wire as an <code>AddItemActor</code>, and merges, is picked up and is unloaded as a legacy body, so folding it into the actor means moving all four paths, which is not small. Slice 9&#39;s travel as messages removes the first and makes the rest a display question.</li><li><code>/summon</code> has no command form that passes a name: only the command host&#39;s <code>entities.summon</code> does, and it now names the entity in the store, where the body&#39;s name had reached no client since C3.</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass: name a mob with a name tag, lead it, ride a saddled pig or a horse, trade with a villager, and walk a mob through a nether portal, then save, restart and find each where it was.</li></ul><p>C4b left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>The player overlay is code, not a pack file: <code>entity/playerOverlay.ts</code>, applied in <code>buildServerContent</code> to whichever pack&#39;s <code>minecraft:player</code> file won the stack. A file under <code>vanilla_overlay/entities/</code> would replace the shipped player file whole, where the overlay adds a component only when that file declares none. It adds <code>minecraft:health</code> 20 of 20 and nothing else. The raw definitions in <code>ServerContent.definitions</code> still hold the file as it shipped; the resolved type carries the overlay. The <code>entities.behavior</code> parity family checks it as <code>player-health</code>.</li><li>The store&#39;s three player rules key on <code>clientOwnsMovement(identifier)</code>, which is true of <code>minecraft:player</code> alone. A player&#39;s update in the entity pass stops after its timers, its components and the void check, so it gets no brain, navigation, coast, step or pushes, and <code>isPushable</code> is false for it while it still pushes mobs. <code>MobBody.onImpulse</code> turns every <code>push</code> on a player&#39;s body, a blow&#39;s shove included, into an <code>impulse</code> occurrence and leaves its velocity alone. <code>EntityRuntime.applyClientMovement</code> writes what the client reported, and <code>MobBody.teleportTo</code> stays the server&#39;s move. <code>kill</code> keeps a player: it stays in the store and in <code>dying</code>, so the <code>present</code> guard vetoes further blows, until <code>EntityRuntime.revive</code> brings it back at full health with one health entry and no membership entry. No live player is a store entity yet, so these rules run only in <code>entity/playerOverlay.test.ts</code>. The package that spawns one (C4e, C4g or C4h) also has to keep it out of chunk saves, which skip only the legacy class today (<code>ChunkSaveContext.ts</code>), and out of the despawn cycle.</li><li>For the live player, every server move is acknowledged. <code>Player.setPosition</code> always sends a teleport, where it sent a <code>Normal</code> move that registered nothing, which chorus fruit used. A move event&#39;s reset (<code>PositionMode.Respawn</code>) registers the same acknowledgment a teleport does. A join, a respawn and a world change move the player without a <code>MovePlayer</code> because their own exchanges place the client. Every handler whose source moves a player is in the input lane, and <code>Lane.test.ts</code> checks that.</li><li>A shove on a live player is logged as an <code>impulse</code> occurrence beside the motion <code>PlayerSession.sendMotion</code> still sends, until slice 7 sends it from the log.</li><li>A live player&#39;s death keeps its runtime id, as the legacy class always did, and appends no membership entry. <code>respawn()</code>&#39;s attribute reset is now logged: health, and the component fields the attributes hold, through <code>PlayerComponents.reportingChanges</code>. A respawn into another dimension moves the player between worlds, which is a transfer under ADR 0011 and keeps the id; once players are store entities that is a leave from one store and an enter into another.</li><li>A blow on a live player commits before it waits. <code>Entity.damage</code> starts its packets instead of awaiting each, and <code>Player.onDeath</code> posts <code>entityDeath</code>, raises its advancement triggers and files the respawn search before its first wait, so the health, the grace period, the death and the request are written when the call returns. What remains: the blow still runs the legacy rules in <code>Entity.damage</code>, not <code>DamagePipeline</code>, so a plugin&#39;s <code>entityDamage</code> handler and the grace period differ by path; the promise still covers the packets, which <code>live.ts</code> starts without awaiting; and <code>applySonicBoom</code> waits for that promise to learn whether the blow landed before it shoves. Running a player&#39;s blows through <code>DamagePipeline</code> needs the player to be a store entity, which is C4h&#39;s.</li><li>No client e2e scenario covers riding, because <code>/ride</code> is unsupported (<code>command/host/entities.ts</code>); a player riding a mob is checked in game. The new <code>player-knockback</code> scenario covers a mob&#39;s shove on a player.</li><li>Found on the way: chorus fruit puts a player&#39;s eyes where their feet belong (#2961). Run alone after <code>fall-damage</code> and <code>damage</code>, the <code>dimensions</code> scenario fails on the health those left, because nothing heals between scenarios. It passes alone, and CI&#39;s full suite has passed with it.</li></ul><p>C4e left these for later packages.</p><ul><li>The record is a projection with one home, <code>world/playerRecord.ts</code>. <code>playerRecordFrom(player, services, dimension)</code> builds it and <code>applyPlayerRecord(player, data, defaultGamemode, content)</code> puts one back, through two structural ports, <code>PlayerRecordSource</code> and <code>PlayerRecordTarget</code>. <code>recordedPosition</code> reads where a record puts a returning player. <code>PlayerPersistence</code> still owns where a record is read and written and the key it is filed under (<code>keyOfPlayer</code>).</li><li>A live <code>Player</code> satisfies both ports. <code>World.savePlayerData</code> calls <code>playerRecordOf</code>, which reads the advancements and the enchantment seed from their keyed services and calls <code>playerRecordFrom</code>, and <code>Player.enable</code> calls <code>applyPlayerRecord</code> and <code>recordedPosition</code>. <code>Player.restoreHealth</code> and <code>Player.restoreFlying</code> are the two silent writes the load needs. Live behavior did not change, and <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> still passes byte for byte.</li><li>A store player is <code>StorePlayer</code> (<code>entity/storePlayer.ts</code>): the <code>minecraft:player</code> store entity, which holds the position, the look and the health, and beside it the parts the store does not model yet, in the objects a live player holds them in: the client&#39;s attribute table, <code>PlayerComponents</code>, <code>HumanInventory</code>, the game mode, the portal cooldown, the head yaw and the identity. The record holds the eye, so <code>StorePlayer</code> adds <code>EYE_HEIGHT</code> to the entity&#39;s feet and <code>placeAt</code> takes it off. A y that is not exact in binary can move by its last bit across a save and a load. The record&#39;s attribute table takes its health entry from the entity&#39;s health and maximum, so a store player saves the table a live player saves. The format test builds a store player the way C4b&#39;s tests do, writes both fixtures byte for byte from it, loads each fixture into a fresh one and writes the same bytes again.</li><li>What C4h has to switch on: <ol><li>Where store players live. Nothing builds a <code>StorePlayer</code> outside tests. C4h keeps one per player entity, keyed by entity id, builds it at login from the spawned <code>minecraft:player</code>, and applies the record before the entity enters the store, so the load appends no entries, as the live load runs before the world holds the player.</li><li>Logging. A <code>StorePlayer</code>&#39;s components and inventory log nothing, because <code>EntityRuntime.writeField</code> throws <code>LoggedFieldError</code> on a field it does not know, and an undo would then stop the world. C4h wires them to <code>EntityStore.written</code> and <code>writtenSlot</code> and adds an inverse writer for the player fields, which replaces the adapter&#39;s <code>restoreLoggedField</code> and <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code>.</li><li>The keyed services. <code>storedAdvancements</code>, <code>loadAdvancements</code>, <code>forgetAdvancements</code>, <code>enchantmentSeedOf</code> and <code>forgetEnchantmentSeed</code> take a <code>Player</code> and use only its key and the server. They need a form that takes the key, and then <code>World.savePlayerData</code>, <code>World.getPlayerData</code> and <code>lastPlayerRecord</code>, which take a <code>Player</code> today, take the key and the store player.</li><li>Copies to delete with <code>Player</code>. <code>StorePlayer.setSaturation</code> and <code>setPortalCooldown</code> repeat the clamps of <code>Player.setSaturation</code> and <code>Entity.setPortalCooldown</code>, and <code>EYE_HEIGHT</code> lives in <code>entity/Human.ts</code>, which C4h deletes.</li><li>The maximum health in a record&#39;s table is not applied to a store entity, whose type decides it. Both are 20 today.</li><li>The head yaw is kept on the <code>StorePlayer</code>, because <code>MobBody</code> has none and <code>EntityRuntime.applyClientMovement</code> takes only the yaw and the pitch.</li><li>C4b&#39;s note still applies: a store player has to stay out of chunk saves (<code>ChunkSaveContext.ts</code>) and out of the despawn cycle.</li></ol></li><li>A saved player record is a deep copy, which closes the question under &quot;Checks to run&quot;. Checked again at this package: <code>storedInventory</code> builds new arrays and builds each stack from primitives, the attribute table, the experience and the position are numbers, and the advancements&#39; <code>toJSON</code> builds new objects. The one shared object was the spawn point, which was the component&#39;s own value. A write replaces it and never changes it in place, but <code>playerRecordFrom</code> now copies it, so a record held until a later commit under ADR 0018 shares nothing with the player. <code>inventory/persistence.test.ts</code> asserts it for the inventory, and the format test for a whole store player record.</li><li>The client e2e <code>rejoin</code> scenario is new. It leaves the Nether hurt and logs back in, and checks the dimension, the position and the health. No scenario left and rejoined before. <code>dimensions</code>, <code>spawnpoint-respawn</code> and <code>death-respawn</code> pass with it.</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass: log out with a damaged, enchanted and renamed tool in the hand, armor on, a shield in the off hand and items in the ender chest, on part health and food, in the Nether, with a bed set in the Overworld, and log back in. Everything comes back, and dying sends the player to the bed.</li></ul><p>C4g, which shows store players to clients, left these for C4h.</p><ul><li>A store player is an entity of type <code>minecraft:player</code> with a <code>StorePlayerActor</code> (<code>entity/data/storePlayers.ts</code>), a <code>StoreActor</code> that other clients are sent through <code>PlayerSession.sendAddPlayer</code>: the <code>AddPlayer</code>, then the abilities, the armor and the status effects. A legacy player goes through the same method, and through the same <code>sendAbilities</code>, <code>sendArmorFor</code>, <code>sendGameTypeFor</code> and <code>playerListEntryOf</code> (<code>network/playerListEntry.ts</code>), which read the structural <code>ShownPlayer</code> (<code>world/ShownPlayer.ts</code>). <code>PlayerSession.sendSpawn</code> and <code>sendDespawn</code> are gone. The moves, metadata and equipment changes go out through <code>StoreActors.sync</code>, one move a tick at most, and removal from the store sends a <code>RemoveActor</code> to each client that tracked it, which covers a leave and a dimension change. A death sends nothing, because the player stays in the store. <code>entity/data/storePlayers.test.ts</code> counts these packets with real sessions and the real replicator.</li><li>What the store does not hold yet the actor reads from C4e&#39;s <code>StorePlayer</code>: the identity, the head yaw, the game mode, flight, and the held item and armor from its <code>HumanInventory</code> (<code>HumanInventory.getEquipment</code>, which <code>Human.getEquipment</code> now calls too). <code>StorePlayer</code> gained the skin, the device and the platform chat id. <code>StorePlayers</code>, one for the server in <code>EntityRuntimes.players</code>, files each store player by entity id, which is the registry C4e&#39;s first switch item asks for, so a player that changes dimension is still found if it keeps its id. A <code>minecraft:player</code> entity with no filed store player is shown as a plain actor.</li><li>To switch, C4h spawns each live player with <code>spawnStorePlayer(runtime, entityRuntimes.players, feet, identity, fill)</code>. It builds the <code>StorePlayer</code> and files it before the entity is announced, because the first <code>AddPlayer</code> is built in the same call, and <code>fill</code> is where the saved record (<code>applyPlayerRecord</code>) and the login&#39;s skin and device go. It writes the name as the entity&#39;s name tag, as a legacy player&#39;s name is its metadata&#39;s. The position is the feet; <code>StorePlayer.getPosition</code> gives the eyes the wire carries. A dimension change restores the player into the destination store with the same id (<code>EntityRuntime.restore(record, at, id)</code>), and <code>players.delete(id)</code> runs when the player leaves the server. A skin change writes <code>StorePlayer.skin</code>.</li><li>The viewers are still <code>World.getPlayers()</code>, legacy players with sessions, in <code>flushMovement</code>, the observer path, <code>getViewers</code> and <code>entityAdded</code>. Six methods key on <code>entity instanceof Player</code> and have to find a store player&#39;s own session by entity id instead: <code>entityAdded</code>, which shows an arriving player the world, and the own-client copies in <code>actorEffect</code>, <code>entityAttributesChanged</code>, <code>statusEffectChanged</code>, <code>entityMetadataChanged</code> and <code>entityEquipmentChanged</code>. The observer path now skips the client whose entity changed by runtime id, as the movement flush did, so the own client gets no second copy.</li><li>The head yaw on the wire is <code>StorePlayer.headYaw</code>, which C4h&#39;s input step writes beside <code>EntityRuntime.applyClientMovement</code>, since that takes only the yaw and the pitch (C4e&#39;s sixth item). Observers are sent equipment changes from the inventory by comparing it each tick, which builds one equipment set for each store player a tick until slice 7 reads the slot entries instead.</li><li>Metadata is the components&#39; projection plus the name tag. The legacy player writes can-fly from the game mode, sprinting, breath and air into its metadata, and each needs a logged home that the projection reads; air and sprinting are C4d&#39;s. No server code writes the sneaking flag for either kind of player, so other clients may not see a player crouch; the combined pass checks it. Observers are sent no attributes for a store player, as for a legacy one, and the player&#39;s own client gets its attributes from its session.</li><li>The held item reaches viewers twice once <code>MobEquipmentHandler</code> addresses a store player: its relay, with the hotbar slot, and <code>StoreActors.sync</code>&#39;s equipment change, with the slots at zero and the armor beside it. C4h deletes the relay. Armor and off-hand changes then reach viewers at once, which they do not for a legacy player (#2965).</li><li>Game mode, abilities, skin and the player list are still triggered by legacy code: <code>Player.setGamemode</code>, <code>Player.enable</code>, <code>PlayerSkinHandler</code> and <code>ResourcePackClientResponseHandler</code>. Each calls a method that takes a <code>ShownPlayer</code> now, so C4h passes the actor. <code>StoreActors.sync</code> does not notice a game mode change, so the code that changes it sends <code>sendGameTypeFor</code> and the abilities, as <code>setGamemode</code> does.</li><li>Animations, sounds and actor events relay by runtime id through <code>PlayerSession.tracks</code>, so they reach a store player&#39;s viewers once their handlers address it.</li><li>The store player is not kept out of chunk saves or the despawn cycle yet, which C4b&#39;s note gives to the package that spawns live players.</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass, with two vanilla clients once C4h lands: the second player sees the first appear with its skin and name, walk, look around, swing, change its held item and armor, sneak, change game mode, die and respawn, change dimension and leave, and the player list shows both throughout. Until C4h, the same checks on a live server confirm that nothing changed for legacy players.</li></ul><p>C4d, the player systems, left these for C4h.</p><ul><li>Each survival rule has one home, over a <code>SurvivalPlayer</code> (<code>entity/survivalPlayer.ts</code>) that hands it a player&#39;s <code>PlayerComponents</code>: hunger, exhaustion, saturation, natural regeneration and starvation in <code>entity/playerHunger.ts</code>, breath and drowning in <code>playerBreath.ts</code>, fall distance and landing damage in <code>playerFall.ts</code>, the meal in progress in <code>playerMeal.ts</code>, crack animation in <code>playerMining.ts</code>, and what a death and a respawn reset in <code>playerRespawn.ts</code>. A rule writes through the components and returns the damage or the heal for its caller to deal, because a legacy blow still waits on its packets. The legacy <code>Player</code> calls the rules for its own components, so live behavior is unchanged but for the two fixes below.</li><li><code>PlayerSystems</code> (<code>entity/data/playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>EntityRuntime.playerSystems</code>) runs them over every store entity the client moves. The entity pass ticks breath and hunger for a player they reach, then the meal and the mining; <code>applyClientMovement</code> adds the move to the fall, charges the walk and pays for a landing; the blow that kills a player ends its drowning and its mining, and <code>revive</code> resets its attributes, lungs and movement flags. Blows go through <code>DamagePipeline</code> with the facts the legacy path gives the cause (<code>route: &#39;component&#39;</code>, graced and mitigated). Each write is logged through <code>EntityStore.written</code> and undone through <code>EntityRuntime.writeField</code>, which hands a player component field to <code>PlayerSystems.restore</code>. <code>entity/data/playerSystems.test.ts</code> tests each system against a store player.</li><li>One store player has one set of components: <code>new StorePlayer(entity, identity, runtime.playerSystems)</code> builds them through <code>PlayerSystems.attach</code> over its own attribute table, which closes C4e&#39;s second switch item for the components (the inventory still logs nothing) and its fourth for the saturation clamp. Built without the systems, as nothing outside tests does, a <code>StorePlayer</code> keeps unlogged components.</li><li>Everything C4c left unlogged is a component field now: <code>vitals.air</code>, <code>vitals.breathing</code> and <code>movement.sprinting</code>, kept in the actor metadata as food is kept in the attributes, <code>mining.block</code> and <code>consumption.meal</code> as plain data, and <code>attributes.&lt;name&gt;</code> for absorption, knockback resistance, the three movement speeds, follow range, attack damage and luck. A legacy write to one of those attributes goes through <code>Entity.setAttributeValue</code>. The meal is data: what a finished meal does is the held item&#39;s <code>onConsume</code>, resolved when it finishes, and a store player reaches its hand and the finish through the host facts <code>heldItemOf</code> and <code>finishMeal</code>, which no live host supplies yet.</li><li>Breath has one owner, the breath rule. A store player&#39;s shipped <code>minecraft:breathable</code> keeps no air: <code>PlayerSystems.beforeComponents</code> clears its state before every component tick, so its own tick does nothing. That appends one component entry on a player&#39;s first tick. The rule keeps today&#39;s numbers, which differ from the shipped component&#39;s (five ticks of air back a tick, not four; two damage every twenty ticks, not every ten).</li><li>Every system reads loaded chunks only, and <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code> lost the breath and landing checks. A head in an unloaded chunk waits: no air is spent or regained. A crack tick on an unloaded block sends nothing and the mining goes on, where it stopped the cracks. A landing on a block nobody can read costs nothing, as before.</li><li>Fixed on the way: the landing check read the blocks under a legacy player&#39;s eyes, which the client reports, so hay, slime or a bed under the feet never broke a fall. The <code>fall-damage</code> client scenario lands on hay now.</li><li>The replay digest hashes the new component fields, so absolute digests of a world with a player changed. Two replays still match, and <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes unchanged.</li><li><code>player-survival</code> in the <code>entities.behavior</code> parity family compares the rules with Java and the shipped player file: a lungful of 300 ticks, two damage twenty ticks after the lungs empty, the fall damage formula, and the exhaustion costs in <code>minecraft:exhaustion_values</code>. Bedrock ships a walk cost of 0 and the server charges 0.01 a meter, which the scenario leaves out.</li><li>What C4h has to switch on: <ol><li>Inputs. The input step calls <code>PlayerSystems.beginMeal</code> and <code>cancelMeal</code> where <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code> calls <code>beginConsuming</code> and <code>cancelConsuming</code>, <code>setMining</code> where <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> sets the mining (after <code>World.startBlockCracking</code>, which sends the start), and the shared <code>addJumpExhaustion</code> rule and the <code>movement.sprinting</code> field where <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> handles a jump and a sprint. A server move of a store player (<code>MobBody.teleportTo</code>) has to zero <code>vitals.fallDistance</code>, as a legacy teleport does; nothing does that yet.</li><li>Game mode. Every store player counts as a survival player (<code>SurvivalPlayer.vulnerable</code> is always true) until its game mode is a component the systems read. A creative store player would starve, drown and take falls.</li><li>Ground state. The fall rule reads a store player&#39;s ground from the body&#39;s <code>on_ground</code> condition, which <code>applyClientMovement</code> writes, and the <code>movement.onGround</code> component field stays stale. One of the two has to go.</li><li>The meal clock. A store meal counts out on the level clock, a legacy one on the server tick. A live host has to supply <code>heldItemOf</code> and <code>finishMeal</code>, or no store meal finishes.</li><li>Metadata. C4g&#39;s note asks for logged homes for air, breathing and sprinting: they are <code>vitals.air</code>, <code>vitals.breathing</code> and <code>movement.sprinting</code> of the store player&#39;s components, held in the components&#39; own <code>Metadata</code>. <code>StorePlayerActor</code>&#39;s projection does not read them yet, and the breath packet throttle (<code>Player.syncBreath</code>) has no store counterpart; replication sends them from the log in slice 7.</li><li>The mining runtime id. A store player&#39;s crack tick reads <code>ContentBlockAccess.runtimeIdAt</code>, which the live adapter supplies. A host without it never stops a store player&#39;s cracks.</li></ol></li><li>Found on the way: starvation ignores the difficulty (#2968), and a player inside a solid block takes no suffocation damage (#2969). Hay, honey and beds cancel a fall outright where Java and Bedrock take most of it, which <code>entity/Environment.ts</code> records as a known simplification.</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass: sprint until the food bar drops, starve on Hard and heal on a full bar, swim under until the bubbles run out and drown, surface and watch the bar refill, fall eleven blocks onto stone and onto a hay bale, eat a steak and switch items halfway through a second one, mine a block slowly and have another player break it, and die while mining underwater, then respawn with full food, air and a standing body.</li></ul><p>C4f, the session&#39;s entity id and the split of the conversions, as of its first run.</p><ul><li>A <code>PlayerSession</code> holds its player&#39;s runtime id (<code>runtimeId</code>) and login identity (<code>identity</code>, a <code>PlayerIdentity</code>), and no <code>Player</code>. <code>getPlayer()</code> is the one accessor from a session to its player&#39;s state: it finds the player by id in a <code>SessionPlayers</code> directory (<code>network/sessionPlayers.ts</code>). Live players are filed in <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, one for the server, from <code>SessionManager.getLegacyPlayers()</code>. <code>createConnectedPlayer</code> files the player, and <code>PlayerSession.disable</code> forgets it, after which <code>getPlayer()</code> throws and <code>isConnected()</code> answers false. <code>SessionManager.claimIdentity</code> reads the held identity.</li><li>The world&#39;s entity map cannot be the lookup: a player is not in it from the login to <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>, nor between the two worlds of a dimension change, and the session speaks for it the whole time. The directory has the <code>of</code> and <code>delete</code> of <code>StorePlayers</code>, so C4h&#39;s switch is the directory <code>createConnectedPlayer</code> hands the session, <code>EntityRuntimes.players</code> in place of <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, and the accessor&#39;s return type. C4h then deletes <code>LegacyPlayers</code> and <code>sessionPlayerRefOf</code>.</li><li>Session methods that await read the player once before their first await, so a player that leaves during a send behaves as before. Tests build sessions with <code>sessionFor</code> and <code>sessionPlayerFields</code>, and test servers get a real directory from <code>sessionsWith</code> (<code>testing/playerSession.test.helper.ts</code>).</li></ul><p>Converting a file means replacing each <code>Player</code> it names with the narrowest of these: <code>session.runtimeId</code> or <code>session.identity</code> (and <code>playerKeyOf(identity)</code> for a keyed service), a <code>ShownPlayer</code>, a structural port the file declares for what it reads (as <code>SurvivalPlayer</code> and <code>PlayerRecordSource</code> do), or, for state the store does not hold yet, <code>session.getPlayer()</code> narrowed to such a port. <code>git grep -lP &quot;\\bPlayer\\b&quot; -- &#39;packages/**/*.ts&#39;</code> finds 86 Prismarine source files at this run, and 60 more reach the player through <code>getPlayer()</code> or <code>getNetworkSession()</code> without naming it, 146 in all. The other packages&#39; 49 hits are Bedrock&#39;s own word (<code>MovePlayerPacket</code>, the <code>Player</code> command origin), not the class. The 127 test files and test helpers that name it (the spike counted 117) are C4h&#39;s, except the tests beside a converted file, which its group updates.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th style="text-align:right;">Files</th><th>What the files need</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F1, handlers that only name the player</td><td style="text-align:right;">42</td><td>The id and the identity: a log line&#39;s name, a comparison with the sender&#39;s runtime id. Six pass the player to <code>blockTargetRejection</code>, and <code>BlockTarget.ts</code> narrows that parameter to the position and world it reads</td></tr><tr><td>F2, handlers that change the player</td><td style="text-align:right;">28</td><td>The identity for logs, and the player&#39;s state through <code>session.getPlayer()</code> narrowed to a port per use: inventory and crafting input (<code>ItemStackRequest</code>, <code>ContainerClose</code>, <code>BookEdit</code>, <code>MobEquipment</code>), position and world (<code>Interact</code>, <code>LevelSoundEvent</code>, <code>ActorEvent</code>), game mode, flight and skin (<code>SetPlayerGameType</code>, <code>SetDefaultGameType</code>, <code>RequestAbility</code>, <code>PlayerSkin</code>, a <code>ShownPlayer</code> for the skin), the join (<code>Login</code>, <code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code>, <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>), movement and survival (<code>MovePlayer</code>, <code>PlayerAction</code>, <code>InventoryTransaction</code>, <code>Respawn</code>), and a kick or a disconnect as a session operation (<code>Disconnect</code>, <code>ResourcePackChunkRequest</code>, <code>PlayerAuthInput</code>). <code>CommandRequest</code>, <code>SettingsCommand</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>RequestPermissions</code> and <code>BossEvent</code> hand the player to F3&#39;s or F4&#39;s code and keep passing <code>session.getPlayer()</code> until those take something narrower</td></tr><tr><td>F3, sessions, replication and worlds</td><td style="text-align:right;">16</td><td><code>PlayerSession</code>&#39;s remaining 40 accessor reads (view distance becomes session state; position and world for chunk streaming, inventory and attributes for its own sends), <code>NetworkWorldReplicator</code> finding a viewer&#39;s session by entity id (C4g&#39;s six <code>instanceof Player</code> methods), <code>BossBar</code> keyed by runtime id, <code>SessionManager</code>&#39;s lookups by id and identity, <code>World</code>&#39;s player map, viewers and <code>savePlayerData</code> keyed by <code>keyOfPlayer</code> (C4e&#39;s third item), <code>playerRecord</code>, <code>PlayerRecords</code>, <code>Level</code>, <code>WorldChangeSink</code>, <code>DragonFight</code>, <code>DimensionTravel</code>, <code>spawnPosition</code>, <code>startGameData</code>, <code>NetworkDispatcher</code>, and <code>entity/data/live.ts</code> and <code>Entities.ts</code>, whose host facts already answer by id</td></tr><tr><td>F4, services, commands, items and events</td><td style="text-align:right;">30</td><td>Keyed services: advancement progress, raising and triggers, and the enchantment seed take the key and the server, the ender chest store takes the key, bans and permissions read the identity. A port for state: <code>anvil</code>, <code>item/actorPort</code>, <code>item/interaction</code> and <code>advancement/subject</code>. <code>CommandManager</code>, <code>command/host/*</code> (7) and <code>ParseTargetSelector</code> take a sender port and reach a target&#39;s session by id. The ten <code>events/player</code> classes carry the id and the identity, plus what each reports</td></tr><tr><td>Nothing until C4h</td><td style="text-align:right;">30</td><td><code>Player.ts</code>, <code>Human.ts</code>, <code>Entity.ts</code>, <code>sessionPlayers.ts</code>, <code>createConnectedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>ClientConnection.ts</code> (its teardown calls <code>Player.disable</code>) and <code>index.ts</code> are <code>Player</code> itself or its wiring. 23 files name it only in prose or a <code>{@link}</code>, among them <code>storePlayer.ts</code>, <code>storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>ShownPlayer.ts</code>, <code>RespawnSearch.ts</code> and <code>jsprismarineCommands.ts</code>; C4h rewords them</td></tr></tbody></table><p>F1 is the files ActorPickRequestHandler, AnimateHandler, AnvilDamageHandler, BlockPickRequestHandler, BlockTarget, ClientCacheBlobStatusHandler, ClientCacheStatusHandler, ClientMovementPredictionSyncHandler, CommandBlockUpdateHandler, EmoteHandler, EmoteListHandler, LabTableHandler, LecternUpdateHandler, MapCreateLockedCopyHandler, MapInfoRequestHandler, ModalFormResponseHandler, MultiplayerSettingsHandler, NetworkStackLatencyHandler, NpcRequestHandler, PacketViolationWarningHandler, PartyChangedHandler, PhotoTransferHandler, PlayerLocationHandler, PlayerToggleCrafterSlotRequestHandler, PositionTrackingDBClientRequestHandler, PurchaseReceiptHandler, RequestChunkRadiusHandler, ResourcePacksReadyForValidationHandler, ServerSettingsRequestHandler, ServerboundDiagnosticsHandler, ServerboundLoadingScreenHandler, ServerboundPackSettingChangeHandler, SetActorLinkHandler, SetDifficultyHandler, SetPlayerFurnaceOptionsHandler, ShowCreditsHandler, SimpleEventHandler, StructureBlockUpdateHandler, StructureTemplateDataRequestHandler, SubChunkRequestHandler, SubClientLoginHandler, UpdateClientOptionsHandler.</p><p>All four groups run side by side, each in its own worktree, because no file is in two groups. They keep to these rules so that none breaks another.</p><ul><li>Narrow a parameter freely: a <code>Player</code> still satisfies any port drawn from it, so a caller in another group keeps compiling. Narrow a return type or an exported field only when every reader in the repository is in the group, and leave it for C4h otherwise.</li><li>A caller whose callee is in another group keeps passing <code>session.getPlayer()</code>.</li><li>Only F3 edits <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> and <code>SessionManager.ts</code>. A group that needs a new session member asks F3 for it or waits for C4h.</li><li>No group edits <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>Human.ts</code>, <code>Entity.ts</code> or an existing file under <code>testing/</code>. A group that needs a test helper adds a file of its own beside the others.</li><li>Each group adds its own Changeset. F4&#39;s events and command host change plugin-facing types, which the versioning policy makes <code>minor</code>.</li></ul><p>F2, the handlers that change the player, left these for C4h.</p><ul><li>Every F2 handler logs <code>session.identity.name</code> and compares the runtime id a packet names with <code>session.runtimeId</code>. The rest of what it reads or changes of the player goes through <code>session.getPlayer()</code> typed as a port for that use. The ports two handlers share are in <code>network/handler/playerPorts.ts</code>: <code>KickedPlayer</code>, <code>PlacedPlayer</code> (the position and the world), <code>InventoryHolder</code>, <code>CraftingHolder</code> and <code>FlyingPlayer</code>. The others live in their handler: <code>GamemodePlayer</code> in <code>SetPlayerGameTypeHandler</code>, <code>ReskinnedPlayer</code> in <code>PlayerSkinHandler</code>, <code>JoiningPlayer</code> in <code>ResourcePackClientResponseHandler</code>, <code>SpawningPlayer</code> in <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitializedHandler</code>, <code>MovedPlayer</code> in <code>MovePlayerHandler</code>, <code>RespawningPlayer</code> in <code>RespawnHandler</code>, <code>ActingPlayer</code> in <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> and <code>Trader</code> in <code>tradeScreen.ts</code>. The rest are picks of <code>ShownPlayer</code> (<code>isOp</code>, <code>gamemode</code>) and <code>ShownActor</code> (<code>getType</code>, and the whole actor for <code>ActorEventHandler</code>&#39;s relay).</li><li>Session operations: <code>DisconnectHandler</code> reads the address from the connection, which is the address the player was built with, and <code>PlayerAuthInputHandler</code> and <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code> ask <code>session.isConnected()</code> in place of <code>Player.isOnline()</code>. After the player has left the server, a <code>Disconnect</code> still closes the connection and an input or a transaction is dropped, where <code>getPlayer()</code> threw before. <code>PlayerSkinHandler</code> matches the packet&#39;s UUID with <code>session.identity.uuid</code> and leaves the sender out of the relay by runtime id, as <code>MobEquipmentHandler</code> does.</li><li>What still takes <code>session.getPlayer()</code>, because the callee is another group&#39;s or C4h&#39;s: <code>PlayerMoveEvent</code>, <code>PlayerSpawnEvent</code>, <code>PlayerSession.sendMove</code>, <code>createStartGameData</code>, <code>playerSpawnPositionPacket</code>, <code>World.addEntity</code>, <code>hitBlock</code>, <code>breakBlock</code>, <code>dropFromPlayer</code> and an audience&#39;s <code>exclude</code>, the content interactions, <code>meleeAttack</code>, <code>loose</code>, <code>consumeTicksOf</code>, the enchanting and anvil code <code>workstation.ts</code> calls, <code>advancementItemCrafted</code>, <code>containerAnchorOf</code> and <code>openContainerSlots</code>, the command manager, the chat, the permission check and <code>BossBar.answerQuery</code>. <code>workstation.ts</code> does nothing with its player but pass it to the enchanting code, so it is unchanged. <code>LoginHandler</code> still builds the <code>Player</code>, which is C4h&#39;s.</li><li>What a <code>StorePlayer</code> lacks, by port. Each is a method a legacy <code>Player</code> has; the store half named after it is where C4h can find the state. <ul><li><code>PlacedPlayer.getWorld</code>, which twelve of the handlers read. A store player&#39;s world is the runtime it is in, which it does not hold.</li><li><code>KickedPlayer.kick</code>. The session can own it, see below.</li><li><code>CraftingHolder.getCraftingInput</code>: no crafting grid.</li><li><code>FlyingPlayer.setFlying</code>: only <code>restoreFlying</code>, with no event and no abilities sent.</li><li><code>GamemodePlayer</code>: <code>isOp</code>, <code>getPermissions</code> and <code>setGamemode</code>; only the <code>gamemode</code> field. <code>StorePlayerActor.isOp</code> answers the <code>ShownPlayer</code> picks.</li><li><code>JoiningPlayer</code>: <code>getJoinSpawn</code> and <code>sendSettings</code>. <code>SpawningPlayer</code>: <code>completeSpawn</code>.</li><li><code>MovedPlayer</code>: <code>isOnGround</code>, <code>setPlayerPosition</code> and <code>setOnGround</code>, which <code>EntityRuntime.applyClientMovement</code> and the <code>on_ground</code> condition cover.</li><li><code>RespawningPlayer</code>: <code>isAlive</code> and <code>getRespawnPoint</code>. <code>EntityRuntime.revive</code> and the level&#39;s <code>RespawnSearch</code> cover them.</li><li><code>ActingPlayer</code>: <code>getMining</code> and <code>setMining</code> (<code>PlayerSystems.setMining</code>), <code>respawn</code> (<code>EntityRuntime.revive</code>), <code>addJumpExhaustion</code> (the shared rule over the components), <code>setSprinting</code> (<code>movement.sprinting</code>), <code>setSneaking</code>, <code>setSleeping</code> and <code>setFlying</code>.</li><li><code>Trader.getExperience</code>: private, though its <code>PlayerComponents.experience()</code> has it.</li><li><code>Pick&lt;ShownActor, &#39;getType&#39;&gt;</code>: none on <code>StorePlayer</code>; the entity&#39;s identifier has it. <code>ActorEventHandler</code> relays a <code>ShownActor</code>, which for a store player is its <code>StorePlayerActor</code>, not the <code>StorePlayer</code>.</li></ul></li><li>Session members F2 needs from F3 or C4h, used through <code>getPlayer()</code> until then: <ol><li><code>PlayerSession.kick(reason, code)</code>. <code>Player.kick</code> is the connection&#39;s <code>disconnect(reason, false, code)</code> and a verbose log line, so the session can own it and <code>Player.kick</code> can call it. <code>KickedPlayer</code> then goes.</li><li>A way from the session to the player&#39;s world, which F3&#39;s chunk streaming needs too. <code>PlacedPlayer.getWorld</code> then reads it.</li></ol></li><li>Tests: the handler tests give their session doubles <code>runtimeId</code> and <code>identity</code>, the latter from <code>identityNamed</code> in <code>testing/sessionIdentity.test.helper.ts</code>. <code>sessionNearBlocks</code> in <code>testing/blockTarget.test.helper.ts</code> builds a session without either, so <code>BookEditHandler.test.ts</code> adds the identity to the session it returns, and F1&#39;s handlers that use the helper may need the same.</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass: none beyond C4f&#39;s, since no handler&#39;s behavior changed.</li></ul><h4 id="c4h-live-players-in-the-store-and-the-legacy-classes-deleted" tabindex="-1">C4h: live players in the store, and the legacy classes deleted <a class="header-anchor" href="#c4h-live-players-in-the-store-and-the-legacy-classes-deleted" aria-label="Permalink to “C4h: live players in the store, and the legacy classes deleted”">​</a></h4><p>Planned on 2026-11-11 at program head <code>7265972882</code>, after F1 to F4 landed. Its steps carry an <code>H-</code> prefix, because phases A to D already use the names A1, B1, C1 and D1. Counts are from that head. F3 and F4 wrote no notes on this page; their commit messages are the record, and what they left is folded in below.</p><p><strong>1. The switch list.</strong> Every item the C4b to C4g and F1 to F4 notes left for C4h, deduplicated. &quot;Done&quot; means a later package closed it, and the step that spawns live players only adds a regression test. &quot;Found&quot; means no note listed it. The last column names the step in part 2.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">#</th><th>Item</th><th>Files</th><th>Asked by</th><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td>Spawn each live player as a <code>minecraft:player</code> store entity, its <code>StorePlayer</code> filed in <code>EntityRuntimes.players</code>. Apply the saved record before the entity enters the store, so the load appends no entries</td><td><code>LoginHandler.ts</code>, <code>createConnectedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>Player.enable</code>, <code>storePlayers.ts</code></td><td>C4e 1, C4g</td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2</td><td>A store player with no entity, from the login to <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> and between the two stores of a dimension change. <code>StorePlayer</code>&#39;s constructor takes a <code>DataEntity</code></td><td><code>storePlayer.ts</code>, <code>storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>PlayerSystems.attach</code></td><td>C4f states the gap; found, since no note designs it</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td>One id allocator outside <code>Entity</code>. <code>Entity.runtimeIdCount</code> is the counter both the legacy constructor and <code>LiveEntityRuntime</code>&#39;s <code>nextId</code> draw from</td><td><code>Entity.ts:103</code>, <code>live.ts:571</code>, new <code>entity/entityIds.ts</code></td><td>adapter item 1; found</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">4</td><td>The session&#39;s directory becomes <code>EntityRuntimes.players</code>, and <code>getPlayer()</code> returns the store player. Delete <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, <code>sessionPlayerRefOf</code> and <code>SessionManager.getLegacyPlayers</code></td><td><code>sessionPlayers.ts</code>, <code>createConnectedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>PlayerSession.ts</code>, <code>SessionManager.ts</code>, <code>testing/playerSession.test.helper.ts</code></td><td>C4f</td><td>H-D</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">5</td><td>Leaving removes the entity from its store and runs <code>players.delete(id)</code>. <code>ClientConnection</code>&#39;s teardown calls <code>session.getPlayer().disable()</code></td><td><code>ClientConnection.ts:90</code>, <code>PlayerSession.disable</code>, <code>Player.disable</code></td><td>C4f, C4g</td><td>H-B2, H-C2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">6</td><td>A dimension change, and a respawn into another dimension, leave one store and enter the other through <code>EntityRuntime.restore(record, at, id)</code> with the same id</td><td><code>PlayerSession.changeWorld</code>, the <code>addEntity</code> and <code>removeEntity</code> calls for players</td><td>C4b, C4g</td><td>H-S0 (entry points), H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">7</td><td>The five <code>instanceof Player</code> in <code>DimensionTravel</code>: <code>isMidDimensionChange</code>, <code>transfer</code>, nether portal creation, the End arrival height and <code>leaveEnd</code></td><td><code>world/portal/DimensionTravel.ts:101,151,295,333,349</code></td><td>F3</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">8</td><td>Inventory logging: a store player&#39;s <code>Inventory.observe</code>, <code>HumanInventory.observeCarried</code> and <code>CraftingInput.observe</code> report to <code>EntityStore.writtenSlot</code>, and the runtime undoes its <code>inventory-slot</code> entries, which replaces <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code></td><td><code>storePlayer.ts</code>, <code>playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>EntityRuntime.writeField</code></td><td>C4c, C4e 2</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">9</td><td>The inverse writer for player fields. Partly done: C4d&#39;s <code>PlayerSystems.restore</code> undoes component fields. <code>gamemode</code> and <code>inventory.handSlot</code> still go through the adapter</td><td><code>playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code></td><td>C4e 2</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">10</td><td>Game mode as a logged component field that <code>SurvivalPlayer.vulnerable</code> reads. Without it a creative store player starves, drowns and takes falls</td><td><code>playerComponents.ts</code>, <code>survivalPlayer.ts</code>, <code>playerSystems.ts:311</code>, <code>storePlayer.ts</code></td><td>C4d 2</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">11</td><td>One ground state: the body&#39;s <code>on_ground</code> condition or <code>movement.onGround</code></td><td><code>playerFall.ts</code>, <code>playerComponents.ts</code></td><td>C4d 3</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">12</td><td>The meal: one clock, and a live host that supplies <code>heldItemOf</code> and <code>finishMeal</code>, which no host does</td><td><code>playerMeal.ts</code>, <code>host.ts:130</code>, new <code>entity/data/playerHostFacts.ts</code>, wired into <code>live.ts</code></td><td>C4d 4</td><td>H-A1, H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">13</td><td>The mining runtime id on a live host. Done: <code>world/contentAdapter.ts:250</code> supplies <code>runtimeIdAt</code></td><td>none</td><td>C4d 6</td><td>done</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">14</td><td>The input step drives the systems: <code>beginMeal</code> and <code>cancelMeal</code> where <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code> calls <code>beginConsuming</code> and <code>cancelConsuming</code>, <code>setMining</code> after <code>World.startBlockCracking</code>, <code>addJumpExhaustion</code> and <code>movement.sprinting</code> on a jump and a sprint, the head yaw beside <code>applyClientMovement</code>, and a server move (<code>MobBody.teleportTo</code>) that zeroes <code>vitals.fallDistance</code></td><td><code>playerSystems.ts</code> (H-A1), the <code>Player</code> methods the handlers call (H-B2)</td><td>C4d 1, C4e 6, C4g</td><td>H-A1, H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">15</td><td><code>StorePlayerActor</code>&#39;s metadata reads <code>vitals.air</code>, <code>vitals.breathing</code>, <code>movement.sprinting</code> and can-fly from the game mode, and <code>Player.syncBreath</code>&#39;s throttle gets a store counterpart</td><td><code>storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>playerBreath.ts</code></td><td>C4d 5, C4g</td><td>H-A1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">16</td><td>The record&#39;s maximum health is not applied, because the type decides it. Both are 20. No change</td><td>none</td><td>C4e 5</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">17</td><td>Copies. <code>EYE_HEIGHT</code> and <code>eyePositionAt</code> live in <code>entity/Human.ts</code>, with 9 importers. <code>StorePlayer.setPortalCooldown</code> repeats <code>Entity.setPortalCooldown</code>&#39;s clamp. The saturation clamp is done (C4d)</td><td>new <code>entity/playerEyes.ts</code>, <code>storePlayer.ts</code></td><td>C4e 4</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">18</td><td>Out of chunk saves and the despawn cycle. Done: <code>ChunkSaveContext.ts:29</code> skips <code>minecraft:player</code> by type, and <code>runDespawnCycle</code> skips an entity with no spawn pool, which a spawned player never has</td><td>none</td><td>C4b, C4e 7, C4g</td><td>done, H-B2 tests it</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">19</td><td>Out of chunk unloads. <code>World</code> removes every store entity <code>storeInChunk</code> files in an unloading chunk (<code>World.ts:3318</code>), and <code>markEntitiesInChunkChanged</code> (<code>:3571</code>) marks a chunk changed for any of them, players included</td><td><code>World.ts</code></td><td>found</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">20</td><td>Ticking anywhere. The legacy loop exempts a player from the ticking-chunk check (<code>World.ts:1153</code>); <code>EntityRuntime.tickEntities</code> and the actor update (<code>World.ts:1159</code>) apply it to every store entity</td><td><code>EntityRuntime.ts</code>, <code>World.ts</code></td><td>found</td><td>H-A1 (store), H-S0 (world)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">21</td><td>A player unit that throws disconnects its session instead of removing the entity</td><td><code>EntityRuntime.ts</code>, <code>EntityStore.ts</code>, <code>live.ts</code></td><td>C1c; missing from the H-C4 notes</td><td>H-A1 (hook), H-B2 (wire)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">22</td><td>A player&#39;s blows run <code>DamagePipeline</code>: a mob&#39;s blow on a player (<code>live.ts:385,395,442</code> start <code>target.damage</code> without awaiting), a player&#39;s swing, a PvP blow, the <code>entityDamage</code> hook and the grace period on one path, and <code>applySonicBoom</code> without a promise</td><td><code>live.ts</code>, <code>Combat.ts</code>, <code>legacyDamage.ts</code></td><td>H-C3, C4b</td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">23</td><td>The PvP rule tests <code>instanceof Human</code></td><td><code>Combat.ts:111</code></td><td>found</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">24</td><td>Death and respawn through the store: <code>kill</code> keeps the player in <code>dying</code>, <code>EntityRuntime.revive</code> brings it back, and the respawn request reads the spawn point from the component</td><td><code>Player.onDeath</code>, <code>Player.respawn</code>, <code>RespawnRequests.ts</code>, <code>playerRespawn.ts</code></td><td>B3b, C4b</td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">25</td><td>Delete <code>MobEquipmentHandler</code>&#39;s relay. <code>StoreActors.sync</code> sends the equipment, and armor and off-hand changes then reach viewers at once (#2965)</td><td><code>MobEquipmentHandler.ts</code></td><td>C4g</td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">26</td><td>Game mode, abilities, skin and the player list pass the actor to <code>sendGameTypeFor</code>, <code>sendAbilities</code> and <code>playerListEntryOf</code>. A skin change writes <code>StorePlayer.skin</code>. A game mode change sends the game type and abilities itself, since <code>StoreActors.sync</code> does not notice it</td><td><code>Player.setGamemode</code>, <code>Player.enable</code>, <code>PlayerSkinHandler.ts</code>, <code>ResourcePackClientResponseHandler.ts</code></td><td>C4g</td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">27</td><td>The replicator&#39;s own-client copies by session. Done by F3: <code>World.viewerOf</code> (<code>072a0f5029</code>), and viewers are sessions (<code>World.getViewers</code>, <code>9c4ec138a2</code>)</td><td>none</td><td>C4g</td><td>done</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">28</td><td>Keyed services. Done: advancements and the enchantment seed take a key (<code>c594bb33f3</code>), and <code>World.getPlayerData</code> and <code>lastPlayerRecord</code> read the key and the world (<code>93f0942ff9</code>, <code>2130d83f79</code>). Left: <code>World.savePlayerData</code> and <code>playerRecordOf</code> need a <code>KeyedPlayer</code>, because <code>enchantmentSeedOf</code> takes <code>getServer()</code> from a legacy player</td><td><code>World.ts:4183</code>, <code>playerRecord.ts:155</code></td><td>C4e 3</td><td>H-B3</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">29</td><td>The legacy-player overloads kept beside the key forms: <code>PlayerKeySource</code> and <code>KeyedPlayerSource</code>, <code>BossBar</code>&#39;s methods, the ban check and <code>PermissionManager</code></td><td><code>keyedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>BossBar.ts</code>, <code>ban/</code>, <code>permission/</code></td><td>F3, F4</td><td>H-C2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">30</td><td>The chat recipient&#39;s <code>receive</code> finds its session by identity</td><td><code>ChatManager.ts</code>, <code>Player.ts</code></td><td>B3b</td><td>H-B3</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">31</td><td><code>Player.restoreHealth</code>, <code>restoreFlying</code> and <code>Player</code> as a <code>PlayerRecordTarget</code> go. <code>StorePlayer</code> is the record&#39;s one target</td><td><code>Player.ts</code>, <code>playerRecord.ts</code></td><td>C4e</td><td>H-D</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">32</td><td>Players found by class: <code>asPlayer</code> (<code>item/actorPort.ts:69</code>), <code>playerSessionOf</code> (<code>command/host/support.ts:181</code>), the trade, filter view and swing in <code>live.ts:852,914,934</code>, and <code>World.ts:1153,3160,3487,3543,3575,3805</code>. Each finds the session by id (<code>World.viewerOf</code>) or tests the type</td><td>those files</td><td>F4, C3b, C4g</td><td>H-S0</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">33</td><td><code>WorldActor</code> narrows to <code>StoreActor</code>. <code>World</code>&#39;s entity map, <code>players</code> map, <code>getEntities</code> and <code>getEntity</code> go, and so do <code>EntityIndex</code>&#39;s legacy half (<code>addLegacy</code>, <code>refileLegacy</code>, <code>removeLegacy</code>, <code>legacyWithin</code>) and <code>Explosion</code>&#39;s legacy query</td><td><code>WorldActor.ts</code>, <code>World.ts</code>, <code>entityIndex.ts</code>, <code>Explosion.ts</code>, the 19 <code>Entity</code> importers</td><td>C1b, C3b</td><td>H-C1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">34</td><td>No array copy. <code>EntityRuntime.all()</code> spreads the store for the entity pass, the despawn cycle and curing. <code>World.getActors</code> copies and sorts for selectors, saves, the digest and the content adapter&#39;s <code>at</code>, <code>near</code> and <code>countOfType</code>, and <code>shownActors</code> concatenates</td><td><code>EntityRuntime.ts:717</code>, <code>World.ts:3660,3698</code></td><td>slice 5 target, C1b, C3b</td><td>H-C4 (<code>all()</code>), H-C1 (<code>getActors</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">35</td><td>The replay digest&#39;s player branch (<code>World.ts:3805</code>) hashes a legacy player&#39;s slot counts and <code>componentSnapshot</code>. It moves to the store player, so digests of a world with a player change once</td><td><code>World.ts</code></td><td>C4c</td><td>H-B2, re-baselined in its own commit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">36</td><td>A world without an entity store. Seven <code>getEntityRuntime?.</code> sites and five <code>hasEntityStore()</code> fallbacks in <code>World.ts</code> keep a path for a world that runs the written entity classes. A world needs a store to hold its players</td><td><code>World.ts</code>, <code>legacyAdapter.ts:52</code></td><td>found</td><td>H-C1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">37</td><td><code>ItemActorBody</code> becomes a <code>StoreActor</code> subclass: portal travel through <code>StoreActor.update</code> and <code>transferStoreEntity</code>, <code>AddItemActor</code> through <code>spawnTo</code>, and merge, pickup and unload through the store</td><td><code>itemBodies.ts</code> (deleted), <code>storeActors.ts</code>, <code>live.ts</code>, <code>EntitySerializer.ts</code>, <code>World.ts:3152</code>, <code>PlayerSession.sendAddItemActor</code>, <code>DimensionTravel.transfer</code></td><td>H-C2, C3b</td><td>H-A2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">38</td><td><code>GenericEntity</code> is the load path for every stored actor. <code>EntitySerializer.fromNBT</code> builds one, <code>World.createStoredEntities</code> and the level snapshot (<code>World.ts:761</code>) hand it to the store, and an actor whose type no pack declares stays in the world as a live <code>GenericEntity</code></td><td><code>EntitySerializer.ts</code>, <code>World.ts:761,1712,3148</code>, <code>live.ts:1104</code>, <code>Entities.ts</code> (<code>writtenEntities</code>)</td><td>found</td><td>H-A2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">39</td><td><code>World.ITEM_ENTITY_LIMIT</code> counts item bodies</td><td><code>World.ts:2927</code></td><td>H-C2</td><td>H-A2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">40</td><td>Delete <code>Entity.ts</code>, <code>GenericEntity.ts</code>, <code>Human.ts</code>, <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code>, <code>Entities.ts</code>&#39;s <code>Human</code> and its deprecated <code>Player</code> alias, <code>Entity.restoreLoggedField</code> and <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code>, <code>testing/playerDouble.test.helper.ts</code>, and <code>Player.viewDistance</code>, which nothing has written since F3</td><td>those files</td><td>C4c, C4f, F3, F4</td><td>H-B2, H-D</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">41</td><td>The public API: the root <code>Player</code> export (<code>index.ts:27</code>), <code>Entity</code> through <code>entity/index.ts</code>&#39;s <code>export *</code>, <code>Entities.Human</code> and <code>Entities.Player</code>, and the <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> subpath to each deleted module. Prismarine is <code>0.20.0</code>, so the Changeset is <code>minor</code></td><td><code>index.ts</code>, <code>entity/index.ts</code>, <code>.changeset/</code></td><td>found</td><td>H-B2 (<code>Entities</code>), H-D</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">42</td><td>Reword prose. 43 source files name <code>Player</code> without importing it; C4f counted 23</td><td>each file&#39;s owner</td><td>C4f</td><td>H-C1 to H-C4</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">43</td><td>The slice 5 target as a proxy test beside <code>noMirror.test.ts</code>: no <code>Entity</code>, <code>GenericEntity</code> or <code>Human</code> class, no subclass of <code>StoreActor</code> but the store&#39;s own, and no copy from <code>all()</code></td><td>new <code>entity/data/noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code></td><td>slice 5 target</td><td>H-C4, gating at H-D</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three items are done (13, 18, 27) and three partly (9, 17, 28). Eight were found in the code: 2, 3, 19, 20, 23, 36, 38 and 41, and item 21 was in C1c&#39;s notes but no H-C4 note.</p><p><strong>2. The sequence.</strong> Every step keeps the server working and lands on the program branch before the next one starts. &quot;Unit tests&quot; means <code>pnpm exec vitest run --since-last-run</code> and the Prismarine suite in full once. &quot;The replay suite&quot; is <code>packages/client/src/SessionReplay.test.ts</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Work</th><th>Gate</th><th>Risks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H-S0 seams</td><td>Items 3, 7, 17, 19, 20 (world side), 23 and 32, and <code>World.addPlayer</code> and <code>removePlayer</code>, which call <code>addEntity</code> and <code>removeEntity</code> for now and which <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitializedHandler</code>, <code>PlayerSession.changeWorld</code> and <code>Player.disable</code> call (item 6). No behavior changes. Afterwards only <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>Human.ts</code>, <code>Entities.ts</code> and the session directory name the player class</td><td>Unit tests; the replay suite with unchanged digests; parity <code>blocks.portals</code>, <code>protocol.dimension-change</code>, <code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code>; client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>item-drop-pickup</code></td><td>A lookup by id that misses a player outside a world&#39;s player map. F3 made <code>World.viewerOf</code> answer for a player joined or not; a unit test covers the join window</td></tr><tr><td>Wave H-A</td><td>H-A1, H-A2 and H-A3 side by side, in part 3. Live players are still legacy</td><td>Per package, below</td><td>Per package</td></tr><tr><td>H-B1 Player stops being an entity</td><td><code>Player</code> holds a body instead of being one: a <code>LegacyPlayerBody extends Human</code>, which the world&#39;s maps, the index and the replicator hold, and <code>Player</code> delegates to it. Code that hands a player to entity code (a blow&#39;s attacker, the portal touch, the events, the triggers) passes the body. A refactor with no behavior change</td><td>Unit tests; the replay suite with unchanged digests; every parity family; the full client e2e suite</td><td>Identity checks that compared the <code>Player</code> object itself, such as <code>attacker === target</code> and <code>world.getEntity(id) === player</code></td></tr><tr><td>H-B2 the body is the store entity</td><td><code>LegacyPlayerBody</code> becomes the <code>StorePlayerActor</code> of a spawned store player. Items 1, 5 (store side), 6, 12 (wired), 14 (routed through <code>Player</code>&#39;s methods), 21 (wired), 22, 24, 25, 26 and 35. Deletes <code>Human.ts</code> with <code>Entities.Human</code> and <code>Entities.Player</code>, the player half of <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code> (<code>logLegacySlot</code>, <code>logLegacyImpulse</code>, the player fields), <code>restoreLoggedField</code>, <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code> and <code>playerDouble</code>. <code>Player</code> keeps no entity state</td><td>Unit tests; the replay suite, with the digest re-baseline in a commit of its own that says only player hashing changed; every parity family; the full client e2e suite, reporting <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>duplicate-login</code>, <code>movement</code>, <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>movement-rotation</code>, <code>look-direction</code>, <code>jump</code>, <code>gait</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>teleport-while-dead</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>fall-damage</code>, <code>player-knockback</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>spawnpoint-respawn</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>villager-trade</code> and <code>attack-entity</code> by name; the in-game checks the C4d, C4e and C4g notes list, with two vanilla clients</td><td>The largest step. What differs by path today becomes one path: the grace period and <code>entityDamage</code> on a player, the breath numbers (C4d), the meal timing and the metadata. A player not yet spawned must be neither targetable nor shown (question 1)</td></tr><tr><td>H-B3 name the homes</td><td>Each <code>Player</code> method moves its body to its home and leaves a one-line delegate. Client operations go to <code>PlayerSession</code>: <code>completeSpawn</code>, <code>sendSettings</code>, <code>getJoinSpawn</code>, <code>sendInitialSpawnChunks</code>, <code>sendMessage</code>, and the sends of <code>setGamemode</code> and <code>setFlying</code>. Simulation goes to <code>PlayerSystems</code> and <code>EntityRuntime</code>: <code>setMining</code>, <code>beginConsuming</code>, <code>respawn</code>, <code>setSprinting</code>, <code>setSneaking</code>, and the writes of <code>setGamemode</code> and <code>setFlying</code>. Items 28 and 30</td><td>Unit tests; the replay suite with unchanged digests; client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>dimensions</code></td><td>Low. The delegates keep every caller compiling</td></tr><tr><td>Wave H-C</td><td>H-C1 to H-C4 side by side, in part 3. Each moves callers off <code>Player</code>, <code>Entity</code> and <code>WorldActor</code></td><td>Per package, below</td><td>A caller in another package&#39;s file; the rules below</td></tr><tr><td>H-D delete</td><td>Items 4, 31, 40 and 41, and what wave C left: <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>Entity.ts</code>, the rest of <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code>, <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, <code>World.getPlayers</code> (its callers use <code>getViewers</code>), the <code>WorldActor</code> alias, the root and <code>Entities</code> exports. Lower the barrel baseline (<code>node scripts/check-barrels.mts --update</code>). The <code>noLegacyEntity</code> test gates. A <code>minor</code> Changeset for <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code>. Update <code>docs/data-driven/entities.md</code>, this page&#39;s progress and <code>server-rearchitecture.md</code></td><td><code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>typecheck</code>, <code>lint</code>, <code>lint:custom</code> and <code>test</code>; the parity suite; the replay suite; the full client e2e suite, and <code>pnpm run e2e:client:dist</code> for the built package</td><td>A plugin-facing type change; question 2</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>3. Side by side.</strong> Two waves of packages, each package in its own worktree. No file is in two packages of one wave, and a file not listed belongs to no package of that wave. The rules are C4f&#39;s: narrow a parameter freely, narrow a return type or an exported member only when every reader is in the package, and leave the rest for the next serial step. Each package converts the tests beside its files and the tests part 4 gives it.</p><p>Wave H-A, after H-S0:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Items</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H-A1 the store player, complete</td><td>2, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 (the facts module), 14 (the system calls), 15, 20 (the store pass), 21 (the hook)</td><td><code>entity/storePlayer.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>entity/playerComponents.ts</code>, <code>entity/survivalPlayer.ts</code>, <code>entity/playerHunger.ts</code>, <code>playerBreath.ts</code>, <code>playerFall.ts</code>, <code>playerMeal.ts</code>, <code>playerMining.ts</code>, <code>playerRespawn.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/EntityRuntime.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/EntityStore.ts</code>, <code>world/playerRecord.ts</code>, new <code>entity/data/playerHostFacts.ts</code>, <code>testing/storePlayer.test.helper.ts</code>. Gate: unit tests, <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> byte for byte, parity <code>entities.behavior</code> (<code>player-health</code>, <code>player-survival</code>). No live change, so no e2e</td></tr><tr><td>H-A2 the bodies that are not players</td><td>37, 38, 39</td><td><code>entity/data/itemBodies.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>, new <code>entity/data/itemActor.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/live.ts</code>, <code>entity/EntitySerializer.ts</code>, <code>entity/GenericEntity.ts</code>, <code>entity/Entities.ts</code>, <code>world/World.ts</code>, <code>world/Level.ts</code>, <code>world/portal/DimensionTravel.ts</code>, <code>network/PlayerSession.ts</code> (<code>sendAddItemActor</code> only). Gate: unit tests; the replay suite, re-baselined in its own commit if the item actors hash in a new order; parity <code>mapping.entities</code>, <code>entities.addactor</code>, <code>blocks.portals</code>; client e2e <code>item-drop-pickup</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>natural-spawn-placement</code>; in game, a stack thrown through a nether portal and a world reloaded with an actor no pack declares. After H-A2 the only subclass of <code>Entity</code> is the player&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td>H-A3 handler tests on ports</td><td>none, tests only</td><td>The 29 <code>network/handler/*.test.ts</code> files: each <code>stub&lt;Player&gt;</code> becomes a stub of the port its handler takes since F1 and F2. Gate: unit tests</td></tr></tbody></table><p>H-A1 does not edit <code>live.ts</code>, which is H-A2&#39;s; H-B2 wires H-A1&#39;s facts module. H-A3 edits no source and no existing file under <code>testing/</code>.</p><p>Wave H-C, after H-B3:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Items</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H-C1 world and entity side</td><td>33, 34 (<code>getActors</code>), 36, 42 for its files</td><td><code>world/World.ts</code>, <code>world/WorldActor.ts</code>, <code>world/Level.ts</code>, <code>world/end/DragonFight.ts</code>, <code>world/providers/</code>, <code>entity/data/entityIndex.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/live.ts</code>, <code>entity/Explosion.ts</code>, <code>entity/Combat.ts</code>, <code>entity/Archery.ts</code>, <code>entity/EntitySerializer.ts</code>, <code>entity/worldContentAdapter.ts</code>, <code>command/CommandExecutor.ts</code>, <code>utils/ParseTargetSelector.ts</code>, <code>utils/ParseTildeCaretNotation.ts</code>, <code>events/entity/</code>, <code>advancement/triggers.ts</code>, <code>content/world/serverSource.ts</code>. Gate: unit tests, the replay suite, every parity family, client e2e <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>summon-entity</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code>, <code>natural-spawn-placement</code></td></tr><tr><td>H-C2 sessions, services and the plugin API</td><td>5 (teardown), 29, the preparation for 4, 42 for its files</td><td><code>network/PlayerSession.ts</code>, <code>network/ClientConnection.ts</code>, <code>network/BossBar.ts</code>, <code>network/NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code>, <code>network/startGameData.ts</code>, <code>network/spawnPosition.ts</code>, <code>network/sessionPlayers.ts</code>, <code>SessionManager.ts</code>, <code>keyedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>events/player/</code> (11 files), <code>command/</code> except <code>CommandExecutor.ts</code>, <code>permission/</code>, <code>ban/</code>, <code>console/</code>, <code>advancement/progress.ts</code>, <code>advancement/raise.ts</code>, <code>enchantment/</code>, <code>item/</code>, <code>testing/playerSession.test.helper.ts</code>, <code>testing/blockTarget.test.helper.ts</code>, <code>testing/namedBlockTarget.test.helper.ts</code>. Gate: unit tests, parity <code>protocol.dimension-change</code>, client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>duplicate-login</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>title</code></td></tr><tr><td>H-C3 handlers</td><td>42 for its files</td><td>Every source file under <code>network/handler/</code>: the 33 that call <code>session.getPlayer()</code> and the modules beside them, such as <code>workstation.ts</code> and <code>tradeScreen.ts</code>. Each handler reads state from the store player and calls the homes H-B3 named. Gate: unit tests, client e2e <code>movement</code>, <code>jump</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>death-respawn</code></td></tr><tr><td>H-C4 copies, benchmarks and the proxy test</td><td>34 (<code>all()</code>), 43</td><td><code>entity/data/EntityRuntime.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>, new <code>entity/data/noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code>, <code>packages/prismarine/benchmarks/</code> (8 files), <code>packages/parity/src/protocol.dimension-change.test.ts</code>, the docs pages H-D updates. Gate: unit tests, the replay suite with unchanged digests (question 9)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>H-C1 moves the <code>World.getPlayers()</code> callers it owns to <code>getViewers()</code>, and H-D deletes <code>getPlayers</code>. Only H-C2 edits <code>PlayerSession.ts</code>; a package that needs a new session member waits for H-D. H-C4 is the smallest, so it also takes the prose of item 42 in files no other package owns.</p><p><strong>4. The test conversion.</strong> 126 files name a legacy class, build a legacy player or use a helper that does: 112 tests, 5 helpers under <code>testing/</code>, 8 benchmarks and 1 parity test. 117 of them import a legacy module. By use, and a file can have several: 66 stub <code>Player</code> (<code>stub&lt;Player&gt;</code>, <code>StubMembers&lt;Player&gt;</code>), 25 stub <code>Entity</code>, 20 call <code>fakePlayer()</code>, 15 build or subclass <code>Entity</code>, <code>GenericEntity</code> or <code>Human</code>, 8 use <code>ItemActorBody</code>, 7 call <code>new Player</code>, 6 build from <code>Player.prototype</code>, 6 test <code>instanceof</code>, and 7 only import a type.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th style="text-align:right;">Files</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H-A2</td><td style="text-align:right;">12: <code>EntitySerializer</code>, <code>Explosion</code>, <code>live</code>, <code>blockBreak</code>, <code>worldDestruction</code>, <code>WorldDrops</code>, <code>WorldPersistence</code>, <code>WorldEntityIndex</code>, <code>WorldAutoSave</code>, <code>WorldReplayState</code>, <code>LevelDB</code>, and <code>benchmarks/entityOnlyChunkSave.ts</code></td><td>wave A</td></tr><tr><td>H-A3</td><td style="text-align:right;">29: every <code>network/handler/*.test.ts</code></td><td>wave A</td></tr><tr><td>H-B2</td><td style="text-align:right;">14: <code>testing/fakePlayer.test.helper.ts</code>, <code>testing/playerDouble.test.helper.ts</code> (deleted), <code>Player</code>, <code>PlayerChangeLog</code>, <code>FallDamage</code>, <code>PlayerPersistence</code>, <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat</code>, <code>SpawnPoint</code>, <code>WorldChangeTick</code>, <code>DimensionTravel</code>, <code>inventory/blockContainer</code>, <code>item/actorPort</code>, <code>item/interaction</code>, <code>enchantment/enchantingTable</code>, and the <code>Player.prototype</code> doubles left in <code>live</code> and <code>blockBreak</code></td><td>H-B2</td></tr><tr><td>H-C1</td><td style="text-align:right;">27: <code>advancement/triggers</code>, <code>chat/ConsoleText</code>, <code>entity/Armor</code>, <code>Combat</code>, <code>Damage.pipeline</code>, <code>Entity</code>, <code>damageTypes</code>, <code>data/actorMetadata</code>, <code>data/storePlayers</code>, <code>worldContentAdapter</code>, <code>utils/ParseTargetSelector</code>, <code>ParseTildeCaretNotation</code>, and in <code>world/</code> <code>Level</code>, <code>RespawnSearch</code>, <code>WorldEntityAddition</code>, <code>WorldGeneratedEntities</code>, <code>WorldTickPhases</code>, <code>EngineWorld</code>, <code>PlayerRecords</code>, <code>WorldBreakBlock</code>, <code>WorldHitBlock</code>, <code>WorldPlayerChunkCounts</code>, <code>WorldPortalIndex</code>, <code>WorldViewers</code>, <code>cooking</code>, <code>spawners</code>, <code>end/DragonFight</code></td><td>wave C</td></tr><tr><td>H-C2</td><td style="text-align:right;">36: <code>SessionManager</code>, <code>advancement/progress</code>, <code>command/CommandManager</code>, <code>commandStartup</code>, <code>host/host</code>, <code>host/support</code>, <code>jsprismarineCommands</code>, <code>console/RemoteConsole</code>, <code>events/player/PlayerEvent</code>, <code>permission/PermissionManager</code>, the three <code>testing/</code> helpers it owns, and 23 in <code>network/</code>: <code>BossBar</code>, <code>ClientConnection</code>, <code>MobEquipment</code>, <code>MovementBatching</code>, <code>NetworkWorldReplicatorEntities</code>, the 14 <code>PlayerSession*</code> files, <code>packet/AvailableCommandsPacket</code>, <code>sessionPlayers</code>, <code>spawnPosition</code>, <code>startGameData</code></td><td>wave C</td></tr><tr><td>H-C4</td><td style="text-align:right;">8: 7 benchmarks and <code>protocol.dimension-change</code></td><td>wave C</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The helpers that have to exist first:</p><ul><li>H-B2 rebuilds <code>fakePlayer()</code> over a store player in a real <code>EntityRuntime</code> (from <code>storePlayerFixture</code>) and keeps its return shape, so its 20 callers change only where they reach legacy-only members. It is the one helper every later group leans on.</li><li>H-B2 deletes <code>withPlayerComponents</code>. Its 6 callers build a store player instead.</li><li>H-A2 replaces <code>new GenericEntity</code> and <code>extends Entity</code> stand-ins with store entities from <code>liveTestEntities</code> (<code>testing/entityServer.ts</code>), and adds <code>testing/storeActor.test.helper.ts</code> only if that is not enough.</li><li>H-C2 moves <code>sessionFor</code>, <code>sessionPlayerFields</code> and <code>sessionsWith</code> to <code>SessionPlayers&lt;StorePlayer&gt;</code>, and H-D drops their <code>Player</code> defaults.</li><li><code>Entity.test.ts</code> and the <code>LegacyPlayers</code> block of <code>sessionPlayers.test.ts</code> are deleted; <code>Entity.test.ts</code>&#39;s id overflow assertion moves to <code>entityIds.ts</code>&#39;s test in H-S0. <code>Damage.pipeline.test.ts</code>, <code>PlayerChangeLog.test.ts</code> and <code>FallDamage.test.ts</code> test behavior a player still has, so their assertions move onto a store player and each file goes only when every assertion has a home.</li></ul><p><strong>5. What C4h does not do.</strong></p><ul><li>Slice 6 (D1, D2): the handler shape and narrow context, <code>Server</code> out of handlers, synchronous input-lane handlers, the eight handlers that load chunks or search, <code>RespawnHandler</code>&#39;s search, and building the player before the join. H-C3 changes only what a handler reads the player through.</li><li>Slice 7 (D3, D4): replication from the log. <code>StoreActors.sync</code>&#39;s comparisons, the per-tick equipment comparison, the breath and motion sends, a stack network id after an undo, the status effect clock and actor record tags, block and block entity logging, and merchant offers.</li><li>Slice 8 (E1): saves at a tick boundary.</li><li>Slice 9 (E2 to E4): <code>DimensionTravel</code> as messages, worlds built outside ticks, the respawn search&#39;s and the dragon fight&#39;s in-tick chunk loads, and <code>TickContext</code>&#39;s <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code>. C4h keeps <code>DimensionTravel</code>&#39;s awaited transfer and removes only its class checks.</li><li>Slice 10: one <code>chat</code> listener and the event bus. Slice 11: explicit exports; H-D removes only the deleted modules&#39; exports.</li><li>Bugs found earlier stay their own: #2961, #2968, #2969. #2965 closes with item 25.</li><li>H-A2 and H-C4 depend on no player work and can be named packages of their own if the program wants them out of C4h&#39;s commitment. Slice 5&#39;s target needs both.</li></ul><p><strong>6. Open questions.</strong></p><ol><li>Where does a store player live before it spawns and between two worlds? Recommendation: <code>StorePlayer</code> outlives its entity. It is filed by an id drawn at the login from H-S0&#39;s allocator, holds the record&#39;s position, look and health while detached, and attaches an entity through <code>EntityRuntime.restore(record, at, id)</code> at <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> and on arrival. This matches today, where a joining player is in no world&#39;s entity map and no mob can target it, and it is ADR 0011&#39;s leave and enter. The other choice, an entity spawned at the login and hidden, needs every query to skip it.</li><li>What replaces <code>Player</code> for callers and plugins? Recommendation: <code>session.getPlayer()</code> returns the <code>StorePlayer</code>, operations live where H-B3 puts them, and the ten player events drop <code>getPlayer()</code> for the <code>getRuntimeId()</code> and <code>getIdentity()</code> F4 added plus a new <code>getSession()</code>. The root <code>Player</code> export goes. Prismarine is below 1.0.0, so this is a <code>minor</code> Changeset.</li><li>Does C4h fold <code>ItemActorBody</code> into <code>StoreActor</code>? Recommendation: yes, as H-A2. It extends <code>Entity</code>, so slice 5&#39;s target cannot pass while it exists. Two of C3b&#39;s four paths already have a store home: <code>StoreActor.update</code> runs the portal clock, and <code>transferStoreEntity</code> moves a store entity between stores.</li><li>What happens to a saved actor whose type no pack declares? Today it loads as a <code>GenericEntity</code> and is shown with its identifier. Without the Bedrock pack every saved mob is such an actor. Recommendation: an inert built-in store definition, as H-C2 added for items, that keeps the identifier, the record and <code>persistentNbt</code>, shows the actor and runs nothing. Dropping the actors or keeping their NBT unseen would make a world loaded without the pack look empty.</li><li>Does the switch split into H-B1 and H-B2? Recommendation: yes. H-B1 changes only types and holds the digests, so H-B2&#39;s diff is behavior alone and its digest change has one cause.</li><li>Where does the game mode live? Recommendation: a logged component field, which <code>SurvivalPlayer.vulnerable</code> reads and the store undoes, replacing the adapter&#39;s <code>gamemode</code> field. <code>StorePlayer.gamemode</code> becomes an accessor over it.</li><li>Which ground state stays? Recommendation: the body&#39;s <code>on_ground</code> condition, which <code>applyClientMovement</code> writes. Delete <code>movement.onGround</code>; the digest change lands in H-B2&#39;s re-baseline.</li><li>Which clock does a meal count on? Recommendation: the store&#39;s tick, which the change log and the entity pass already count by. The level clock moves backwards under <code>/time set</code>.</li><li>How does the entity pass walk the store without a copy? Recommendation: iterate the store&#39;s map in insertion order and skip an entity added after the pass began, by a sequence number the store stamps on insertion. The copy left out the same entities, so the digests do not change.</li><li>What replaces <code>World.getActors</code>&#39; sorted copy? Recommendation: the content adapter&#39;s <code>at</code>, <code>near</code> and <code>countOfType</code> query the index, which runs inside the tick. Selectors and the save context iterate the store and sort only what they return, and the digest sorts, as it runs outside the tick.</li></ol><p>H-S0, the seams, left these for the later steps. Items 3, 7, 17, 19, 20 (world side), 23 and 32 are done, and so is the entry point of item 6.</p><ul><li>One id pool: <code>entityIds</code> in <code>entity/entityIds.ts</code>, an <code>EntityIdAllocator</code>. The legacy constructor and <code>LiveEntityRuntime</code>&#39;s <code>nextId</code> draw from it, and <code>Entity.runtimeIdCount</code> is gone. The overflow guard the legacy constructor had now covers the store&#39;s ids too. <code>Entity.test.ts</code>&#39;s overflow assertion moved to <code>entityIds.test.ts</code>.</li><li><code>EYE_HEIGHT</code> and <code>eyePositionAt</code> live in <code>entity/playerEyes.ts</code>. The portal cooldown clamp had a third copy, in <code>StoreActor</code>; <code>portalCooldownOf</code> in <code>PortalContact.ts</code> is the one home.</li><li>A player enters a world through <code>World.addPlayer</code> and leaves through <code>World.removePlayer</code>. <code>addPlayer</code> files the player in the player map and then calls <code>addEntity</code>, which no longer files anything there; <code>removePlayer</code> calls <code>removeEntity</code>, which still deletes the id from the player map, so the content host&#39;s <code>remove</code> behaves as before. <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitializedHandler</code>, <code>PlayerSession.changeWorld</code> and <code>Player.disable</code> call them. H-B2 points both at the store.</li><li>Two rules replaced the class tests. Where the code needs the player&#39;s state, it finds the session by entity id with <code>World.viewerOf</code> and reads <code>session.getPlayer()</code>: <code>DimensionTravel</code>&#39;s mid-change check, transfer and End exit, the command host&#39;s <code>playerSessionOf</code>, the item actor port, and the live host&#39;s trade screen, filter view and swing relay. Where it needs only to know that an entity is a player, it tests the type against <code>PLAYER_IDENTIFIER</code>: the PvP rule, nether portal creation, the End arrival height, and <code>World</code>&#39;s tick exemption, attribute flush and chunk membership. The replay digest reads the player from the world&#39;s player map by id. The live host&#39;s filter view reads a structural <code>FilteredPlayer</code>.</li><li>The store side of items 19 and 20: a store entity of type <code>minecraft:player</code> ticks outside the ticking chunks, stays in the store when its chunk unloads, and does not make <code>markEntitiesInChunkChanged</code> mark the chunk. No live player is a store entity yet, so nothing changed at run time.</li><li>Not closed: the entity index&#39;s <code>chunkChanged</code> callback (<code>World.storeEntityChunkChanged</code>, wired in <code>live.ts</code>) marks a chunk changed when any store entity crosses into it, a store player included. A changed chunk is saved before it can unload, and the save skips players, so the cost is an extra save. It gets only the chunk coordinates, so the fix belongs beside the index: H-B2, which spawns store players and tests that they stay out of chunk saves.</li><li><code>viewerOf</code> answers only for a player in the world&#39;s player map, which the plan named as the risk. Every production caller reaches a player through that world&#39;s portal pass, entity update, entity lookup or tick, so each one is in the map. The test doubles that built a player outside it now put it there or answer <code>viewerOf</code>: <code>DimensionTravel</code>, <code>WorldTickPhases</code>, <code>item/interaction</code>, <code>item/actorPort</code>, <code>world/blockBreak</code>, <code>advancement/triggers</code>, <code>command/host/host</code>, <code>CommandManager</code>, the trade double in <code>live</code>, <code>fakePlayer</code>, the parity family <code>protocol.dimension-change</code>, and the tests that called <code>addEntity</code> with a player. <code>WorldViewers.test.ts</code> checks that a player added through <code>addPlayer</code> is found before <code>entityAdded</code> runs and not after <code>removePlayer</code>.</li><li>One edge case remains, as with F3&#39;s lookups: if <code>Player.disable</code> throws before <code>removePlayer</code>, <code>ClientConnection</code> still runs <code>PlayerSession.disable</code>. The player then stays in its world&#39;s map with a forgotten session, and <code>session.getPlayer()</code> throws where <code>instanceof Player</code> passed.</li><li>What still names the class. As a value: <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>Entities.ts</code>, the root export in <code>index.ts</code> (H-D) and <code>LoginHandler.ts</code>, which builds the player (H-B2). As a type only: the session directory (<code>sessionPlayers.ts</code>, <code>createConnectedPlayer.ts</code>, <code>PlayerSession.ts</code>, <code>SessionManager.ts</code>), the eleven <code>events/player</code> classes (H-C2), <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code>, <code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code> and <code>workstation.ts</code> (H-C3), and <code>World.ts</code>&#39;s player map, <code>getPlayers</code>, <code>breakBlock</code> and <code>addPlayer</code> (H-C1, H-D). Nothing outside tests uses <code>instanceof Player</code> or <code>instanceof Human</code>.</li><li>Copies found on the way, for item 42&#39;s packages: the literal <code>&#39;minecraft:player&#39;</code> is repeated in fourteen source files beside <code>PLAYER_IDENTIFIER</code> (among them <code>DeathMessages.ts</code>, <code>worldContentAdapter.ts</code>, <code>mobLoot.ts</code>, <code>advancement/triggers.ts</code>, <code>command/host/support.ts</code>, <code>goalWorld.ts</code>, <code>item/interaction.ts</code>, <code>ParseTargetSelector.ts</code> and <code>ChunkSaveContext.ts</code>), and <code>Explosion.ts</code> and <code>entity/data/explosion.ts</code> each define an eye height fraction of 0.85 that <code>ENTITY_EYE_HEIGHT_FRACTION</code> in <code>entity/data/position.ts</code> already holds.</li><li>Checks at program head <code>d2c8ec355f</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code>, the replay suite unchanged, the whole parity suite, and client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>attack-entity</code> and <code>item-drop-pickup</code> pass.</li></ul><p>H-A1, the store player, left these for H-B2. Items 2, 8, 9, 10, 12 (the facts module), 14 (the system calls), 15 (the projection), 20 (the store pass) and 21 (the hook) are done. Item 11 is done for the store player and leaves one field for H-B2.</p><ul><li>A <code>StorePlayer</code> outlives its entity (question 1). It is built from an id and the identity, <code>new StorePlayer(id, identity)</code>, and holds its position, look and health while it is in no store, health in its attribute table&#39;s entry. <code>enterStore(runtime, players, player)</code> files it and gives it an entity under its id: <code>EntityRuntime.spawnPlayer</code> the first time, and <code>EntityRuntime.restore(record, at, id, prepare)</code> from the record its last entity left with after that. The player attaches in the prepare step, before the entity enters, so what it held is part of the entering entry and the load appends nothing else. <code>leaveStore(runtime, player, reason)</code> keeps the record, the tags and the status effects and removes the entity, and the player stays filed. <code>StorePlayers.delete</code> is still the server leave. <code>spawnStorePlayer</code> builds, fills and enters in one call, drawing the id from <code>EntityRuntime.nextEntityId</code>; H-B2&#39;s login draws it from <code>entityIds</code> instead and enters at <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>.</li><li><code>StorePlayerActor</code> takes the entity beside the player, and <code>StorePlayers.actorOf</code> builds one only for the entity the player is attached to.</li><li>Logging (items 8 and 9). The player owns one <code>PlayerComponents</code>, one <code>HumanInventory</code> and now a <code>CraftingInput</code> from the login on. <code>PlayerSystems.attach(entity, parts)</code> takes the components and two writers, and returns the <code>PlayerLog</code> the player writes through while attached: component fields and <code>inventory.handSlot</code> through <code>EntityStore.written</code>, and slots through <code>EntityStore.writtenSlot</code> once the entity has entered. <code>EntityRuntime.writeInverse</code> undoes an <code>inventory-slot</code> entry and <code>inventory.handSlot</code> through the player&#39;s writers, which is what <code>Human.restoreLoggedSlot</code> and <code>restoreLoggedField</code> do for a legacy player. <code>StorePlayer.restoreSlot</code> repeats <code>Human.restoreLoggedSlot</code>&#39;s body, because <code>Human.ts</code> was outside this package; the copy goes when H-B2 deletes <code>Human.ts</code>.</li><li>Game mode (item 10, question 6) is the component field <code>abilities.gamemode</code>, and <code>StorePlayer.gamemode</code> is an accessor over it. A fresh player&#39;s components start in survival, where the old field started at <code>WORLD_DEFAULT</code>, so a raw <code>minecraft:player</code> spawned in a test still starves. <code>survivalReaches</code> (<code>survivalPlayer.ts</code>) and <code>mayFlyIn</code> (<code>playerComponents.ts</code>) are the rules&#39; homes; <code>playerRecord.ts</code> reads <code>mayFlyIn</code>. <code>Player</code> keeps its own copies of both and its <code>gamemode</code> field, which the legacy adapter logs as <code>gamemode</code>. The legacy player never writes <code>abilities.gamemode</code>, so its <code>componentSnapshot</code> gained one constant entry and the absolute digest of a world with a player changed. The replay suite compares two replays and passes unchanged.</li><li>Ground (item 11, question 7). A store player&#39;s ground is its body&#39;s <code>on_ground</code> condition, which <code>StorePlayer.isOnGround</code> reads, and nothing reads or writes <code>movement.onGround</code> for one. Deleting the field needs <code>Player.ts</code>, which reads it, so H-B2 deletes it, with the digest change in its re-baseline.</li><li>The meal (item 12, question 8) counts on <code>EntityStore.tick</code>, the change log&#39;s tick, which the systems reach through the <code>tick</code> port. <code>storePlayerHostFacts(players, finish)</code> (<code>entity/data/playerHostFacts.ts</code>) answers <code>heldItemOf</code> and <code>finishMeal</code> from <code>StorePlayers</code>. Finishing is handed in, because <code>consumeHeldItem</code> takes an <code>InteractingPlayer</code>, which a store player does not satisfy; H-B2 supplies it when it spreads the facts into the live host.</li><li>The system calls (item 14): <code>PlayerSystems.setSprinting</code>, <code>setSneaking</code> and <code>jumped</code>, beside the existing <code>setMining</code>, <code>beginMeal</code> and <code>cancelMeal</code>; <code>EntityRuntime.teleportPlayer</code>, the server&#39;s move, which teleports the body and ends the fall; and <code>moveStorePlayer</code>, which calls <code>applyClientMovement</code> and writes the head yaw. Nothing calls them yet: H-B2 routes the handlers&#39; <code>Player</code> methods to them.</li><li>Metadata (item 15). <code>StorePlayerActor.showPlayerState</code> writes air, breathing, sprinting and can-fly from the components into the actor&#39;s metadata and answers whether a send is due, with the legacy throttle, now <code>breathWorthSending</code> in <code>playerBreath.ts</code>. The constructor calls it, so the first <code>AddPlayer</code> carries them. <code>StoreActors.sync</code> does not call it, because <code>storeActors.ts</code> is H-A2&#39;s. H-B2 calls it after the component projection, which writes its own can-fly flag, and sends the metadata when it answers true. <code>Player.syncBreath</code> keeps its copy of the throttle until the class goes.</li><li>The store pass (item 20) ticks a <code>minecraft:player</code> wherever it stands, as the world&#39;s loops do.</li><li>The hook (item 21) is <code>EntityRuntime.onPlayerFailed</code>, a settable field like <code>rides.onChange</code>, because the fixture in <code>entity/data/testing.ts</code> passes no new options and that file was outside this package. After <code>ENTITY_FAILURE_LIMIT</code> failures in a row the runtime calls it in place of removing a player. It fires again on every later failure while the player stays in the store, so H-B2&#39;s disconnect has to tolerate a second call. Left unset, the player is removed as a mob is.</li><li>What is not done: no live code builds, enters or ticks a store player, and <code>live.ts</code> is untouched. H-B2 wires <code>onPlayerFailed</code>, <code>storePlayerHostFacts</code>, <code>showPlayerState</code>, the system calls and the login, and deletes <code>movement.onGround</code>, <code>Player.syncBreath</code>&#39;s throttle and the copies of the game mode rules.</li><li>Checks at H-A1&#39;s head: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (428 files), <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> byte for byte, the whole parity suite with <code>entities.behavior</code> (<code>player-health</code>, <code>player-survival</code>), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass.</li></ul><p>H-A2, the bodies that are not players, left these for the later steps. Items 37, 38 and 39 are done, and so are questions 3 and 4.</p><ul><li>A dropped stack (item 37) has an <code>ItemActor</code> (<code>entity/data/itemActor.ts</code>), a <code>StoreActor</code> that <code>StoreActors</code> builds through a new <code>builtInActor</code> option beside <code>playerActor</code>. It keeps what its legacy body sent: an <code>AddItemActor</code> from <code>spawnTo</code>, a tracking range of six chunks, never on the ground, no metadata (<code>StoreActor.projectMetadata</code> answers an empty projection) and the default attributes, never sent (<code>StoreActor.showHealth</code> does nothing for it). A blow goes to <code>DroppedItems.hurt</code>, and the <code>entityDamage</code> hook and <code>damageCommitted</code> skip an item actor, because a stack&#39;s body raised no plugin event or trigger.</li><li>Its portal clock runs in <code>StoreActor.update</code>. <code>DimensionTravel.transferStoreEntity</code> moves it with <code>LiveEntityRuntime.adoptItem(actor, at, cooldown)</code>, which spawns the stack in the destination from <code>savedItem</code>, as the legacy transfer did, rather than from the data entity record, so the motion carries over and the stack&#39;s saved NBT gains no record. <code>persist</code> and <code>savedRecords</code> skip item and inert actors for the same reason. A stack leaves an unloading chunk with the chunk&#39;s other store entities. <code>ItemBodies</code>, <code>LiveEntityRuntime.unload</code> and <code>removeBody</code> are gone, and so is <code>World.addEntity</code>&#39;s adoption of a body.</li><li><code>World.ITEM_ENTITY_LIMIT</code> (item 39) counts <code>StoreActors.droppedItemCount</code>, kept as actors are added and removed.</li><li><code>EntitySerializer.fromNBT</code> (item 38) reads a plain <code>StoredActor</code> and builds no entity. <code>World.restoreDataEntity</code> takes it to the item path, to the record or <code>restoreExisting</code>, and then to <code>LiveEntityRuntime.restoreInert</code> (question 4). The inert type is the entity overlay&#39;s <code>jsprismarine:inert_actor</code>, which has health, the twenty a legacy body defaulted to, and nothing else. Its actor is an <code>InertActor</code> (<code>storeActors.ts</code>) that reports the saved identifier through <code>getType</code> and <code>getBox</code>; the live runtime files the identifier by entity id in the spawn&#39;s prepare step, before the actor is built. It keeps the actor record tags, a record of an undeclared type among them, the tags, the status effects, the portal cooldown and a TNT&#39;s fuse, and a save writes it back unchanged. Through a portal it is restored inert in the destination; its health does not carry over, where a legacy <code>GenericEntity</code> kept its own.</li><li>The level snapshot builds a <code>StoredActor</code> and skips <code>minecraft:player</code>, which <code>Level.entitySnapshots</code> never writes. A snapshot of a dropped stack, which has no stack, no longer becomes a body in the world.</li><li>The player body is the one subclass of <code>Entity</code> in the server&#39;s source, which <code>noMirror.test.ts</code> now checks. <code>GenericEntity</code> moved to <code>testing/genericEntity.test.helper.ts</code> for the tests that still hand a legacy mob to code that takes an <code>Entity</code>: <code>Combat</code>, <code>ParseTargetSelector</code>, <code>WorldChangeTick</code>, <code>DimensionTravel</code>&#39;s legacy mobs, <code>WorldEntityIndex</code>&#39;s legacy-half tests, and, of H-A2&#39;s own group, <code>Explosion</code>, <code>WorldAutoSave</code>, <code>WorldReplayState</code> and <code>benchmarks/entityOnlyChunkSave.ts</code>. Each goes when H-C1 or H-B2 narrows the code it tests. <code>Entities.writtenEntities</code> is gone; the prose of <code>SpawnableEntityClass</code> in <code>Entity.ts</code> still names it, for H-D.</li><li>Outside the file list, for the inert type and the test conversion: the overlay file, <code>content/testing.ts</code>&#39;s built-in list, <code>content/packStack.test.ts</code>, which allows the one non-<code>minecraft:</code> type, the prose of <code>content/README.md</code> and <code>docs/data-driven/entities.md</code>, <code>inventory/blockContainer.test.ts</code>, <code>world/WorldBreakBlock.test.ts</code>, and two helpers, <code>testing/savedActor.test.helper.ts</code> and <code>testing/storeWorld.test.helper.ts</code>.</li><li>No digest changed. An item actor hashes the legacy body&#39;s attributes, and <code>World.getActors</code> sorts by runtime id, so the stacks hash in the same order. The replay suite needed no re-baseline.</li><li>Found: <code>/summon</code> ignores <code>is_summonable</code>, so it also spawns <code>jsprismarine:inert_actor</code>, as an invisible entity (#2975), and a saved actor without a record comes back with its health capped at twenty, which <code>restoreExisting</code> keeps from the legacy body (#2976).</li><li>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass: a stack thrown into a nether portal arrives in the Nether with its count and can be picked up there; and a world saved with mobs, loaded by a server started without the Bedrock pack, shows each mob where it stood, standing still, and after a restart with the pack the mobs come back alive.</li><li>Checks at H-A2&#39;s head, on program head <code>13f30f1407</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (429 files), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, the whole parity suite with <code>mapping.entities</code>, <code>entities.addactor</code> and <code>blocks.portals</code>, the entities package, client e2e <code>item-drop-pickup</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>dimensions</code> and <code>natural-spawn-placement</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass.</li></ul><p>H-B1, the player body, left these for H-B2. No behavior changed and no digest moved.</p><ul><li>The shape. <code>Player</code> no longer extends <code>Human</code>. It builds a <code>LegacyPlayerBody</code> (<code>entity/LegacyPlayerBody.ts</code>) in its constructor and returns it from <code>Player.getBody()</code>. The body is a <code>Human</code> that implements <code>ShownPlayer</code> and holds <code>public readonly player</code>, as <code>StorePlayerActor</code> does, and answers the profile members (<code>getUUID</code>, <code>xuid</code>, <code>skin</code>, <code>device</code>, <code>gamemode</code>, <code>isOp</code>, <code>isFlying</code>, <code>getName</code>, <code>getFormattedUsername</code>) from the player. H-B2&#39;s swap is <code>Player.body</code> and <code>getBody()</code>&#39;s return type, from the body to the spawned store player&#39;s actor and its <code>StorePlayer</code>.</li><li>What holds the body: the world&#39;s entity map (<code>World.addPlayer</code> and <code>removePlayer</code> pass it to <code>addEntity</code> and <code>removeEntity</code>), the entity index, the chunk membership writes, the replicator, the change log&#39;s <code>legacyStoreOf</code> and the legacy inverse writer. The world&#39;s player map still holds the <code>Player</code>, because <code>getPlayers</code> and <code>viewerOf</code> hand out players and sessions; H-D deletes <code>getPlayers</code>.</li><li>What the body keeps: the entity state, the inventory and crafting grid, and the <code>PlayerComponents</code> with the survival view (<code>getComponents</code>, <code>getSurvival</code>). <code>Player</code> reads them through two private accessors, <code>components</code> and <code>survival</code>, which H-B2 points at the <code>StorePlayer</code>. The body&#39;s overrides are the player&#39;s entity rules: the damage gate, knockback as an impulse (<code>Player.applyImpulse</code>), the hunger effects, the logged attributes, component undo, the portal delays and the held cooldown, the tracking range and interval, <code>spawnTo</code> and <code>isOnGround</code>. Each has a store counterpart already, named in items 9, 14, 15 and 22, except <code>processPortalCooldown</code>&#39;s hold during a dimension change, which <code>StoreActor</code>&#39;s portal clock does not have yet.</li><li>The tick and the death go back to the player. The world ticks the body; <code>LegacyPlayerBody.update</code> runs <code>Entity.update</code> and then <code>Player.updatePlayer</code>, the session, vitals, meal, void, mining, attribute sync and trigger part. <code>Player.update</code> runs the body&#39;s. The body&#39;s <code>onDeath</code> calls <code>Player.die(source, dieAsEntity)</code>, which runs <code>Entity.onDeath</code> at the point the old <code>super.onDeath</code> call stood. Once the store pass ticks the player, something has to call <code>updatePlayer</code>, or its parts move to <code>PlayerSystems</code> and the session, as H-B3 plans.</li><li><code>Player</code> delegates what its callers read: the id, type, position, world, server, box, inventory, crafting grid, health, <code>setHealth</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>heal</code>, feet and eye height, equipment, tags, status effects, portal cooldown, and <code>metadata</code>, <code>attributes</code>, <code>pitch</code>, <code>yaw</code> and <code>headYaw</code> as accessors. It does not implement <code>spawnTo</code> or <code>getTrackingRange</code>, so it is no <code>ShownActor</code>, and the compiler finds every show. These delegates are the second half of H-B2&#39;s swap.</li><li>Where source passes the body: a swing (<code>meleeAttack</code>) and an arrow (<code>loose</code>) in <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code>, the move broadcasts and resets in <code>MovePlayerHandler</code>, <code>ActorEventHandler</code>&#39;s relay, <code>RequestPermissionsHandler</code>&#39;s abilities, the death message and animation, the change log writes in <code>Player</code>, the content adapter&#39;s <code>players</code>, the command host&#39;s <code>@a</code>, <code>@p</code> and <code>@r</code> candidates, and every show in <code>Player</code> (moves, abilities, game type, status effects). The session shows its own player through <code>PlayerSession.shown()</code>, which reads <code>getPlayer().getBody()</code>; <code>OwnPlayer</code> lost its <code>ShownPlayer</code> half. <code>item/actorPort.ts</code> hands <code>interactWithEntity</code> the entity the world answers for the source rather than the player. Of these, <code>addEntity</code>, <code>removeEntity</code>, <code>meleeAttack</code>, <code>loose</code> and the <code>logLegacy*</code> writers take a nominal <code>Entity</code> and need retyping in H-B2; the rest take ports <code>StorePlayerActor</code> satisfies.</li><li>The identity checks. Every comparison entity code makes now compares bodies, because the world answers the body for the player&#39;s id and every caller above passes it: <code>legacyStoreOf</code>&#39;s <code>world.getEntity(id) !== entity</code>, <code>canHarm</code>&#39;s <code>attacker === target</code>, <code>deathMessage</code>&#39;s <code>killer !== victim</code>, <code>World.entityRelocated</code>&#39;s map check and <code>EntityIndex</code>&#39;s. Two comparisons stay between players, because both sides come from the session manager or <code>getPlayers</code>: <code>Player.sendSettings</code>&#39; <code>target !== this</code> and the shared-container check in <code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code>. <code>entity/LegacyPlayerBody.test.ts</code> covers the world&#39;s answer, a swing at the player&#39;s own id and a self-inflicted death; <code>WorldViewers.test.ts</code> and <code>PlayerChangeLog.test.ts</code> now assert the body where they asserted the player, and the change log test&#39;s world double answered the player, which the identity check would have refused.</li><li>Unchecked narrowing. <code>advancement/triggers.ts</code> narrowed an actor of type <code>minecraft:player</code> to <code>AdvancementPlayer</code> with a type guard, which a body would not satisfy. It now checks the type and whether anything listens, then finds the player through <code>World.viewerOf</code> by entity id, which also serves a store player. <code>ParseTargetSelector</code>&#39;s guard reads only <code>gamemode</code>, and the inverse writer&#39;s <code>&#39;gamemode&#39; in entity</code> writes through the body&#39;s <code>gamemode</code> setter.</li><li>The plugin surface. <code>entityDamage</code> and <code>entityDeath</code> carry the body for a player; the player events still carry the <code>Player</code>. The Changeset (<code>player-holds-a-body.md</code>, <code>minor</code>) says so and lists what moved to the body.</li><li>Test doubles. <code>playerOnPrototype(members, body)</code> in <code>testing/playerDouble.test.helper.ts</code> builds a prototype player with a prototype body, puts <code>metadata</code>, <code>attributes</code> and the rotation on the body, and <code>withPlayerComponents</code> now writes the body&#39;s components. 16 test files call it; H-B2 deletes the file and its callers build store players. <code>stubPlayerPort</code>&#39;s doubles answer <code>getBody</code> with a plain object of the same members. A test that hands a player to entity code passes <code>getBody()</code>, and a double the world holds is a <code>stubOf(LegacyPlayerBody, ...)</code>.</li><li>Checks at H-B1&#39;s head, on program head <code>baea11e568</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (430 files), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, the whole parity suite (53 files), all 43 client e2e scenarios in fifteen batches with none failing, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass. No adjacent bug was confirmed.</li></ul><p>H-B2, the body is the store entity, left these for H-B3. Items 1, 5 (store side), 6, 12, 14 (through <code>Player</code>&#39;s methods), 21, 22, 24, 25, 26 and 35 are done, and so is the regression test item 18 asked for.</p><ul><li>The shape. A <code>Player</code> holds a <code>StorePlayer</code> from its constructor on, under an id from <code>entityIds</code>, and keeps no entity state. It keeps three references the store player does not: the world it is in or about to enter, the server, and a <code>DetachedPlayerView</code>. <code>World.addPlayer</code> files the player in the world&#39;s player map and calls <code>enterStore</code> with <code>Server.getStorePlayers()</code> (<code>EntityRuntimes.players</code>). <code>World.removePlayer</code> takes it out of the map first and then calls <code>leaveStore</code>. <code>Player.disable</code> calls <code>StorePlayers.delete</code>. A dimension change and a respawn into another dimension are a leave and an enter with the same id. <code>LegacyPlayerBody</code> is deleted.</li><li>Two accessors replace the body. <code>Player.getActor()</code> returns the <code>StorePlayerActor</code> while the entity is in the store of the player&#39;s world, and undefined before the spawn and between two worlds. Entity code takes the actor: a swing, an arrow, the content adapter&#39;s players, the command selectors and <code>ActorEventHandler</code>. <code>Player.getBody()</code> returns the actor or the detached view, which is a <code>ShownPlayer</code> over the store player that the session shows its own player through before the spawn. Question 1 holds: no world holds a player that has not spawned, so nothing targets or shows it. <code>entity/playerActor.test.ts</code> checks that, and that <code>Player.damage</code> does nothing to such a player.</li><li>One damage path (item 22). A blow on a player runs <code>DamagePipeline</code> through its actor. <code>intentOfLegacyBlow</code> gives a player a mob&#39;s rules, since the shipped player is of the <code>player</code> family alone. A new <code>game_mode</code> guard (<code>PlayerSystems.reaches</code>) turns aside every blow in creative but the void and <code>/kill</code>, and every blow in spectator. That is <code>LegacyPlayerBody.damage</code>&#39;s rule. One change: <code>/kill</code> on a spectator, which the legacy <code>setHealth</code> reached, is now refused. A mob&#39;s bite, area attack and projectile hit a store player as a store target, so <code>live.ts</code>&#39;s <code>hurt</code>, <code>hurtByProjectile</code> and <code>applySonicBoom</code> no longer meet a player. The live host reads a player&#39;s armor from its inventory. The pipeline spends a player&#39;s absorption from <code>attributes.absorption</code>, which its entity has no attribute for. A shove on a player logs the <code>impulse</code> occurrence and the new host fact <code>playerImpulse</code> sends it to the client.</li><li>Death and respawn (item 24). The store keeps a dead player <code>dying</code>. <code>damageCommitted</code> in <code>live.ts</code> calls <code>Player.die(source)</code> after the <code>entityDeath</code> event and the kill triggers, for the death message, the respawn request and the death screen. <code>Player.respawn</code> calls <code>EntityRuntime.revive</code>, which now also revives a player restored with no health into another store on its way to a spawn point in another dimension.</li><li>The tick. <code>StorePlayerActor.update</code> runs the actor&#39;s part and then <code>Player.updatePlayer</code>, which is now the session, the copy of the entity&#39;s health into the client&#39;s attribute table, the attribute sync, the advancement check and the time. Breath, hunger, the void, the meal on the store&#39;s tick and the crack animation run in <code>PlayerSystems</code>. A player keeps its one actor update a tick in <code>StorePlayer.claimUpdate</code>, because a dimension change mid-tick gives it a new actor in a world the level ticks later. The client sees a health change one tick after the store makes it.</li><li>The inputs (item 14). <code>setMining</code>, <code>beginConsuming</code>, <code>cancelConsuming</code>, <code>setSprinting</code>, <code>setSneaking</code>, <code>addJumpExhaustion</code> and <code>addExhaustion</code> call the systems. A client move goes through <code>moveStorePlayer</code>, <code>setOnGround</code> reports a move to where the player stands, and a server move goes through <code>EntityRuntime.teleportPlayer</code>. Every move broadcasts the actor, so a head-only turn still reaches viewers. <code>Player.isSprinting()</code> replaces reading <code>metadata.sprinting</code>.</li><li>Metadata. <code>StoreActors.sync</code> calls <code>showPlayerState</code> after the projection through a <code>StoreActor</code> method a plain actor answers false to. <code>setGamemode</code> writes <code>abilities.gamemode</code>, calls <code>showPlayerState</code> and sends the metadata at once. The client&#39;s own metadata is the actor&#39;s while the player is in a store and the store player&#39;s <code>metadata</code> while it is in none. <code>Player.syncBreath</code>, its throttle copy, <code>Player</code>&#39;s game mode rule copies and <code>movement.onGround</code> are gone.</li><li>Equipment (item 25). <code>MobEquipmentHandler</code> only moves the hand. <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.entityEquipmentChanged</code> no longer sends the owner a copy, which named hotbar slot zero and would have moved the client&#39;s selection.</li><li>Scans that read the store and then the host&#39;s players (<code>brainWorld.livingNear</code>, <code>goalWorld.mobs</code>, the area attack, the entity sensor, the projectile hit) skip a client-owned store entity, so they meet a player once, through the view that knows its game mode. Before this, a piglin and a warden targeted a creative player in <code>live.test.ts</code>. A store blast takes its candidates from the index, which now holds the player, so it hits a player through the store. The entity index reports no chunk for a player&#39;s move, which closes the gap H-S0 left.</li><li>A pack stack with no <code>minecraft:player</code> file gets <code>FALLBACK_PLAYER_FILE</code> (<code>playerOverlay.ts</code>), so a server without the Bedrock pack still spawns players, and the fixture content has a player. A portal arrival&#39;s offset is now worked out over the player&#39;s 0.6 box where the fixture&#39;s default box was a block wide.</li><li>Deleted: <code>Human.ts</code>, <code>Entities.ts</code> with <code>Entities.Human</code> and <code>Entities.Player</code> and the root <code>Entities</code> export, the player half of <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code> (<code>logLegacySlot</code>, <code>logLegacyImpulse</code>, the player fields and the inventory-slot undo), <code>Entity.restoreLoggedField</code> and <code>restoreLoggedSlot</code>, <code>testing/playerDouble.test.helper.ts</code> and <code>withPlayerComponents</code>. Nothing subclasses <code>Entity</code> in source, which <code>noMirror.test.ts</code> now asserts. The barrel baseline lost one entry.</li><li>The digest (item 35). The player branch reads the <code>StorePlayerActor</code>&#39;s store player, and the component snapshot lost <code>movement.onGround</code>, in a commit of its own. The replay suite compares two replays and passed unchanged.</li><li>Test doubles. <code>fakePlayer()</code> enters a store player into a real <code>LiveEntityRuntime</code> of the fixture content and returns <code>live</code> and <code>clock</code> beside its old fields; its world&#39;s <code>removePlayer</code> leaves the store. <code>playerOnPrototype(members, { runtimeId, position, actor })</code> in <code>testing/storePlayer.test.helper.ts</code> builds a prototype player over a real <code>StorePlayer</code>, and its <code>actor</code> is a <code>StorePlayerActor</code> double. <code>stubPlayerPort</code> answers <code>getActor</code>. Tests of rules that moved to <code>PlayerSystems</code> (breath, hunger over time, meals, crack animation, the void, the walking cost) lost their <code>Player.test.ts</code> copies, and <code>FallDamage.test.ts</code> runs a store player. <code>Level.test.ts</code>&#39;s cross-world tick case moved to <code>storePlayers.test.ts</code>.</li></ul><p>What H-B3 must know:</p><ul><li>The homes are still on <code>Player</code>. Most simulation methods already call <code>PlayerSystems</code> or <code>EntityRuntime</code> through <code>Player.placed()</code>, so moving them is moving one call each. The sends (<code>completeSpawn</code>, <code>sendSettings</code>, <code>setGamemode</code>&#39;s sends, <code>setFlying</code>&#39;s, the death screen in <code>die</code>) are the session&#39;s.</li><li><code>Player</code> keeps <code>world</code> and <code>server</code>, which a <code>StorePlayer</code> has no way to answer. Either the session keeps them, or the store player learns its runtime.</li><li>The session directory is still <code>LegacyPlayers</code> of <code>Player</code> (item 4, H-D), and <code>PlayerSession.shown()</code> reads <code>getBody()</code>.</li><li><code>ParseTargetSelector</code> and the <code>getEntity</code>/<code>getEntities</code> half of <code>World</code> still type <code>Entity</code>. Nothing live is one, so H-C1 can narrow them freely.</li><li><code>Player.getPosition()</code> builds a <code>Position</code> over the player&#39;s world on every call. A caller in a loop should read the store player.</li><li>Not done: no parity family covers the <code>game_mode</code> guard, because it keeps the legacy rule, and the spectator half of Java&#39;s answer for the void was not confirmed against Java.</li></ul><p>In-game checks for the orchestrator&#39;s combined pass, with two vanilla clients A and B on one server:</p><ol><li>A joins. B joins and sees A appear with its skin and name in the world and in the player list.</li><li>A walks, sprints, jumps, sneaks and turns its head only. B sees each, the head turn included.</li><li>A changes the held hotbar slot, puts on a helmet and holds a shield in the off hand. B sees each change at once. A&#39;s own hotbar selection does not jump to slot one.</li><li>A switches to creative. B sees the game mode change. A is not attacked by a zombie or a piglin, and arrows pass it. A switches back to survival and the zombie attacks.</li><li>A eats a golden apple, then takes a zombie&#39;s bite. The yellow hearts go first.</li><li>A sprints until the food bar drops, swims under water until the bubbles go and drowns, and B sees the death animation. A respawns with full food and air.</li><li>A falls eleven blocks onto stone and onto a hay bale. Only the stone costs health.</li><li>A mines a block slowly while B breaks it. A&#39;s cracks stop.</li><li>A walks through a nether portal and back. B sees A leave and come back. A&#39;s cooldown holds while its loading screen is up.</li><li>A sets a bed in the Overworld, goes to the Nether and dies there. A respawns at the bed.</li><li>A leaves. B sees A leave the world and the player list. A logs back in where it left, with its inventory and health.</li></ol><ul><li>Checks at H-B2&#39;s head, <code>8b8e0e51f7</code> on program head <code>d4c6a9a1f2</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (431 files, 4348 tests), <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> byte for byte, <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, the whole parity suite (53 files), all 41 client e2e scenarios in fourteen batches with none failing or flaky, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass.</li></ul><p>H-B3, the homes, left these for waves H-C and H-D. Items 28 and 30 are done, and every method the step named has its home. No behavior changed and no digest moved.</p><ul><li>The client operations are the session&#39;s. <code>PlayerSession.completeSpawn</code>, <code>announceSettings</code> (what <code>Player.sendSettings</code> sends, each half to its audience), <code>getJoinSpawn</code> and <code>setJoinSpawn</code>, <code>sendInitialSpawnChunks</code>, <code>sendDeathScreen</code> (the <code>Respawn</code> and <code>DeathInfo</code> a death sends once the respawn search answers), and the two halves of a game mode change&#39;s sends, <code>sendGameTypeChange</code> and <code>sendAbilityChange</code>. The session&#39;s own join and world change call <code>sendInitialSpawnChunks</code> on themselves, so <code>OwnPlayer</code> lost it. <code>announceSettings</code> fans the abilities out over <code>SessionManager.getSessions</code>, which lists the sessions of the players <code>getAllPlayers</code> did, in the same order. <code>Player.sendMessage</code> was already one call into <code>PlayerSession.sendMessage</code>; it only turns its positional arguments into the delivery, so it moved nothing.</li><li>The simulation is the entity runtime&#39;s. <code>EntityRuntime</code> takes a store player&#39;s inputs: <code>setPlayerMining</code>, <code>beginPlayerMeal</code>, <code>cancelPlayerMeal</code>, <code>setPlayerSprinting</code>, <code>setPlayerSneaking</code>, <code>setPlayerGamemode</code>, <code>setPlayerFlying</code>, <code>exhaustPlayer</code>, <code>playerJumped</code> and <code>revivePlayer</code>. Each takes a <code>PlayerInputTarget</code> (the store player&#39;s <code>entity</code> and <code>getComponents</code>), writes through <code>PlayerSystems</code> while <code>entityOfPlayer</code> finds the entity in this store, and onto the components, or not at all, while it is in none. That is what <code>Player.placed()</code> decided, and <code>placed()</code> now reads <code>entityOfPlayer</code>. <code>PlayerSystems</code> gained <code>setGamemode</code> and <code>setFlying</code>. The rules stay in <code>PlayerSystems</code>; the runtime methods only choose between the two branches.</li><li>What stays on <code>Player</code> besides one-line delegates: the plugin events and the guards before them (<code>setGamemode</code>, <code>setFlying</code> and <code>setSprinting</code> post their events, <code>setSneaking</code> and <code>setSprinting</code> skip a write that changes nothing), and the order of the parts where one method spans both homes. <code>setGamemode</code> is the event, <code>stopMining</code>, <code>setPlayerGamemode</code>, <code>sendGameTypeChange</code>, <code>setFlying(false)</code> for a mode that may not fly, then <code>sendAbilityChange</code>. <code>respawn</code> is <code>revivePlayer</code>, the own-health copy, <code>showPlayerState</code>, <code>getRespawnPoint</code> and <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, then the session&#39;s <code>sendRespawned</code> and <code>releaseChangeHeldOverDeath</code>. The revival has to come before the respawn search&#39;s wait, so the two cannot become one runtime call without changing when the player is alive. <code>die</code> keeps the death message, the respawn request and the death animation and chat line; its death screen is <code>sendDeathScreen</code>. <code>Player</code> also still holds <code>world</code>, <code>server</code>, the detached view, the address, the permissions, the timer, <code>connected</code> and the chat recipient, and <code>enable</code>, <code>disable</code>, <code>getRespawnPoint</code>, <code>forgetLostSpawnPoint</code>, <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, <code>setOnGround</code>, <code>updatePlayer</code> and the item-use clock.</li><li>Item 28. <code>playerRecordOf(player, keyed, dimension)</code> takes a <code>KeyedPlayer</code>, and <code>World.savePlayerData</code> takes any <code>PlayerRecordSource</code> with a name and an XUID, a <code>StorePlayer</code> among them, building the key and taking the server from the world. <code>LivePlayerRecordSource</code> is gone. <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code> now writes the store player&#39;s record through <code>World.savePlayerData</code>, byte for byte against the fixtures.</li><li>Item 30. <code>chatRecipientOf({ runtimeId, identity }, server)</code> in <code>chat/ChatManager.ts</code> builds the recipient from what a session holds: the key and name from the identity, the operator check from the permission list by UUID, and each line sent to <code>SessionManager.sessionOf(runtimeId)</code>. It matches by entity id rather than by name or XUID, because a replaced login can briefly leave two sessions of one account connected. A line for a player who has left reaches nobody, where <code>getNetworkSession()</code> asserted. <code>Player</code> builds its recipient with it, so H-C2 or H-D can build it from the session.</li><li>For H-C3: a handler that called one of these methods on <code>session.getPlayer()</code> calls the home instead: the session&#39;s method on <code>session</code>, or <code>session.getWorld().dataEntities().runtime.setPlayerX(storePlayer, ...)</code> for an input. The runtime methods read <code>world.dataEntities()</code> even for a player in no store, which builds the dimension&#39;s content adapter if nothing had; a live player&#39;s world always has one by then. A handler that posts a player event keeps calling the <code>Player</code> method until H-C2 settles what the events carry. <code>fakePlayer</code>&#39;s session manager now answers <code>getSessions</code>.</li><li>For H-D: <code>Player.getJoinSpawn</code> and the join spawn writes in <code>Player.enable</code> go when <code>enable</code> moves; the session field is already the home. <code>sendGameTypeChange</code> and <code>sendAbilityChange</code> read <code>this.getPlayer().getActor()</code> and <code>getWorld()</code>, which H-D answers from the store player and the session.</li><li>Checks at H-B3&#39;s head, on program head <code>3426f22bdb</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (431 files, 4355 tests), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, the whole parity suite (53 files), client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>death-respawn</code> and <code>dimensions</code> in three batches, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass. No adjacent bug was confirmed.</li></ul><p>H-C3, the handlers, left these for H-D. Item 42 is done for <code>network/handler/</code>, and no handler&#39;s behavior changed.</p><ul><li>The shape. <code>playerPorts.ts</code> holds two helpers and no ports: <code>storePlayerOf(session)</code> is the session&#39;s <code>StorePlayer</code>, <code>session.getPlayer().getStorePlayer()</code> for now, and <code>playerInputsOf(session)</code> is <code>session.getWorld().dataEntities().runtime</code>. <code>KickedPlayer</code>, <code>PlacedPlayer</code>, <code>InventoryHolder</code>, <code>CraftingHolder</code> and <code>FlyingPlayer</code> are gone, and so are the one-handler ports <code>JoiningPlayer</code>, <code>SpawningPlayer</code>, <code>MovedPlayer</code>, <code>RespawningPlayer</code>, <code>ActingPlayer</code> and <code>ReskinnedPlayer</code>. <code>GamemodePlayer</code> stays, because <code>SetPlayerGameTypeHandler</code> reads only what the permission check and <code>setGamemode</code> take. H-D&#39;s switch in the handlers is the body of <code>storePlayerOf</code>, which becomes <code>session.getPlayer()</code>.</li><li>What a handler reads of the store player: the inventory, the crafting grid, the eyes, the look, the ground state, the health, the game mode, the flight, the skin and the components (<code>mining.block</code>, <code>movement.sleeping</code>, <code>movement.sprinting</code>, <code>vitals.fallDistance</code> and the experience a trade pays). The world is the session&#39;s everywhere, and so are the kick, the join spawn, <code>announceSettings</code> and <code>completeSpawn</code>. The inputs go to the runtime: <code>setPlayerMining</code>, <code>setPlayerSneaking</code>, <code>playerJumped</code>, <code>exhaustPlayer</code>, <code>beginPlayerMeal</code> and <code>cancelPlayerMeal</code>. <code>movement.sleeping</code> has no runtime input, so <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> writes it on the components, as <code>Player.setSleeping</code> did.</li><li>Session lookups in place of player lookups. The arm swing, the emote and the shared-container fan-out walk <code>World.getViewers()</code>, the skin relay <code>SessionManager.getSessions()</code>, and the permission target is <code>SessionManager.sessionOf</code>. A sender&#39;s op status is <code>PermissionManager.isOp(session.identity.uuid)</code>. <code>blockTargetRejection</code> takes the session and reads <code>getViewPosition</code> and <code>getWorld</code>. A sound excludes the sender by <code>session.runtimeId</code>, and its actor type is compared with <code>PLAYER_IDENTIFIER</code>.</li><li>What still calls <code>session.getPlayer()</code>, and why: <ul><li>Methods that post a player event: <code>setSprinting</code> and <code>setFlying</code> (<code>PlayerActionHandler</code>, <code>RequestAbilityHandler</code>), <code>setGamemode</code> (<code>SetPlayerGameTypeHandler</code>, <code>SetDefaultGameTypeHandler</code>), and the <code>Player</code> that <code>PlayerMoveEvent</code> and <code>PlayerSpawnEvent</code> carry.</li><li>What the player still keeps, which H-B3 left there: <code>respawn</code>, <code>getRespawnPoint</code>, a move&#39;s <code>setPlayerPosition</code> and <code>setOnGround</code>, <code>getBody</code>, <code>getActor</code>, and the item-use clock (<code>beginItemUse</code>, <code>takeItemUseTicks</code>).</li><li>Callees in other packages that take a <code>Player</code>, or a port the store player does not satisfy: the content interactions (<code>InteractingPlayer</code>), <code>World.breakBlock</code>, <code>World.hitBlock</code>&#39;s striker, <code>World.addPlayer</code>, <code>createStartGameData</code>, <code>playerSpawnPositionPacket</code>, <code>CommandManager.dispatchCommand</code>, the chat sender, <code>PermissionManager.can</code>, <code>advancementItemCrafted</code>, <code>containerAnchorOf</code> and <code>openContainerSlots</code>, and the enchanting and anvil code <code>workstation.ts</code> calls, which now takes a <code>WorkstationPlayer</code> port.</li><li><code>LoginHandler</code> still builds the <code>Player</code>, which is H-D&#39;s.</li></ul></li><li>Doubles. <code>storePlayerPort(store, port)</code> (<code>network/handler/storePlayerPort.test.helper.ts</code>) builds a <code>Player</code> double whose <code>getStorePlayer</code> answers a store player double, and <code>sessionSeeingBlocks</code> (<code>blockTargetSession.test.helper.ts</code>) adds <code>getViewPosition</code> and <code>getWorld</code> to <code>sessionNamedNearBlocks</code>&#39; session. Tests of the input handlers give the world a <code>dataEntities</code> whose runtime double records the inputs, over a real <code>PlayerComponents</code>. The four handler tests H-A3 left on <code>Player</code> (<code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code>, its replay, <code>workstation</code> and <code>BossEventHandler</code>) build ports or a session instead; <code>BossEventHandler.test.ts</code> still takes its session from <code>fakePlayer()</code>.</li><li>For H-D: <code>testing/playerPort.test.helper.ts</code>&#39; prose names the deleted <code>KickedPlayer</code> and <code>PlacedPlayer</code>, and no wave H-C package owns that file. <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> maps between <code>PlayerMining</code> and <code>BlockMining</code> itself where <code>Player.getMining</code> and <code>setMining</code> did, because <code>world/BlockMining.ts</code> was H-C1&#39;s; once <code>Player</code> goes, a null-tolerant pair beside <code>blockMiningOf</code> is the one home.</li><li>Checks at H-C3&#39;s head, on program head <code>48e6a23ee1</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (429 files and 4355 tests passed, 2 files skipped), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, client e2e <code>movement</code>, <code>jump</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>teleport</code> and <code>death-respawn</code> in three batches, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code> and <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> pass. No adjacent bug was confirmed.</li></ul><p>H-C2, the sessions, services and the plugin API, left these for H-D. Item 5&#39;s teardown and question 2 are done. Of item 29, only the boss bar&#39;s legacy form is gone; every other one still has a caller outside H-C2&#39;s files. Item 4 is prepared, and item 42 is done for H-C2&#39;s files. No behavior changed but the plugin API: question 2&#39;s events, and the boss bar.</p><ul><li>The player events (question 2). <code>PlayerEvent.getPlayer()</code> is gone, and <code>getSession()</code> returns the player&#39;s <code>PlayerSession</code> beside <code>getRuntimeId()</code> and <code>getIdentity()</code>, which now read the session. The constructors take a <code>PlayerEventSubject</code>: the session, or anything whose <code>getNetworkSession()</code> returns one. The session is found the first time a listener asks, as the ref was, because the handler tests (H-C3&#39;s) pass player doubles with no session. <code>PlayerMoveEvent</code> starts from the position of the player it is given, or of the session&#39;s player when it is given the session. <code>Player.ts</code> (<code>setGamemode</code>, <code>setSprinting</code>, <code>setFlying</code>) and <code>DimensionTravel</code> still pass the player. <code>MovePlayerHandler</code> and <code>ResourcePackClientResponseHandler</code> pass <code>session.getPlayer()</code> and can pass <code>session</code> as it is, which changes nothing a listener sees. H-D moves the three <code>Player</code> methods&#39; events with their bodies, passes the session everywhere and drops the second form. The Changeset is <code>player-events-carry-the-session.md</code> (<code>minor</code>), and <code>docs/guide/extending.md</code>&#39;s example reads <code>event.getIdentity().name</code>. <code>playerConnect</code>, <code>playerDisconnect</code> and <code>playerDespawn</code> are declared but never posted (#2972).</li><li>The teardown (item 5). <code>ClientConnection.closePlayerSession</code> calls <code>PlayerSession.leave()</code>, which runs <code>getPlayer().disable()</code> and then <code>disable()</code> whether or not the first part threw, as the connection did. H-D replaces the first part with the session&#39;s own leave. H-S0&#39;s edge case holds: when <code>Player.disable</code> throws before <code>removePlayer</code>, the player stays in its world&#39;s map with a forgotten session.</li><li>Item 29. <code>BossBar.addPlayer</code>, <code>removePlayer</code> and <code>answerQuery</code> take a <code>PlayerSession</code> only, since <code>BossEventHandler</code> passes the session after H-C3; the Changeset is <code>boss-bar-takes-the-session.md</code> (<code>minor</code>). Each other legacy form names its remaining callers in its comment. <code>PlayerKeySource</code> and <code>KeyedPlayerSource</code>: <code>Player.enable</code> (<code>loadAdvancements(this)</code>, <code>enchantmentSeedOf(this)</code>), <code>raiseAdvancement</code> and the enchanting table, which take the ports <code>advancement/triggers.ts</code> declares (H-C1), <code>workstation.ts</code> (H-C3), and <code>triggers.test.ts</code> and <code>PlayerPersistenceFormat.test.ts</code>. <code>BanSubject</code>: <code>LoginHandler</code>, which passes <code>{ getName }</code> with the display name. <code>PermissionHolder</code>: <code>Player.enable</code>. Narrowing any of them breaks a file no H-C package of this wave owns, so H-D drops them once those callers pass the key, the session or the identity.</li><li>Item 4, prepared. <code>SessionPlayer</code> in <code>sessionPlayers.ts</code> is what <code>getPlayer()</code> returns and what the session directory, <code>SessionManager</code>&#39;s player lookups and <code>sessionFor</code> hold. It is <code>Player</code> today, and H-D points it at <code>StorePlayer</code>. The session tests&#39; doubles for a session&#39;s player stub <code>SessionPlayer</code>, so they follow the switch, and break only where they reach members a store player lacks. <code>sessionPlayerRefOf</code> lost the events as a caller; <code>createConnectedPlayer</code> is the last. <code>getAllPlayers</code> keeps its callers in <code>Server.ts</code>, <code>modules/network.ts</code>, the handlers and the benchmarks; the ones in H-C2&#39;s files read <code>getSessions()</code>.</li><li>The session as the way in. <code>PermissionManager.setOp</code> finds the session with <code>findSession</code> and sends through it. The remote console&#39;s status, the <code>AvailableCommands</code> soft enum, <code>kickAllPlayers</code> and <code>/ban</code> read each session&#39;s identity and kick through the session. The command host&#39;s verbs that only send take <code>sessionsOf(server, refs)</code>; the ones that change the player and then tell the client take <code>resolveTarget</code>, which hands back the session and the player. <code>CommandPlayer</code> dropped <code>getNetworkSession</code>, <code>kick</code> and <code>sendMessage</code>. <code>/msg</code>, <code>/say</code> and the rest send through <code>PlayerSession.sendMessage</code> with the defaults <code>Player.sendMessage</code> gave them.</li><li>Ports in the session. <code>sendMoveActor</code> and <code>queueMoveActor</code> take a <code>MovedActor</code> instead of an <code>Entity</code>, and <code>OpenTrade.trader</code> is a <code>TradingActor</code> instead of a <code>WorldActor</code>. <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> imports neither class.</li><li>What H-C2&#39;s files still name, and why: <ul><li><code>Player</code>: <code>sessionPlayers.ts</code> (<code>SessionPlayer</code>, <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, <code>sessionPlayerRefOf</code>), which is H-D&#39;s switch.</li><li><code>Entity</code>: <code>CommandManager.dispatchCommand</code>&#39;s target. Its guard <code>isCommandExecutor</code> is in <code>CommandExecutor.ts</code>, which is H-C1&#39;s.</li><li><code>WorldActor</code>: <code>command/host/entities.ts</code>, <code>command/host/support.ts</code> and <code>item/actorPort.ts</code>. They hold what <code>World.getActor</code> returns and follow H-C1&#39;s narrowing of the alias. <code>entities.ts</code>&#39;s <code>/tp</code> keeps the <code>entity.setPosition</code> branch for a legacy entity after the <code>StoreActor</code> one. Once the alias is <code>StoreActor</code>, the narrowing leaves <code>never</code> there and the branch no longer compiles, so H-C1&#39;s narrowing has to come with deleting it. <code>host.test.ts</code> builds its mobs and a dropped item with <code>stubOf(Entity, ...)</code> for the same code.</li><li><code>getNetworkSession()</code> through a port: <code>enchantment/enchantingTable.ts</code> and <code>item/interaction.ts</code>, whose ports the content interactions and <code>workstation.ts</code> hand a <code>Player</code>. <code>commandStartup.test.ts</code>, <code>CommandManager.test.ts</code>, <code>host.test.ts</code> and <code>support.test.ts</code> use <code>playerOnPrototype</code> for players the command host reads, and <code>PermissionManager.test.ts</code> stubs <code>Player</code> as a permission subject. The rest of part 4&#39;s H-C2 tests no longer import a legacy class.</li></ul></li><li>Checks at H-C2&#39;s head, rebased on program head <code>35e5656efc</code>: the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (432 files, 4365 tests), the commands package (11 files), the whole parity suite (53 files, <code>protocol.dimension-change</code> among them), client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code> and <code>duplicate-login</code>, then <code>chat</code>, <code>dimensions</code> and <code>title</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. <code>rejoin</code> passed on a second run: the first time the client exited before its control endpoint came up, with &quot;macOS did not apply the hidden rendering activation policy&quot;, before any join. The adjacent gap found was already #2972.</li></ul><p>H-C1, the world and entity side, left these for H-D. Item 36 is done but for one call, item 34 is done for H-C1&#39;s callers of <code>getActors</code>, item 33 is done for the entity index and the blast, and item 42 is done for H-C1&#39;s files. No behavior changed and no digest moved.</p><ul><li>Item 33, the index. <code>EntityIndex</code> lost <code>addLegacy</code>, <code>refileLegacy</code>, <code>removeLegacy</code>, <code>legacyWithin</code> and the body attachment, and <code>World</code> no longer files a legacy entity in it. A blast in <code>entity/Explosion.ts</code> takes store actors from <code>storeWithin</code> alone, and reads its eye height fraction from <code>ENTITY_EYE_HEIGHT_FRACTION</code>. <code>Explosion.test.ts</code> catches store entities through actor doubles over a real store, and <code>WorldEntityIndex.test.ts</code> lost the cases that only exercised the legacy half.</li><li>Item 33, what is left. <code>World</code>&#39;s legacy half stays whole: the entity map, <code>addEntity</code>, <code>removeEntity</code>, <code>getEntity</code>, <code>getEntities</code>, <code>entityRelocated</code> and the chunk membership that serves them. Their callers are outside H-C1&#39;s files: <code>DimensionTravel.transfer</code>&#39;s legacy branch, <code>/kill</code> in <code>command/host/entities.ts</code>, <code>Entity.ts</code> (<code>getNearestEntity</code>, <code>relocate</code>), <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code>, and the tests <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, <code>DimensionTravel.test.ts</code>, <code>PlayerPersistence.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldChangeTick.test.ts</code>, <code>live.test.ts</code>, <code>serverSource.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldAutoSave.test.ts</code> and <code>benchmarks/entityOnlyChunkSave.ts</code>. The player map stays typed by <code>Player</code>, because <code>item/interaction.test.ts</code> and <code>benchmarks/worldUpdateChunkWork.bench.ts</code> fill it with players, and <code>getPlayers</code>, <code>addPlayer</code> and <code>removePlayer</code> read it.</li><li>Item 33, the alias. <code>WorldActor</code> cannot narrow to <code>StoreActor</code> yet. Narrowing it breaks more than the <code>/tp</code> branch H-C2 named: <code>command/host/entities.ts</code> at <code>familiesOf</code>, <code>/kill</code>&#39;s two legacy branches and <code>/damage</code> as well, <code>item/actorPort.ts</code>&#39;s teleport, <code>DimensionTravel.transfer</code>, and <code>Entity.ts</code>, which hands <code>this</code> to <code>EntityDamageEvent</code>, <code>EntityDeathEvent</code>, <code>advancementDamage</code>, <code>advancementKill</code> and the portal travel. H-D narrows it with <code>Entity.ts</code> gone and those branches deleted.</li><li>Item 34. <code>World.actors()</code> walks the legacy map and the store&#39;s actors in place, in no order. The chunk save context, the provider&#39;s <code>EntitySource</code> (now any iterable), the level&#39;s entity snapshot, the stored actor restore and the end crystal&#39;s overlap check walk it, and <code>createChunkSaveContext</code> sorts each chunk&#39;s lists by runtime id and gives a duplicate actor id to the lower runtime id, which is what the sorted input gave. The content adapter&#39;s <code>at</code>, <code>near</code> and <code>countOfType</code> ask the index for store entities and dropped stacks within a horizontal radius, merge the two answers by id, and apply the old exact test (question 10). <code>getActors</code> keeps its sorted copy for the replay digest and for <code>command/host/entities.ts</code> and <code>jsprismarineCommands.ts</code>, outside H-C1&#39;s files. <code>shownActors</code> still concatenates, because <code>WorldChangeSink.reconcile</code> and the session&#39;s reconcile take an array.</li><li>After H-C4&#39;s note: <code>LiveEntityRuntime.savedRecords</code> and the dragon fight&#39;s eight store reads walk <code>EntityRuntime.entities()</code>. The one caller of <code>all()</code> left in source is <code>entity/contentAdapter.ts</code>, with the tests, so <code>noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code>&#39; <code>it.todo</code> for <code>all()</code> waits on those.</li><li>Item 36. <code>World</code> reads its store and runtime without asking: <code>update</code>, the change tick, <code>runInput</code>, chunk entry and exit, the stored actor restore, the chunk change mark, <code>shownActors</code> and <code>getActors</code>. <code>getActor</code> keeps <code>getEntityRuntime?.</code>, because <code>item/interaction.test.ts</code> builds a world whose server has no runtime. <code>hasEntityStore</code> stays for <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code>. <code>inertEntityRuntime</code> and three world doubles gained a real, empty <code>EntityStore</code>.</li><li>Players through the viewers. <code>World</code>&#39;s spawner proximity check, raid host and both saves walk <code>getViewers</code>; the spawner measures from <code>PlayerSession.getViewPosition</code> less the eye height. The content adapter&#39;s <code>players</code> and the dragon fight&#39;s boss bar and killer read <code>getViewers</code>. <code>breakBlock</code> takes a <code>BlockBreaker</code> and the chunk coverage a <code>CoveringPlayer</code> port. Two doubles outside H-C1&#39;s files followed: <code>worldDestruction.test.ts</code>&#39; world answers a viewer, and <code>fakePlayer</code>&#39;s world, which already listed its player as a viewer, answers <code>getKnownSpawnPosition</code>, which the spawn cycle asks once the adapter sees that player.</li><li>Narrowed to ports. <code>ParseTargetSelector</code> is generic over a <code>SelectorEntity</code> and finds <code>@p</code> itself with <code>getNearestEntity</code>&#39;s rule; <code>ParseTildeCaretNotation</code> reads only the source&#39;s position; <code>isCommandExecutor</code> takes a <code>CommandTarget</code>. <code>CommandManager.dispatchCommand</code> still annotates its target <code>CommandExecutor | Entity</code>, which can now read <code>CommandTarget</code>. <code>loose</code> and <code>LiveEntityRuntime.killEntity</code> take a <code>StoreActor</code>. After H-C3&#39;s note, <code>playerMiningOf</code> and <code>blockMiningOf</code> take and answer null through overloads, and <code>PlayerActionHandler</code> calls them directly; <code>Player.ts</code> keeps its own null checks.</li><li>What H-C1&#39;s files still name, and why: <ul><li><code>Player</code>: <code>World.ts</code>&#39;s player map, <code>getPlayers</code>, <code>addPlayer</code> and <code>removePlayer</code>, and the prose of the legacy methods.</li><li><code>Entity</code>: <code>World.ts</code>&#39;s legacy half above.</li><li><code>WorldActor</code>: the alias itself, <code>World.ts</code> (<code>actors</code>, <code>getActors</code>, <code>getActor</code>, the digest), <code>ChunkSaveContext.ts</code>, <code>BaseProvider.ts</code>, <code>EntitySerializer.toNBT</code>, the three <code>events/entity</code> classes and <code>advancement/triggers.ts</code>, which <code>Entity.ts</code> hands <code>this</code>; <code>Combat.ts</code>&#39;s targets, which <code>InventoryTransactionHandler</code> takes from <code>World.getActor</code>; and <code>live.ts</code>&#39;s <code>damageEntity</code>, the naming, dyeing, projectile and item interaction methods, which <code>item/actorPort.ts</code> calls with a <code>WorldActor</code>, and <code>savedRecords</code>, which <code>WorldReplayState.test.ts</code> keys with legacy bodies. <code>Level.ts</code> imports only <code>byRuntimeId</code> from <code>WorldActor.ts</code>.</li><li>Tests: <code>Combat.test.ts</code> still swings <code>GenericEntity</code> doubles, because its attacker and target stay <code>WorldActor</code>; <code>WorldEntityIndex.test.ts</code> keeps two cases for the legacy chunk membership; <code>WorldGeneratedEntities.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldEntityAddition.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldTickPhases.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldReplayState.test.ts</code> and <code>triggers.test.ts</code> still build or stub a legacy entity for the code above. They go with the legacy half.</li></ul></li><li>The Changeset is <code>world-entities-from-the-store.md</code> (<code>minor</code>): the index&#39;s legacy methods are gone and two parameters narrowed.</li><li>Found: neither blast path destroys a dropped stack, because both read the index&#39;s mob cells and a stack is filed only in its item cells (#2981).</li><li>Checks at H-C1&#39;s head, on program head <code>6c24c5a6f1</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (432 files, 4370 tests), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, the whole parity suite (53 files), client e2e <code>attack-entity</code> and <code>summon-entity</code>, then <code>ender-dragon</code> and <code>natural-spawn-placement</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass.</li></ul><p>H-D, the deletion, closed C4h and slice 5. Items 4, 31, 40, 41 and 43 are done, and so are what wave H-C left of items 29, 33, 34, 36 and 42. No behavior changed but the plugin API, and no digest moved.</p><ul><li>The switch (item 4). <code>LoginHandler</code> builds a <code>StorePlayer</code> with an id from <code>entityIds</code> and the login&#39;s identity, skin and device, standing <code>EYE_HEIGHT</code> below the spawn position, and <code>createConnectedPlayer</code> files it in <code>Server.getStorePlayers()</code>, which is <code>EntityRuntimes.players</code> and the one session directory. <code>session.getPlayer()</code> returns it. <code>LegacyPlayers</code>, <code>sessionPlayerRefOf</code>, <code>SessionManager.getLegacyPlayers</code> and the handler helpers <code>storePlayerOf</code> and <code>playerInputsOf</code> are gone; a handler reads <code>session.getPlayer()</code> and <code>session.inputs()</code>. <code>SessionPlayer</code> is <code>StorePlayer</code>.</li><li>What <code>Player</code> held moved to the session: the world, the detached view, the permissions, the joined flag (<code>isConnected()</code>), the chat recipient and the timer. <code>join()</code> and <code>leave()</code> are what <code>Player.enable</code> and <code>Player.disable</code> did, with the join spawn writes H-B3 named. The session owns <code>setGamemode</code>, <code>setFlying</code> and <code>setSprinting</code> with their events, <code>respawn</code>, <code>die</code>, <code>getRespawnPoint</code>, <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, <code>setOnGround</code>, <code>setWorld</code>, <code>updatePlayer</code>, <code>stopMining</code>, <code>getActor</code>, <code>shown()</code> and <code>keyed()</code>, and one-line state reads so it satisfies the mixed ports the content interactions, advancements, workstations and commands take. The store player took the state the class only forwarded: the item-use clock, the sprint, sneak and sleep flags, the fall distance, the experience, the equipment, the tags and status effects, and <code>setAttributeValue</code>. <code>StorePlayer</code> is the record&#39;s one <code>PlayerRecordTarget</code> (item 31).</li><li>The world (items 33 and 36). <code>World</code>&#39;s player map holds sessions, <code>getViewers</code> reads it, and <code>getPlayers</code> is gone; <code>addPlayer</code> and <code>removePlayer</code> take the session. The entity map, <code>addEntity</code>, <code>removeEntity</code>, <code>getEntity</code>, <code>getEntities</code>, <code>entityRelocated</code>, <code>hasEntityStore</code> and the chunk membership are gone. <code>actors()</code>, <code>getActors()</code> and <code>getActor()</code> answer <code>StoreActor</code>s, and the <code>WorldActor</code> alias is gone: <code>byRuntimeId</code> lives in <code>world/runtimeIdOrder.ts</code>. <code>getActor</code> keeps <code>getEntityRuntime?.</code> for the world doubles whose server has no runtime. The legacy branches after a store one are deleted: <code>/kill</code>, <code>/damage</code>, <code>/tp</code> and <code>familiesOf</code> in the command host, the item actor port&#39;s teleport and <code>DimensionTravel.transfer</code>, which always moves a store entity through <code>transferStoreEntity</code>. <code>CommandManager.dispatchCommand</code>&#39;s target is a <code>CommandTarget</code>.</li><li>The copies (item 34). <code>EntityRuntime.all()</code> is gone. The content adapter&#39;s <code>at</code> walks <code>entities()</code> in place, <code>EntityScene</code> declares <code>entities()</code>, and the attribute flush walks <code>World.actors()</code>. <code>getActors</code> keeps its sorted copy for callers outside the tick: the replay digest and the commands.</li><li>The keyed services (item 29). <code>PlayerKeySource</code> is a key or a <code>KeyedPlayer</code>; <code>KeyedPlayerSource</code> and <code>keyedPlayerOf</code> are gone, and a session answers <code>keyed()</code>. <code>BanSubject</code> is <code>{ name }</code> and <code>PermissionHolder</code> is <code>{ uuid }</code>. The player events take the session only.</li><li>The deletions (items 40 and 41). <code>Player.ts</code>, <code>entity/Entity.ts</code>, <code>legacyAdapter.ts</code>, <code>network/handler/playerPorts.ts</code>, the root <code>Player</code> export and <code>entity/index.ts</code>&#39;s <code>export * from &#39;./Entity&#39;</code>, which became a named <code>DamageCause</code> export and lowered the barrel baseline from 5 to 4. The JSDoc baseline lost the deleted classes&#39; entries. The Changeset is <code>player-is-the-store-player.md</code> (<code>minor</code>) and lists each plugin-facing change.</li><li>The proxy test (item 43). <code>noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code>&#39; two <code>it.todo</code> are real: no <code>Entity</code> or <code>Player</code> class is declared and neither file exists, the runtime has no <code>all()</code> and no source calls <code>getEntities()</code>, and no source copies the store through <code>all()</code>. Its eight cases pass.</li><li>Tests. <code>fakePlayer</code> returns the session beside its store player, <code>sessionOnPrototype</code> replaces <code>playerOnPrototype</code>, <code>connectedSession</code> builds a login&#39;s pair, and <code>Player.test.ts</code> became <code>network/PlayerSessionPlayer.test.ts</code>. <code>testing/genericEntity.test.helper.ts</code>, <code>storePlayerPort.test.helper.ts</code>, <code>Entity.test.ts</code>, <code>WorldEntityAddition.test.ts</code> and <code>Damage.pipeline.test.ts</code> are gone with the code they covered. <code>Damage.pipeline.test.ts</code> drove <code>Entity.damage</code>. The store&#39;s versions of its rules are covered elsewhere: the grace period, the knockback and the void in <code>damagePipeline.test.ts</code>, which gained a block for the rules a swing keeps; absorption in <code>playerSystems.test.ts</code>; knockback resistance in <code>EntityRuntime.test.ts</code>; and the hurt animation and the plugin events in <code>live.test.ts</code>. <code>Combat.test.ts</code> swings at store cows. It lost its grace-period case, because the fixture cow is not of the <code>mob</code> family, which the grace needs; <code>damagePipeline.test.ts</code> covers the grace. <code>PlayerPersistence.test.ts</code>&#39; cross-level digest case lost its mob, because a store mob&#39;s <code>persistentIdentity</code> is a fresh UUID each spawn; <code>WorldReplayState.test.ts</code> covers mob hashing.</li><li>What still names a deleted class, and why: Java references such as <code>Entity.checkInsideBlocks</code>, which name Mojang&#39;s classes; the comments in <code>storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>playerSystems.ts</code>, <code>live.ts</code>, <code>itemActor.ts</code> and <code>EntityRuntime.ts</code> that compare a store rule with what a legacy player or body did, which is the parity they keep; <code>legacyDamage.ts</code>, whose <code>DamageSource</code> the plugin events and the advancement triggers still take; <code>EntityStore.writtenLegacy</code>, which logs a rider&#39;s vehicle link when the rider is in no store; and the test names and prose of <code>noMirror.test.ts</code> and <code>noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code>.</li><li>Flaky, not caused by H-D: in the full client e2e run, the batch of <code>teleport</code>, <code>teleport-while-dead</code> and <code>title</code> failed once at the suite reset (&quot;the client never stood at 0.5, 64, 0.5; at 0.5, -60, 0.5&quot;), with the server log repeating &quot;Teleported 1 entities&quot; and &quot;Dropped a position ... sent before its teleport&quot;. The batch then passed three times, each scenario passed alone, and the batch passed three times on the program head. In the root <code>pnpm run test</code>, run at a load average near 12, <code>modules/ticker.test.ts</code>&#39; &quot;uses a timeout for a tick that finishes before its fixed deadline&quot; and <code>HttpSignaling.loopback.test.ts</code>&#39; WebRTC join (a 20-second timeout) failed once and passed three times alone; neither file changed.</li><li>Checks at H-D&#39;s head, on program head <code>7f8b1e99f0</code>: <code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (430 files, 4334 tests), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, the whole parity suite, the full client e2e suite in batches of two or three, <code>pnpm run e2e:client:dist</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. <code>pnpm run test</code> passed but for the two flaky cases above. No adjacent bug was confirmed.</li></ul><h3 id="phase-c-the-store" tabindex="-1">Phase C: the store <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-c-the-store" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase C: the store”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C1</td><td>2</td><td>A4, A5, B1</td><td>The entity store, logged setters, the one index and the adapter over legacy entities. Component state the entities package keeps in <code>WeakMap</code>s moves onto the instance, where the store can log and save it</td></tr><tr><td>C2</td><td>3</td><td>C1</td><td>Items and the eye of ender move into the store</td></tr><tr><td>C3</td><td>4</td><td>C1</td><td>Staged damage and death, the mirror deleted, <code>damage</code> synchronous</td></tr><tr><td>C4</td><td>5</td><td>C2, C3, B3, B5</td><td>Players move into the store. The legacy entity classes and the adapter are deleted. Split into the packages below</td></tr></tbody></table><p>C2 and C3 run side by side if C1 leaves item code and damage code in separate files. Otherwise C3 follows C2.</p><p><a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/research/player-entity-spike.html">The player spike</a> found that a player fits the store with three host rules, which ADR 0008 now records, and that C4 is too large for one package. 79 source files and 117 test files name <code>Player</code>. C4 splits as follows.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C4b</td><td>C1, C3</td><td>The player overlay, the rule that the client owns a player&#39;s movement, and a death that keeps the entity</td></tr><tr><td>C4c</td><td>C1</td><td>Player components with logged setters, and an inventory that logs one entry for each slot</td></tr><tr><td>C4d</td><td>C4b, C4c</td><td>Player systems: hunger, breath, fall damage, consumption and mining, reading loaded chunks only</td></tr><tr><td>C4e</td><td>C4c, B3</td><td>Persistence from the store entity, with the saved record unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>C4f</td><td>C4c</td><td>The session holds the entity id. Convert the files that name <code>Player</code>, split by directory</td></tr><tr><td>C4g</td><td>C4b, C4c</td><td>Show store players to clients through the current replicator until slice 7</td></tr><tr><td>C4h</td><td>C4b to C4g</td><td>Convert the tests, then delete the legacy entity classes and the adapter</td></tr></tbody></table><p>C4b and C4c run side by side. C4d, C4e, C4f and C4g run side by side after C4c. The spike&#39;s note lists what the adapter of C1 must provide while players are still legacy, and that list is part of C1&#39;s brief. One item needs an answer when C3 lands: the mirror is what shows store mobs to legacy sessions today, so from the slice that deletes it until slice 5 something else has to encode them.</p><p>The change log module of A4 left questions for C1, answered here.</p><ul><li>A chunk entering the world is a unit of its own. The input step admits a chunk as one input, and no other write shares its span, so a span that would have to be undone never holds a chunk entry.</li><li>A membership entry carries one record, the entity&#39;s saved form, and that form has to contain everything replication sends on first sight. ADR 0012 and ADR 0016 describe the same record.</li><li>Inverses are written through setters that do not log. If writing an inverse throws, the store can no longer be trusted, so the world stops ticking and reports the failure. It does not go on with a half-undone unit.</li><li>A chest&#39;s slots are fields of its block entity, addressed by block position. An entity&#39;s inventory slots are addressed by entity id.</li></ul><h3 id="phase-d-handlers-and-replication" tabindex="-1">Phase D: handlers and replication <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-d-handlers-and-replication" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase D: handlers and replication”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D1</td><td>6</td><td>C4</td><td>The shared handler shape in <code>@jsprismarine/protocol</code> and the narrow server context</td></tr><tr><td>D2</td><td>6</td><td>D1</td><td>Convert the input-lane handlers to the shape, split into groups that run side by side. The client adopts the shape</td></tr><tr><td>D3</td><td>7</td><td>C4</td><td>Block and block entity writes go through logged setters</td></tr><tr><td>D4</td><td>7</td><td>D2, D3</td><td>The replica, one audience per change, one encode per codec group, and sends out of the simulation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>D3 runs beside D1 and D2.</p><p>D1, the handler shape and the narrow context, left these for D2. Counts are from program head <code>d2d36c1c2f</code>.</p><ul><li>The shared shape is <code>@jsprismarine/protocol/handler/PacketHandler</code>. <code>PacketHandler&lt;P, C, Phase&gt;</code> has <code>phases</code> and <code>handle(packet, context)</code>, which may return a promise. <code>LaneHandler&lt;P, C, L, Phase&gt;</code> adds the <code>lane</code>. <code>InputHandler&lt;P, C, Phase&gt;</code> is the input-lane form, and its <code>handle</code> returns <code>undefined</code>, not <code>void</code>: a function that returns a promise is assignable to one that returns <code>void</code>, so only <code>undefined</code> makes an asynchronous input-lane handler fail to typecheck. <code>ServerboundHandler&lt;P, Contexts, Phase&gt;</code> is the union, with each lane&#39;s context taken from <code>Contexts</code>. The packet, the context and the phases are the side&#39;s own, so the client&#39;s decoded payloads fit as they are. The lanes (<code>LANES</code>, <code>Lane</code>, <code>strictestLane</code>) moved to <code>@jsprismarine/protocol/handler/Lane</code>, and Prismarine&#39;s <code>network/handler/Lane.ts</code> is gone. The protocol root exports neither module (ADR 0010).</li><li>The server&#39;s context is <code>network/handler/HandlerContext.ts</code>. A connection-lane or read-lane handler gets a <code>SessionContext</code>: the <code>session</code>, a <code>log</code> (the logger&#39;s levels) and a <code>config</code> picked to the six settings handlers read. An input-lane handler gets an <code>InputContext</code>, which adds <code>services</code> and <code>step</code>. <code>HandlerServices</code> is every server-wide service a handler calls today, each a <code>Pick</code> of the members it calls: the content, the content world&#39;s <code>actorsAt</code>, the creative inventory, the sessions, the permissions, the commands, the chat, the console sender, the plugin events and the default game mode. It covers all 72 handlers, so a D2 package does not need to add one. <code>InputStep</code> has the <code>tick</code>, <code>output(send)</code>, which takes a send the handler started without awaiting it, and <code>defer(work)</code>, which takes work that still has to wait.</li><li>The server&#39;s handler shapes are <code>network/handler/SessionHandler.ts</code>: <code>ConnectionLaneHandler</code>, <code>ReadLaneHandler</code> and <code>InputLaneHandler</code>, each with <code>takes: &#39;context&#39;</code>, which is how <code>takesContext</code> tells them from the handlers that take the <code>Server</code>. The field goes when the last handler that takes the server goes.</li><li>The adapter is <code>network/handlerContexts.ts</code>, the one module that turns a <code>Server</code> into a context, outside <code>network/handler/</code>. <code>HandlerContexts.of(server).run(handler, packet, session)</code> awaits a connection-lane or read-lane handler. It applies an input-lane one inside <code>World.runInputNow</code>, a synchronous change log unit (<code>EntityStore.unit</code>) reached through <code>applyInputNow</code> in <code>world/inputUnit.ts</code>. Then it runs the deferred work in order, each piece through <code>applyInput</code> as a unit of its own, and last it awaits the outputs, so the next input still starts after all of it. The context reads the server&#39;s parts when a handler uses them, so the content is not read before the pack stack is built. <code>NetworkDispatcher.runHandler</code> sends a handler with <code>takes</code> there and every other handler down the path it took before. <code>appendHandler</code> merges either shape with the other into a handler that takes the server.</li><li>What changed for a converted input handler: its writes close as one unit before its sends are awaited, so a failed send no longer undoes them, and a deferred piece that throws undoes only its own writes. The order of effects is unchanged.</li><li>Converted, one of each kind: <code>NetworkStackLatencyHandler</code> (connection), <code>SubChunkRequestHandler</code> (read), <code>AnimateHandler</code> (an input that only relays, through <code>step.output</code>) and <code>MobEquipmentHandler</code> (an input that writes the hand slot). Their tests build contexts with <code>sessionContext</code> and <code>inputContext</code> in <code>testing/handlerContext.test.helper.ts</code>; <code>inputContext</code> keeps the outputs and the deferred work and <code>settled()</code> runs them as the step does. Each converted handler destructures its context (<code>{ session, log, step }: InputContext</code>), which keeps the diff of a conversion to the signature and the log calls.</li><li><code>Lane.test.ts</code>&#39; check that connection-lane handlers keep out of the world now reads a context parameter: the <code>session</code> destructured from a <code>SessionContext</code> or <code>InputContext</code>, or read as <code>context.session</code>, is checked as a <code>PlayerSession</code>.</li><li>The proxy test is <code>network/handler/noServerAccess.test.ts</code>, an explicit allowlist that D2 empties, not a script baseline in the style of <code>check-barrels.mts</code>. Each module either reaches the server or does not, so there is no count to lower; the test lives beside the handlers like <code>noMirror.test.ts</code> and <code>noLegacyEntity.test.ts</code> and runs in every Prismarine test run; and the list is grouped by D2 package, so packages that run side by side delete lines in their own block. A module reaches <code>Server</code> when it imports anything from <code>src/Server</code>, imports a binding named <code>Server</code> from the package root, or calls <code>getServer()</code> on something it holds, which <code>PlayerSession.getServer()</code> and <code>World.getServer()</code> still allow. The test fails when an unlisted module reaches the server and when a listed one has stopped. 9 of the 80 modules under <code>network/handler/</code> reach no server. The target gates once the list is empty.</li><li>Not narrowed: the context&#39;s <code>session</code> is the whole <code>PlayerSession</code>, which still answers <code>getServer()</code> and <code>getWorld()</code>. The test catches the call in a handler module, not in the code a handler calls.</li><li>Found: in the client suite, four NetherNet tests fail together with <code>connecterror:10</code> (#2983), and the replay test can stop on a held RakNet client timer when files run one after another (#2984). Both happen on program head <code>d2d36c1c2f</code> too, and each file passes alone.</li><li>Checks at D1&#39;s head, on program head <code>d2d36c1c2f</code>: <code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (432 files, 4349 tests), the protocol suite (395 files), the parity suite (53 files), client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>movement</code>, <code>duplicate-login</code> and <code>subchunk-request-delivery</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passed with no baseline touched, in two parallel client runs and three times alone. The client suite&#39;s other failures are #2983 and #2984.</li></ul><h4 id="d2-the-remaining-handlers-on-the-context" tabindex="-1">D2: the remaining handlers on the context <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2-the-remaining-handlers-on-the-context" aria-label="Permalink to “D2: the remaining handlers on the context”">​</a></h4><p>D2 moves the other 68 handlers and <code>workstation.ts</code> off the <code>Server</code>, in seven packages that run side by side, each in its own worktree, and one serial close. A package owns the files in its row and the tests beside them, and nothing else. The lists match the blocks of <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code>; a package deletes its block as it converts.</p><p>How a handler converts:</p><ul><li>It implements <code>ConnectionLaneHandler</code>, <code>ReadLaneHandler</code> or <code>InputLaneHandler</code>, declares <code>takes = &#39;context&#39;</code>, destructures its context, and logs through <code>log</code>.</li><li>An input-lane handler&#39;s <code>handle</code> returns <code>undefined</code> and awaits nothing. A send goes to <code>step.output</code>. Work that still waits for something a later slice removes goes to <code>step.defer</code>, with a comment naming that slice: a world change or the credits (9), the respawn search (9), a command or a chat line (9, unless the command is synchronous by then), or a disk write that the ADR moves off the tick. A block read uses a loaded-chunk accessor and rejects an unloaded chunk, as ADR 0014 says, and does not defer.</li><li>A helper the handler passes the <code>Server</code> to takes the service it reads instead, such as <code>contentUse(content, contentWorld, player)</code> for <code>contentUse(server, player)</code>.</li><li>Each package traces every one of its handlers&#39; lanes through what the handler calls, which closes the last item of &quot;Checks to run&quot; for its files, and moves a handler whose trace shows another lane.</li></ul><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Files under <code>network/handler/</code></th><th>What is not mechanical</th><th>Gate beyond the unit tests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D2a connection lane</td><td>the 31 connection-lane session handlers in its block, from <code>ActorPickRequestHandler</code> to <code>UpdateClientOptionsHandler</code></td><td>Nothing. <code>SetDifficultyHandler</code> reads <code>config.getDifficulty</code></td><td><code>Lane.test.ts</code>; client e2e <code>spawn</code></td></tr><tr><td>D2b read lane</td><td><code>AnvilDamage</code>, <code>BookEdit</code>, <code>BossEvent</code>, <code>LabTable</code>, <code>LecternUpdate</code>, <code>PlayerToggleCrafterSlotRequest</code>, <code>StructureBlockUpdate</code>, <code>StructureTemplateDataRequest</code>, and <code>BlockTarget.ts</code></td><td>The six that are write requests the server drops stay in the read lane until their write exists (B1). The parity check that rejecting an interaction with an unloaded chunk matches Java (&quot;Checks to run&quot;), as a test under a declared family, since <code>BlockTarget</code> is where the rejection lives</td><td>The new parity test; client e2e <code>block-placement</code></td></tr><tr><td>D2c relays and session inputs</td><td><code>ActorEvent</code>, <code>ContainerClose</code>, <code>Disconnect</code>, <code>Emote</code>, <code>Interact</code>, <code>LevelSoundEvent</code>, <code>PlayerSkin</code>, <code>RequestAbility</code>, <code>ResourcePackChunkRequest</code>, <code>ServerboundLoadingScreen</code>, <code>SetPlayerGameType</code>, <code>ShowCredits</code></td><td>The relays move to <code>step.output</code>. <code>ShowCredits</code> and <code>ServerboundLoadingScreen</code> complete a held world change, which they defer. <code>Disconnect</code> closes the connection, which the dispatcher already holds as an input</td><td>Client e2e <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code>, <code>title</code>; parity <code>protocol.dimension-change</code></td></tr><tr><td>D2d movement, actions and inventory</td><td><code>InventoryTransaction</code>, <code>ItemStackRequest</code>, <code>MovePlayer</code>, <code>PlayerAction</code>, <code>PlayerAuthInput</code>, <code>RequestChunkRadius</code>, <code>Respawn</code>, and <code>workstation.ts</code>, <code>tradeScreen.ts</code>, <code>eyeOfEnderEcho.ts</code></td><td>One package because <code>PlayerAuthInputHandler</code> runs the other three&#39;s code and <code>applyClientMovement</code>. <code>PlayerAction</code>&#39;s block read is the <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code> entry for slice 6, which goes with it. The respawn search is deferred. <code>workstation.ts</code> stops calling <code>getServer()</code></td><td>The replay suite with unchanged digests; client e2e <code>movement</code>, <code>jump</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>death-respawn</code></td></tr><tr><td>D2e commands, chat and permissions</td><td><code>CommandRequest</code>, <code>RequestPermissions</code>, <code>SetDefaultGameType</code>, <code>SettingsCommand</code>, <code>Text</code></td><td>ADR 0014: an operator list change updates memory synchronously and persists off the tick, which needs a synchronous <code>PermissionManager</code> member. The default game mode&#39;s config write the same. Commands and chat are deferred</td><td>Client e2e <code>chat</code>; the commands package&#39;s suite</td></tr><tr><td>D2f the join</td><td><code>Login</code>, <code>ClientToServerHandshake</code>, <code>RequestNetworkSettings</code>, <code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code>, <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>, <code>ConnectionPacketHandler.ts</code></td><td>The three handlers before a session exist take a <code>ClientConnection</code>, so the package designs their context in a file of its own. B1&#39;s note moves building the player and reading the spawn in front of the join, whose result enters the world as an input. <code>ConnectionPacketHandler.ts</code> goes</td><td>The replay suite; client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>duplicate-login</code>, <code>dimensions</code></td></tr><tr><td>D2g the client</td><td><code>packages/client</code>: <code>ClientPacketRegistry.ts</code>, <code>ClientSession.ts</code>, <code>Client.ts</code>, <code>DimensionChange.ts</code>, <code>Credits.ts</code>, <code>login/LoginSequence.ts</code> and their tests</td><td>The client registers <code>PacketHandler</code>s by packet name and phase over its decoded payloads with a client context, in place of each responder&#39;s <code>on(&#39;packet&#39;)</code> listener and <code>isPacket</code> chain. The client has no world tick, so its handlers stay asynchronous</td><td>The client suite and <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with unchanged digests</td></tr><tr><td>D2 close, after the others</td><td><code>PacketHandler.ts</code>, <code>SessionHandler.ts</code>, <code>network/handlerContexts.ts</code>, <code>NetworkDispatcher.ts</code>, <code>PacketHandlerRegistry.ts</code>, <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code></td><td>Delete the shape that takes the server, the <code>takes</code> field and <code>takesContext</code>, and the dispatcher&#39;s and <code>appendHandler</code>&#39;s second path. The allowlist is empty, and the test asserts that nothing reaches the server. Count the <code>step.defer</code> calls through <code>reportProgress</code>, as the work slice 9 has left</td><td>Everything in the gate for each slice; the full client e2e suite</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Rules that keep the packages apart:</p><ul><li>Only D2e edits <code>HandlerContext.ts</code>, for the members it owns (<code>permissions</code>, <code>defaults</code>, <code>commands</code>, <code>chat</code>). Another package that needs a service changed waits for the close.</li><li>Only D2f edits <code>NetworkDispatcher.ts</code> and the <code>KNOWN_WORLD_ACCESS</code> list in <code>Lane.test.ts</code>. No package edits <code>handlerContexts.ts</code> or <code>testing/handlerContext.test.helper.ts</code>; a package that needs another helper adds a file of its own under <code>testing/</code>.</li><li>Narrow a helper&#39;s parameter freely. Narrow a return type or an exported member only when every reader is in the package.</li><li>Each package adds its own Changeset. A handler&#39;s <code>handle</code> signature is public through Prismarine&#39;s <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> export, so a package that converts one adds a <code>minor</code> one; D2g&#39;s changes the client&#39;s public API, which is <code>minor</code> too.</li></ul><h5 id="d2a-the-connection-lane" tabindex="-1">D2a: the connection lane <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2a-the-connection-lane" aria-label="Permalink to “D2a: the connection lane”">​</a></h5><ul><li>The 31 connection-lane session handlers in D2a&#39;s block, from <code>ActorPickRequestHandler</code> to <code>UpdateClientOptionsHandler</code>, take a <code>SessionContext</code>, and the block is gone from <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code>. 40 of the 80 modules under <code>network/handler/</code> reach no server.</li><li>Each handler&#39;s lane was traced through what it calls, and none moves. 25 log the packet and drop it, reading only the session&#39;s <code>identity</code> or <code>runtimeId</code>; <code>PacketViolationWarningHandler</code> also names the rejected packet from <code>latestCodec</code>, which is fixed at startup. <code>ClientCameraAimAssistHandler</code>, <code>SetPlayerInventoryOptionsHandler</code> and <code>TickingAreasLoadStatusHandler</code> do nothing. <code>ClientCacheStatusHandler</code> and <code>ClientCacheBlobStatusHandler</code> call <code>setClientCacheEnabled</code> and <code>handleClientCacheBlobStatus</code>, which touch only the session&#39;s blob cache and send on its own connection. <code>SetDifficultyHandler</code> reads <code>config.getDifficulty</code> and answers on its own connection.</li><li>No <code>step.defer</code>, since a connection-lane handler has no input step. No service was missing from <code>HandlerContext.ts</code>, and the package edited no shared file.</li><li>For the close: <code>Lane.test.ts</code> still lets a connection-lane handler call <code>server.getLogger</code> and <code>server.getConfig</code> (<code>SERVER_MEMBERS</code>) and import <code>../../Server</code> (<code>CONNECTION_IMPORTS</code>). No session handler in the connection lane uses either now, but the join&#39;s handlers and <code>ResourcePackChunkRequestHandler</code> still take the server. Both allowances go with the shape that takes the server.</li><li>Found: <code>Server.test.ts</code>&#39; &quot;refreshes the process title every five seconds&quot; waits on the real clock and failed once in the full Prismarine run on a loaded host, then passed alone. It is tagged <code>performance</code>, which CI leaves out, so no issue was opened.</li><li>Checks at D2a&#39;s head, on program head <code>e671fe72cb</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code>, <code>pnpm run lint</code>, the protocol suite (395 files), <code>Lane.test.ts</code>, <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, and client e2e <code>spawn</code> pass. The Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passed 432 of 433 files (4376 tests), the one failure being the <code>performance</code> test above. In the <code>spawn</code> run the server logged <code>ClientCacheStatusHandler</code> and <code>ClientCacheBlobStatusHandler</code> handling the join&#39;s packets through the context.</li></ul><h5 id="d2b-the-read-lane" tabindex="-1">D2b: the read lane <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2b-the-read-lane" aria-label="Permalink to “D2b: the read lane”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Converted, all eight in the read lane: <code>AnvilDamage</code>, <code>BookEdit</code>, <code>BossEvent</code>, <code>LabTable</code>, <code>LecternUpdate</code>, <code>PlayerToggleCrafterSlotRequest</code>, <code>StructureBlockUpdate</code> and <code>StructureTemplateDataRequest</code>. Each implements <code>ReadLaneHandler</code>, declares <code>takes = &#39;context&#39;</code> and destructures <code>{ session, log }</code>. D2b&#39;s block of <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> is gone. No handler is in the input lane, so there is no <code>step.defer</code>.</li><li>The lane trace agrees with the declarations. Six read the sender&#39;s view position and one loaded chunk through <code>blockTargetRejection</code> and log; <code>BookEdit</code> reads the sender&#39;s inventory and logs; <code>BossEvent</code> replays a bar to the sender alone through <code>BossBar.answerQuery</code>. None writes and none answers anyone but its sender. The six write requests the server drops (<code>AnvilDamage</code>, <code>BookEdit</code>, <code>LabTable</code>, <code>LecternUpdate</code>, <code>PlayerToggleCrafterSlotRequest</code>, <code>StructureBlockUpdate</code>) move to the input lane when their writes exist.</li><li><code>BlockTarget.ts</code> did not change. Its <code>BlockTargetSender</code> was already <code>Pick&lt;PlayerSession, &#39;getViewPosition&#39; | &#39;getWorld&#39;&gt;</code>, and it reaches no server.</li><li><code>sessionSeeingBlocks</code> in <code>network/handler/blockTargetSession.test.helper.ts</code> now also returns a <code>SessionContext</code> over its session, whose log fills <code>logged</code>, and takes player members, so the <code>BookEdit</code> test uses it too. The shared <code>testing/</code> helpers were not edited.</li><li>The &quot;Checks to run&quot; item on rejecting an interaction with an unloaded chunk is confirmed by the new parity family <code>gameplay.block-targets</code>. <code>packages/parity/src/javaClassFile.ts</code> parses a class file from the pinned Java jar into each method&#39;s calls in bytecode order. Java 26.3&#39;s <code>ServerGamePacketListenerImpl.handlePickItemFromBlock</code> checks reach (<code>isWithinBlockInteractionRange</code>), then <code>ServerLevel.isLoaded</code>, then reads the block, and returns at the first check that fails; <code>updateSignText</code> checks <code>hasChunkAt</code> before <code>getBlockEntity</code>. <code>blockTargetRejection</code> makes the same checks in the same order. Against a real <code>World</code> with one chunk in memory, the six handlers drop a position in reach in an unloaded chunk before reading its block, and the provider is asked for no chunk. The reach bound itself differs and is not compared: Java measures from the eyes to the block&#39;s box with the interaction range plus one, and the server from the view position to the block&#39;s corner with the creative reach plus one. <code>handleUseItemOn</code> checks reach and <code>mayInteract</code> but not whether the chunk is loaded, so it is not part of the row.</li><li>Left for the D2 close: nothing. These handlers need no <code>HandlerContext</code> member, and the close can tick the &quot;Checks to run&quot; item.</li><li>Checks on program head <code>0b2c42f684</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code>, the parity suite (54 files, 312 tests), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> and client e2e <code>block-placement</code> pass. The Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passes except files that fail differently on each full run under load (<code>Server.test.ts</code>, <code>live.test.ts</code>, <code>blockStateAllocation.test.ts</code>); each passes when run alone. No replay digest changed.</li></ul><h5 id="d2c-relays-and-session-inputs" tabindex="-1">D2c: relays and session inputs <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2c-relays-and-session-inputs" aria-label="Permalink to “D2c: relays and session inputs”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Converted, with the lane each trace showed: <code>ActorEvent</code>, <code>Emote</code> and <code>PlayerSkin</code> (input, relays through <code>step.output</code>); <code>ContainerClose</code>, <code>Interact</code>, <code>RequestAbility</code>, <code>SetPlayerGameType</code>, <code>ShowCredits</code>, <code>ServerboundLoadingScreen</code> and <code>ResourcePackChunkRequest</code> (input); <code>Disconnect</code> (connection, moved from input).</li><li>Lane traces. Each input handler writes or shows something: the skin and the player list entry, the inventory, the crafting grid and dropped stacks, the window table, the flight state, the game mode, a world change, or a teardown. <code>ResourcePackChunkRequest</code> stays an input because its kick goes through <code>ClientConnection.disconnect</code>, which takes the player off directly and not through the held removal. <code>Disconnect</code> only logs and closes the transport, and the dispatcher holds the removal that the closed transport causes as an input of its own, behind the client&#39;s earlier inputs. That makes it a connection-lane handler, and it closes the <code>Disconnect</code> half of the last item under &quot;Checks to run&quot;. The goodbye no longer waits for an input step before the transport closes.</li><li><code>step.defer</code> calls, four in all: <ol><li><code>SetPlayerGameType</code>: <code>PlayerSession.setGamemode</code>, which awaits the send that stops block cracking before it writes the game mode. Slice 7.</li><li><code>ShowCredits</code>: <code>completeCredits</code>, a world change. Slice 9.</li><li><code>ServerboundLoadingScreen</code>: <code>completeDimensionChange</code>, which waits for the arrival&#39;s spawn and may start a queued world change. Slice 9.</li><li><code>ResourcePackChunkRequest</code>: the kick, whose teardown saves the player record and announces the departure in chat. Slice 9.</li></ol></li><li>Four writes stay in the handler&#39;s unit only because the method that makes them writes before its first await: <code>PlayerSession.setFlying</code> (<code>RequestAbility</code>), <code>openMainInventory</code>&#39;s window table entry (<code>Interact</code>), <code>releaseArrivalChunks</code>&#39; released flag (<code>ServerboundLoadingScreen</code>) and <code>World.dropContents</code> (<code>ContainerClose</code>). The handler tests stub these methods, so nothing fails if one of them gains an await before its write; the write would then leave the unit. Slice 7, which takes sends out of these methods, removes the risk.</li><li><code>ContainerClose</code> now empties the grid and drops the overflow before its reply settles rather than after. The writes are the same unit and the sends go out in the same order.</li><li>Left for the close, then done in D2e: <code>LevelSoundEventHandler</code> stayed on the shape that takes the server. It relays the client&#39;s own packet through <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.sendAround</code> for the sender&#39;s world, and <code>HandlerServices</code> has no replicator. It needs a member such as <code>replicators: Pick&lt;WorldReplicators, &#39;for&#39;&gt;</code>, or one narrowed to <code>sendAround</code>. Rebuilding the audience in the handler would copy <code>getViewers</code>, and <code>getProximityBroadcast</code> is not in <code>HandlerConfig</code> either.</li><li>Edited outside the handler files: one assertion in <code>Lane.test.ts</code>&#39; &quot;flags the world access it guards against&quot;, which read <code>SetPlayerGameType</code>&#39;s <code>server.getPermissionManager</code> call and now reads its <code>session.sendGamemode</code> call. No converted handler exercises the test&#39;s <code>Server</code> branch any more, so the close can delete that branch with the shape that takes the server. <code>PlayerSessionDimensionChange.test.ts</code> gained a <code>reportScreen</code> helper for its three loading screen calls; D2d edits the <code>PlayerAuthInputHandler</code> calls in the same file.</li><li>Checks at this package&#39;s head, on program head <code>e671fe72cb</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code>; the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (433 files, 4378 tests), the protocol suite (395 files), <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched, parity <code>protocol.dimension-change</code>, and client e2e <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code> and <code>title</code> pass.</li></ul><h5 id="d2d-movement-actions-and-inventory" tabindex="-1">D2d: movement, actions and inventory <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2d-movement-actions-and-inventory" aria-label="Permalink to “D2d: movement, actions and inventory”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Converted, all in the input lane, where each trace kept them: <code>InventoryTransaction</code>, <code>ItemStackRequest</code>, <code>MovePlayer</code>, <code>PlayerAction</code>, <code>PlayerAuthInput</code>, <code>RequestChunkRadius</code> and <code>Respawn</code>. Each writes the player, its inventory, its window, the world or the session&#39;s view distance. The D2d block of <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> is gone. <code>workstation.ts</code> takes the enchantment registry and the world&#39;s loaded blocks in place of <code>getServer()</code>. <code>tradeScreen.ts</code> and <code>eyeOfEnderEcho.ts</code> reached no server already; <code>InventoryTransaction</code> hands the echo <code>step.tick</code>.</li><li><code>PlayerAuthInput</code> applies a movement, flag actions, block actions, an item use and a stack request from one packet, each through the handler that owns it. Once one part defers, a part applied at once after it would take effect first, so <code>orderedParts</code> (<code>network/handler/inputParts.ts</code>) defers every later part whole behind it and runs the work a deferred part defers inside it. A block placed in the same input as a break still goes down after the break.</li><li><code>PlayerAction</code>&#39;s block read, the slice 6 entry in <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code>, is gone. A block action, and a break transaction with client-timed breaking, is dropped at verbose level when its chunk is not loaded, and the crack timing reads the block through <code>World.loadedBlockAt</code>. That is a behavior change: such a packet used to load or generate the chunk.</li><li><code>step.defer</code> calls, nine in all: <ol><li><code>PlayerAction</code> <code>StartDestroyBlock</code> and <code>ContinueDestroyBlock</code> with server-timed breaking: <code>World.startBlockCracking</code>, which reads the block through <code>getBlockState</code>. Slice 6.</li><li>The same actions with client-timed breaking: <code>World.hitBlock</code> and the crack event. Slice 6.</li><li><code>PlayerAction</code> <code>CrackBlock</code>: <code>World.hitBlock</code>. Slice 6.</li><li><code>PlayerAction</code> <code>PredictDestroyBlock</code>: <code>World.breakBlock</code>, which writes through <code>setBlockByName</code>. Slice 9.</li><li><code>PlayerAction</code> <code>CreativeDestroyBlock</code>: <code>World.breakBlock</code>. Slice 9.</li><li><code>PlayerAction</code> <code>Respawn</code>: <code>PlayerSession.respawn</code>, which asks the respawn search. Slice 9.</li><li><code>InventoryTransaction</code> <code>DESTROY</code> with client-timed breaking: <code>World.breakBlock</code>. Slice 9.</li><li><code>InventoryTransaction</code> melee: <code>meleeAttack</code>, which awaits its sounds before it says whether the blow landed, and the hunger depends on that. Slice 7.</li><li><code>Respawn</code> for a dead player: the respawn search and the change of world. Slice 9.</li></ol></li><li>Writes that happen after a wait, in an output, so outside the handler&#39;s unit: <code>refreshEnchantOptions</code> sets the window&#39;s <code>bookshelfPower</code> after it counts the shelves, and <code>PlayerSession.setViewDistance</code> starts the chunk streaming after its first send. Nothing reads either before the next input. Several outputs depend on a write that comes before the method&#39;s first await, as D2c noted for its four: <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, <code>setSprinting</code>, <code>setFlying</code>, <code>dropFromPlayer</code> and <code>loose</code>. Slice 7 removes the risk.</li><li><code>ItemStackRequest</code> drops its overflow stacks before the response is sent rather than after. The stacks are the same unit&#39;s writes, and nothing the response or the trade offers read changes with them.</li><li>Left for the close: the workstation input slots (<code>OpenBlockContainer.inputs</code>, written by <code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code>) are still not logged. Logging them as an <code>inventory-slot</code> container of the player needs a container name in <code>inventory/slotLog.ts</code>, a restore in the player systems&#39; <code>restoreSlot</code>, and the setter on <code>PlayerSession</code>&#39;s open window, all outside this package&#39;s files.</li><li>Edited outside the handler files: <code>world/inTickChunkLoads.ts</code> (the entry above), <code>contentPayloads.ts</code> (<code>joinContentPayloads</code> takes the three log levels it writes at), the <code>idleMovement</code> benchmark, and the callers in <code>entity/data/live.test.ts</code>, <code>inventory/blockContainer.test.ts</code> and <code>PlayerSessionDimensionChange.test.ts</code>. The tests that build a <code>Server</code> double reach the handlers through <code>testing/serverInputContext.test.helper.ts</code>, a new helper that reads each part from the double.</li><li>Checks at this package&#39;s head, on program head <code>0b2c42f684</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched. Client e2e <code>movement</code>, <code>jump</code>, <code>teleport</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code> and <code>death-respawn</code> pass. The Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passed 431 of 434 files (4380 tests) at load average 33 to 51. Its failures were the two <code>performance</code> tests in <code>Server.test.ts</code> and timeouts in <code>vanillaEntityRuntime.test.ts</code>, <code>live.test.ts</code> and <code>Server.test.ts</code>, and each of those files passes when run alone. The replay test timed out once while the suite ran beside it, and passed when run alone.</li></ul><h5 id="d2e-commands-chat-and-permissions" tabindex="-1">D2e: commands, chat and permissions <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2e-commands-chat-and-permissions" aria-label="Permalink to “D2e: commands, chat and permissions”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Converted, all input lane: <code>CommandRequest</code>, <code>SettingsCommand</code> and <code>Text</code> (a command or a chat line), <code>RequestPermissions</code> (an operator list change), <code>SetDefaultGameType</code> and <code>LevelSoundEvent</code>. Each writes or shows something to other players, so none moves lane. <code>LevelSoundEvent</code> is D2c&#39;s leftover: it relays through <code>WorldReplicators.for(world).sendAround</code>, which is synchronous, so it sends nothing through <code>step.output</code>.</li><li><code>HandlerServices</code> gained <code>permissions</code> (<code>isOp</code>, <code>setOpNow</code>, <code>can</code>), <code>defaults</code> (<code>setGamemode</code>), <code>commands</code> (<code>dispatchCommand</code>), <code>chat</code> (<code>send</code>) and <code>replicators</code> (<code>for</code>). <code>handlerContexts.ts</code> wires each as a getter or a bound method.</li><li>Memory first, disk after: <code>PermissionManager.setOpNow</code> runs <code>PlayerToggleOperatorEvent</code> and changes the operator list before it returns, then queues the <code>ops.json</code> write behind the writes queued earlier. <code>Config.setGamemode(gamemode, true)</code> changes the value at once and queues the file write. A failed write is logged, and the change in memory stays. <code>PermissionManager.setOp</code> keeps its contract on the same pieces, and now logs a failed write.</li><li><code>step.defer</code> calls, four in all: <ol><li><code>CommandRequest</code>: <code>dispatchCommand</code> and the <code>CommandOutputPacket</code> reply. A command can do anything. Slice 9, unless the command is synchronous by then.</li><li><code>SettingsCommand</code>: <code>dispatchCommand</code>. Slice 9, the same.</li><li><code>Text</code>: <code>ChatManager.send</code>, which runs the plugins&#39; chat listeners. Slice 9.</li><li><code>SetDefaultGameType</code>: <code>PlayerSession.setGamemode</code> for every session, which awaits its sends and writes players in other worlds, then the default&#39;s resend. Slice 9, where a change to another world travels as a message.</li></ol></li><li>Fixed: <code>PermissionManager.setOp</code> sent the target&#39;s command list before it changed the operator list, so a player made an operator got the commands of a non-operator and one who lost the status kept the operator commands until something resent the list. It sends after the change now. <code>PermissionManager.test.ts</code> records <code>isOp</code> at each <code>sendAvailableCommands</code> call.</li><li>Left for the D2 close: <code>HandlerContext.ts</code> and <code>handlerContexts.ts</code> now carry every member the converted handlers use. The <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> blocks of D2c and D2e are gone.</li></ul><h5 id="d2f-the-join" tabindex="-1">D2f: the join <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2f-the-join" aria-label="Permalink to “D2f: the join”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Converted, with the lane each trace showed: <code>RequestNetworkSettings</code>, <code>Login</code> and <code>ClientToServerHandshake</code> (connection, unchanged), and <code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code> and <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> (input, unchanged). The D2f block of <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> is gone, and <code>ConnectionPacketHandler.ts</code> with it.</li><li>The three handlers before a session implement <code>ConnectionHandler</code> and take a <code>ConnectionContext</code>, both in <code>network/handler/ConnectionContext.ts</code> with <code>takesConnection</code>. The context holds the connection, a logger, the four settings they read (<code>ConnectionConfig</code>) and <code>login</code>: the sessions, the ban list and <code>players</code>, a <code>JoinPreparation</code>. <code>network/connectionContexts.ts</code> builds it from the server, outside <code>network/handler/</code>, and the dispatcher hands it to a handler whose phases all precede the session.</li><li>B1&#39;s note: <code>JoinPreparation.prepare</code> reads the default world&#39;s spawn, builds the store player standing there, attaches its session and loads its record, in the connection layer in front of the join, as ADR 0014 places login I/O. Its result is a session whose player is in no world. That player enters its world as an input, when <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> adds it.</li><li>Lane traces. <code>RequestNetworkSettings</code> sets the codec and the compression and answers on its own connection. <code>Login</code> verifies the token, reserves and claims the account, kicks a duplicate and prepares the player; <code>ClientToServerHandshake</code> resumes that admission. The login reaches a world twice, through <code>login.players.prepare</code> and through the kick of a replaced session, so both stay in <code>KNOWN_WORLD_ACCESS</code>; neither runs inside a tick. <code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code> opens the play phase and raises <code>playerSpawn</code>, and <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> adds the player to its world, so both stay inputs.</li><li><code>step.defer</code> calls, four in all: <ol><li><code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code>, <code>Refused</code>: the kick, which saves the player&#39;s record and announces the departure in chat. Slices 8 and 9.</li><li><code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code>, <code>SendPacks</code>: the same kick. Slices 8 and 9.</li><li><code>ResourcePackClientResponse</code>, <code>Completed</code>: the join burst, which waits for the join world&#39;s spawn and ends with the join line in chat. Slice 9.</li><li><code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>: <code>World.addPlayer</code> and <code>completeSpawn</code> as one piece, so a failure in <code>completeSpawn</code> still undoes the entry. <code>World.addPlayer</code> writes before it returns and is asynchronous only in its signature; <code>completeSpawn</code> waits on sends before it writes the game mode. Slice 7.</li></ol></li><li><code>createStartGameData</code> takes the three settings and the tick it reads in place of the server, and <code>joinContentPayloads</code> takes only the logger levels it writes at.</li><li>Edited outside the handler files: <code>NetworkDispatcher.ts</code>, which builds the connection context; <code>PacketHandlerRegistry.ts</code>&#39; <code>RegisteredHandler</code> union; <code>Lane.test.ts</code>, which reads a <code>ConnectionContext</code>&#39;s <code>connection</code> as a <code>ClientConnection</code> and keeps the two <code>KNOWN_WORLD_ACCESS</code> entries with new reasons; the inbound latency benchmark&#39;s handler stub; and the parity family <code>protocol.negotiation</code>, which hands <code>RequestNetworkSettingsHandler</code> the context.</li><li>For the close: the <code>connectionContexts.ts</code> getters read the server&#39;s logger, config, session manager and ban manager when a handler uses them, as <code>handlerContexts.ts</code> does. <code>KNOWN_WORLD_ACCESS</code> keeps <code>LoginHandler</code> and <code>ClientToServerHandshakeHandler</code> until the duplicate kick is a message to the replaced session&#39;s world. <code>World.addPlayer</code> is asynchronous only in its signature; making it synchronous lets <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> add the player in the handler and defer only <code>completeSpawn</code>.</li><li>Checks at this package&#39;s head, on program head <code>0b2c42f684</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code>; <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> with no baseline touched; parity <code>protocol.negotiation</code>; and client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>duplicate-login</code> and <code>dimensions</code> pass. The Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passed 430 of 434 files (4375 tests) at load average 50 to 68, the four failures all timeouts. Alone with a longer timeout, <code>vanillaEntityRuntime.test.ts</code>, <code>bedrockSections.test.ts</code> and <code>registryBundle.test.ts</code> pass, and <code>Server.test.ts</code> fails only the <code>performance</code> test D2a&#39;s notes name.</li></ul><h5 id="d2g-the-client" tabindex="-1">D2g: the client <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2g-the-client" aria-label="Permalink to “D2g: the client”">​</a></h5><ul><li>The client&#39;s shape is <code>packages/client/src/net/ClientHandler.ts</code>. <code>ClientPacketHandler&lt;T&gt;</code> is <code>PacketHandler&lt;T, ClientContext, ClientPhase&gt;</code>, with no lane, since the client has no world tick. <code>ClientPhase</code> is <code>&#39;login&#39; | &#39;play&#39;</code>. <code>ClientContext</code> has the <code>session</code>, picked to <code>send</code>, <code>sendImmediate</code>, <code>enableCompression</code> and <code>enableEncryption</code>, and a <code>log</code> with the logger&#39;s levels. <code>PacketHandler.ts</code> needed no change.</li><li><code>ClientPacketRegistry.registerHandler(PacketClass, handler)</code> holds a handler under the class&#39;s name and returns a function that removes it. The handler runs for a packet of that name only when that class decoded it, so its payload type comes from the class: an <code>instanceof</code> check on the packet narrows the payload, with no cast and no reliance on an id table. A <code>DecodedPacket</code> now carries the <code>packet</code> that decoded it. <code>dispatch</code> runs the handlers that accept the phase in registration order, over a copy of the list as <code>EventEmitter</code> does, and one that throws does not stop the rest.</li><li><code>ClientSession</code> holds the phase, starting in <code>login</code>, and <code>registerHandler</code> delegates to the registry. It dispatches each packet after its <code>packet</code> event and before <code>packet:&lt;id&gt;</code>, counts what a handler returns in <code>isIdle</code> and <code>settled</code> until it settles, and logs a failed handler as a warning that names the packet.</li><li>Converted, each in its own commit: <code>Credits</code> handles <code>ShowCreditsPacket</code>. <code>DimensionChange</code> handles <code>ChangeDimensionPacket</code> and <code>LevelChunkPacket</code>. <code>LoginSequence</code> handles <code>NetworkSettingsPacket</code>, <code>ServerToClientHandshakePacket</code>, <code>PlayStatusPacket</code>, <code>StartGamePacket</code>, <code>DisconnectPacket</code>, <code>ResourcePacksInfoPacket</code> and <code>ResourcePackStackPacket</code>, registered when <code>run</code> starts and removed when the login settles. A step that throws still fails the login. After it sends <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> it moves the session to <code>play</code>.</li><li>Phases: the login&#39;s steps accept <code>login</code> only. <code>Credits</code> and <code>DimensionChange</code> accept both, because they listened to every packet from the moment <code>Client</code> was built, and a change can arrive while the spawn answer is still being sent, before the session reaches <code>play</code>.</li><li>What changed in behavior: the listeners tracked a promise for every packet the session read, and now only a packet with a handler starts work. The <code>LevelChunk</code> handler returns nothing when no change is pending, so a chunk leaves nothing to wait on. The order of sends is unchanged, and <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched.</li><li>The public API change, in the <code>minor</code> Changeset <code>client-packet-handlers.md</code>: the new registration, phase and context, <code>DecodedPacket.packet</code>, and constructors without a logger (<code>new LoginSequence(session, auth, options)</code>, <code>new DimensionChange(session, runtimeEntityId)</code>, <code>new Credits(session, runtimeEntityId)</code>). Emitting <code>packet</code> on a session by hand no longer drives the responders.</li><li>For the D2 close: <code>isPacket</code> and <code>ClientboundPacketData</code> stay. They narrow on the id alone, and the readers left watch the <code>packet</code> event: <code>cli/commands.ts</code>, which prints <code>Text</code>, and <code>TeleportChunks.test.ts</code>, both outside this package&#39;s row, and the real-server trip in <code>DimensionChange.test.ts</code>, which reads the whole sequence back in arrival order. Once those register handlers or narrow with <code>decoded.packet instanceof</code>, both can go.</li><li>Found: the published declarations used relative imports without an extension, so a consumer that resolves with <code>NodeNext</code> got no members from any package&#39;s root (#2985). It predated this package, and program head carries the fix.</li><li>Checks at D2g&#39;s head: <code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The client suite passes, 14 files and 83 tests, its NetherNet tests included on that run (#2983 failed the four of them on the baseline run at <code>e671fe72cb</code>). <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes in the suite and alone. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>dimensions</code> and <code>ender-dragon</code> pass. The packed client and its workspace dependencies install in a scratch project, where a consumer that registers a handler typechecks with TypeScript 5.9.3 and <code>skipLibCheck</code> off under both <code>NodeNext</code> and <code>Bundler</code>, and a handler of the wrong payload is rejected. Rerun on program head <code>0b2c42f684</code>: the build, typecheck, both lint gates, the client suite (14 files, 83 tests) and the three e2e scenarios pass.</li></ul><h5 id="d2-close" tabindex="-1">D2 close <a class="header-anchor" href="#d2-close" aria-label="Permalink to “D2 close”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Deleted: <code>network/handler/PacketHandler.ts</code>, the shape that took the server, <code>handle(packet, server, session)</code>. With it went the <code>takes</code> field of every handler and of <code>ConnectionLaneHandler</code>, <code>ReadLaneHandler</code> and <code>InputLaneHandler</code>, <code>takesContext</code>, and the second path in <code>NetworkDispatcher</code> and in <code>appendHandler</code>. The dispatcher hands a session&#39;s handler to <code>HandlerContexts.run</code> and nothing else. No plugin or other caller registered a handler in the old shape: <code>git grep</code> found <code>appendHandler</code> and <code>registerHandler</code> only in <code>PacketHandlerRegistry</code> and its test.</li><li><code>PacketHandlerRegistry.appendHandler</code> takes and returns <code>SessionHandler</code>s. The merged handler runs in the first handler&#39;s phases and the stricter of the two lanes. An input-lane merge returns nothing to wait for, so a part that returns a promise has it handed to the step&#39;s outputs. A merge of connection or read parts is awaited part by part. It no longer takes the server, which is how a merged handler used to build its parts&#39; contexts, so a merge now runs in the lane it declares and not as one asynchronous input.</li><li><code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> has no allowlist and asserts that no module under <code>network/handler/</code> imports <code>Server</code> or calls <code>getServer()</code>, which meets the slice 6 target &quot;No <code>Server</code> import in a handler&quot;. <code>Lane.test.ts</code> lost <code>SERVER_MEMBERS</code>, the <code>Server</code> entry of <code>CONNECTION_IMPORTS</code> and its parameter kind, and the check for a call on a <code>Server</code>. The other import patterns stay, since they guard a connection-lane handler against the world. <code>KNOWN_WORLD_ACCESS</code> keeps <code>LoginHandler</code> and <code>ClientToServerHandshakeHandler</code>, which still reach the world as the test shows.</li><li>The <code>NetworkDispatcher</code> tests registered doubles in the old shape. Those in the input lane now register a handler for a session in the <code>play</code> phase, because an input-lane double in a connection phase is a pair that production cannot have, and the harness awaits an input double through the step&#39;s outputs so the ordering tests still wait for it.</li><li>The <code>step.defer</code> calls are counted in <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> through <code>reportProgress</code>: 23 calls in 14 handler modules at this head. That is the work slice 9 has left, and the number is not gated. It is more than D2d&#39;s nine because the other packages deferred a world change, a command, a chat line or a disk write too.</li><li>The client lost <code>isPacket</code> and <code>ClientboundPacketData</code>. The CLI&#39;s <code>connect</code> command registers a <code>TextPacket</code> handler, <code>TeleportChunks.test.ts</code> registers three, and the portal trip in <code>DimensionChange.test.ts</code> reads the whole sequence back through a local <code>payloadOf</code>, which narrows <code>decoded.packet</code> with <code>instanceof</code> and reads <code>getPacketData()</code>.</li><li>Not done: the workstation input slots (<code>OpenBlockContainer.inputs</code>) are still not logged. A logged slot needs a place in the snapshot, because &quot;a snapshot plus the log reproduces the replay digest&quot; (slice 7) replays the log onto a snapshot, and the player record keeps no workstation slots: the window is session state, nothing logs that it opened or closed, and <code>inputs</code> is a plain array that 18 lines in <code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code>, <code>workstation.ts</code>, <code>tradeScreen.ts</code> and <code>ContainerCloseHandler</code> index directly. Logging them would take a class like <code>CraftingInput</code> owned by the store player, a window that borrows it, and a rule for an undo that reaches a window already closed. Either the digest or the snapshot would then carry state they do not carry now, so this is not a change that leaves digests alone. It needs a decision about whether a workstation window is player state, which goes with D4&#39;s replica and slice 7&#39;s snapshot, and it stays where D3 left it.</li><li>Changesets: <code>minor</code> for <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code> (the old shape was public through its <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> export) and for <code>@jsprismarine/client</code> (<code>isPacket</code> and <code>ClientboundPacketData</code> were public through its <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> export). Both packages are below <code>1.0.0</code>, where a breaking change is <code>minor</code>.</li><li>Checks at this head, on program head <code>204b9acc66</code>: <code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The Prismarine, client, parity and protocol handler suites pass (504 files, 4807 tests) apart from two NetherNet tests in <code>Login.test.ts</code> (#2983), which pass alone (11 tests); one run before it also failed three tests, among them <code>enchantingTable.test.ts</code>&#39;s seed test, which passes alone and on program head. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>movement</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>dimensions</code>, <code>villager-trade</code> and <code>death-respawn</code> pass. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched.</li></ul><p>D3, the logged block writes, left these for D4 and later packages.</p><ul><li>Every block write in a loaded chunk goes through <code>World.writeBlockRuntimeId</code>, which now appends a <code>block</code> field entry with the runtime id before and after it. The paths that reach it are <code>setBlockRuntimeId</code>, <code>setBlockByName</code>, <code>setLoadedBlockRuntimeId</code> and <code>setLoadedBlockState</code>. Physics, the engine systems through <code>EngineWorld</code> (fluids, fire, redstone, blasts), the block behaviors through <code>worldBlockStorage</code> (writes, piston moves and destruction), commands, the dragon fight&#39;s features, item interactions and a cooking station&#39;s lit swap all use them. No code outside world generation and chunk loading writes a loaded chunk around it. Layer 1 is never written by the world today; the entry kind carries the layer for when it is.</li><li>Every block entity write goes through <code>writeBlockEntity</code>, which hands <code>World.blockEntityChanged</code> the tags before the write, and every removal happens inside a block write. That covers content records (<code>setBlockData</code>), chest and furnace slots through <code>openContainerSlots</code>, loot fills, a piston&#39;s carried block entity, cooking stations and spawners. A block entity is logged as <code>block-entity</code> field entries addressed by its block position (<code>blockentity/blockEntityLog.ts</code>): <code>data</code> holds every tag but the position and the stacks, with <code>Items</code> kept as an empty list so the tags keep their order, and <code>items.&lt;slot&gt;</code> holds one stack. A value is the field&#39;s tags as network NBT bytes, or null. A block entity appears with <code>data</code> first and leaves with <code>data</code> last, and a block write logs the removal of its block entity before the block, so an undo, which runs newest first, always finds what it writes into. An <code>Items</code> list whose stacks do not each name a distinct slot stays whole in <code>data</code>.</li><li>A workstation that is a block entity (furnace, blast furnace, smoker, chest, barrel, hopper, shulker box) keeps its slots in <code>Items</code>, so they are <code>items.&lt;slot&gt;</code> fields. The enchanting table, anvil, stonecutter, smithing table and trade screen keep their input slots on the player&#39;s open window (<code>OpenBlockContainer.inputs</code>), because two players at one table each have their own, and <code>ItemStackRequestHandler</code> writes them. They are player state, not block state, so they are not block entity fields. They are not logged yet. The handler is D1&#39;s and D2&#39;s, and the natural home is an <code>inventory-slot</code> container of the player, as the cursor and the crafting grid are.</li><li>The log stays in <code>EntityStore</code>, which is per dimension and so per world. The world registers its inverse writer and its block update journal on the store when its first chunk enters, which is before any write into it and before any unit that could make one. A world-owned name for the log is a rename D4 can make if replication wants one; nothing depends on where it lives.</li><li>An undo writes the chunk without logging (<code>Chunk.restoreBlockRuntimeId</code>, which keeps the block entity, and <code>Chunk.restoreBlockEntity</code>, which files a block entity back in the place it held, since the chunk serializes them in that order and the digest reads it). It tells light, the portal index and the clients, as any block change does, and runs no block behavior and schedules no neighbor updates. The block update queue is in the replay digest, so <code>BlockUpdateScheduler</code> is a <code>UnitJournal</code>: the store marks it when a unit starts and rolls it back with the unit&#39;s span.</li><li>Not undone yet: scheduled block and fluid ticks that a write&#39;s neighbors queue (<code>@jsprismarine/engine-systems</code>&#39; tick lists) and the light queue. Both are in the digest, so a unit that throws after a write whose neighbors scheduled a tick, or whose light changed, does not return the digest to where it was. The proxy tests use writes that touch neither. Block updates, world systems, cooking stations, spawners and the dragon fight run outside units, so a throw there keeps its writes, as before; ADR 0015 makes each block update a unit, which is still to do.</li><li>Writes between ticks, such as a command from a tick function, are counted as unlogged, as C1c set out. A chunk entering the world is still one chunk entry and carries no block entries.</li><li>Proxy tests: <code>WorldChangeTick.test.ts</code> checks that N block writes append exactly N entries, that an undone unit of block writes, of block entity changes and a removal, or of a placement leaves the replay digest unchanged, that the engine systems&#39; write and a piston push are logged, and that undoing a second push restores what the first left. <code>inventory/blockContainer.test.ts</code> drives a real world: a stack moved into a chest is one <code>items.&lt;slot&gt;</code> entry, a lit furnace is one <code>data</code> entry a tick, and a world with an idle chest and furnace appends no block entries over twenty ticks.</li><li>Counts: <code>ChangeLogCost</code> reports <code>blockEntries</code> and <code>blockBytes</code>, and <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> reports them (non-gating). The scripted replay session places no blocks, so its 19 sampled ticks log 19 entries and 2212 bytes in all and no block entries. A replay scenario that builds and mines would give D4 a block figure.</li><li>For D4: <ol><li>Replication can send <code>UpdateBlock</code> from <code>block</code> entries and <code>BlockActorData</code> from <code>block-entity</code> entries, folding <code>data</code> and the <code>items.&lt;slot&gt;</code> fields back into one compound with <code>withBlockEntityField</code>, which is the form <code>BlockActorData</code> carries. The log&#39;s order already puts a placement&#39;s block before its block entity, which the client needs.</li><li><code>WorldChangeSink</code>&#39;s <code>block-set</code> and <code>block-entity-set</code> still go out for every write and every undo. D4 deletes them once it reads the log.</li><li>A lit furnace appends one <code>data</code> entry a tick, and a spawner near a player one each tick its delay counts down. Each carries the block entity&#39;s tags without its stacks, before and after.</li><li>A change that only reorders an <code>Items</code> list logs nothing, though the chunk&#39;s bytes change. Every writer in this server keeps stacks in slot order.</li></ol></li><li>Checks at D3&#39;s head, on program head <code>d2d36c1c2f</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, the Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> (431 files, 4362 tests), the block-behaviors (27 files) and engine-systems (8 files) suites, <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, the whole parity suite (53 files, the block and redstone families among them), and client e2e <code>block-placement</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>furnace</code>, <code>lit-furnace</code>, <code>farmland</code>, <code>edge-chunk-block-change</code> and <code>sub-chunk-block-changes</code> pass. No replay digest changed.</li></ul><h4 id="d4-replication-reads-the-change-log" tabindex="-1">D4: replication reads the change log <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4-replication-reads-the-change-log" aria-label="Permalink to “D4: replication reads the change log”">​</a></h4><p>D4 is the rest of slice 7: replication reads each sealed tick of the change log, keeps a replica, computes each change&#39;s audience once and encodes once for each codec and compression group, and the simulation stops sending. It splits into six packages. D4a comes first, because every other package reads the log through what it builds. D4b and D4d then run side by side, D4c and D4e run side by side after them, and D4f closes the slice.</p><p>Where things stand at program head <code>204b9acc66</code>:</p><ul><li>The log is complete for blocks, block entities, entity fields, inventory slots and membership (D3, C1c, C4c). Writes between ticks are counted as unlogged and are not in it.</li><li>The replicator (<code>network/NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code>) is a <code>WorldChangeSink</code>. The world pushes <code>block-set</code> and <code>block-entity-set</code> through <code>worldChanged</code> for every write and every undo, and the replicator collects them and sends them in the tick&#39;s flush phases, which run before the tick&#39;s log is sealed.</li><li>Entities reach clients through <code>StoreActors.sync</code>, which compares each actor with what it last sent: health, equipment (one equipment set built per store player a tick), the owner, the metadata inputs, the synced properties and the pose. <code>showPlayerState</code> throttles breath with <code>breathWorthSending</code>. A first sight calls <code>spawnTo</code> once for each viewer, which reads the live entity and encodes for that viewer.</li><li>Transient facts (sounds, particles, level events, actor events) are sink methods that send at once. Handlers relay through <code>step.output</code>, and <code>PlayerSession</code> sends to its own client and awaits the send in the middle of simulation code. 184 lines of <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> await something.</li><li><code>broadcastSharedBatches</code> and the block flush already encode once for each codec and compression group. <code>spawnTo</code>, <code>sendAddPlayer</code> and the per-session sends do not.</li></ul><p>The slice 7 items that earlier packages left, and the package that takes each:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>From</th><th>Package</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>UpdateBlock</code> from <code>block</code> entries, <code>BlockActorData</code> from <code>block-entity</code> entries folded with <code>withBlockEntityField</code>, and <code>WorldChangeSink</code>&#39;s <code>block-set</code> and <code>block-entity-set</code> deleted</td><td>D3&#39;s &quot;For D4&quot; 1 and 2</td><td>D4a</td></tr><tr><td>A lit furnace&#39;s and a spawner&#39;s <code>data</code> entry each tick, and an <code>Items</code> reorder that logs nothing</td><td>D3&#39;s &quot;For D4&quot; 3 and 4</td><td>D4a (sent as before; the reorder stays unlogged, as D3 found no writer that makes one)</td></tr><tr><td><code>StoreActors.sync</code>&#39;s comparisons and the per-tick equipment comparison</td><td>C3, C4g, C4h</td><td>D4c, over D4b&#39;s replica</td></tr><tr><td>The breath send, its throttle and the player state metadata</td><td>C4d, C4h item 15</td><td>D4c</td></tr><tr><td>The motion send for an <code>impulse</code> occurrence</td><td>C3, C4b</td><td>D4d</td></tr><tr><td>The status effect clock and the actor record tags (<code>persistentNbt</code>), which the actor keeps outside the store</td><td>C3</td><td>D4b gives them logged homes</td></tr><tr><td>Merchant offers, saved and hashed but not logged, because a trade changes the <code>Merchant</code> in place</td><td>C2</td><td>D4b logs them, D4e sends them to the trader from the log</td></tr><tr><td>A stack network id after an undo: the slot has to be sent again</td><td>C4c</td><td>D4e</td></tr><tr><td><code>step.defer</code> 1 of D2c: <code>SetPlayerGameType</code>, <code>PlayerSession.setGamemode</code></td><td>D2c</td><td>D4e</td></tr><tr><td><code>step.defer</code> 8 of D2d: <code>InventoryTransaction</code> melee, <code>meleeAttack</code> awaits its sounds</td><td>D2d</td><td>D4e, after D4d makes the sounds occurrences</td></tr><tr><td><code>step.defer</code> 4 of D2f: <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>, <code>World.addPlayer</code> and <code>completeSpawn</code></td><td>D2f</td><td>D4e</td></tr><tr><td>Writes that stay in a handler&#39;s unit only because they come before the method&#39;s first <code>await</code>: <code>setFlying</code>, <code>openMainInventory</code>, <code>releaseArrivalChunks</code>, <code>dropContents</code>, <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, <code>setSprinting</code>, <code>dropFromPlayer</code>, <code>loose</code></td><td>D2c, D2d</td><td>D4e removes the sends those methods await</td></tr><tr><td>Advancement triggers read from the log keep their timing (<code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code>)</td><td>C3, &quot;Checks to run&quot;</td><td>D4d</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Encode calls for one broadcast occurrence equal the number of codec and compression groups&quot;</td><td>Targets</td><td>D4d for occurrences, D4c for first sights</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Replication visits entities in proportion to log entries, not to the number of entities&quot;</td><td>Targets</td><td>D4a for blocks and block entities, D4c for entities</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A snapshot plus the log reproduces the replay digest&quot;</td><td>Targets</td><td>D4f</td></tr><tr><td>Outbound packets count toward the tick that produced them</td><td>Slice 7, ADR 0006</td><td>D4f</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Files</th><th>What is not mechanical</th><th>Gate beyond the unit tests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D4a the block replica</td><td>D3</td><td>New <code>network/replica/BlockReplica.ts</code>; the block and block entity parts of <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code> and <code>WorldChangeSink.ts</code>; <code>WorldChange.ts</code>, deleted; in <code>World.ts</code>, <code>update</code> and the block write, undo, resend and chunk paths; <code>EntityStore.ts</code>&#39;s handling of writes between ticks; a <code>block-shown</code> occurrence in <code>ChangeEntry.ts</code></td><td>A block entity&#39;s compound is folded from its fields, and the fields a tick did not change come from the replica, which takes a chunk&#39;s block entities when the chunk enters (the chunk&#39;s content arrives as an input under ADR 0012, so this is the input and not a read of the simulation). The seal moves ahead of the flush phases, so the flushes read a sealed tick. Block writes between ticks, such as a command from a tick function, are logged at the start of the next tick, which is when their packets went out before. <code>resendBlock</code> changes no state and becomes an occurrence. An undone unit&#39;s writes leave the log, so they send nothing</td><td>Proxy tests: reading a tick folds only the block entities the tick&#39;s entries name, whatever number the replica holds; a <code>BlockActorData</code> carries the bytes the block entity&#39;s own <code>toNBT</code> gives; a unit of block writes that throws sends nothing; a block write between ticks is sent in the next tick&#39;s flush. Replica entries and bytes through <code>reportProgress</code>. Replay digests; parity <code>blocks.behavior</code>, <code>integration.block-behaviors</code>; client e2e <code>block-placement</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>furnace</code>, <code>lit-furnace</code>, <code>farmland</code>, <code>edge-chunk-block-change</code>, <code>sub-chunk-block-changes</code></td></tr><tr><td>D4b the entity replica</td><td>D4a</td><td>New <code>network/replica/EntityReplica.ts</code>; the metadata projection helpers moved out of <code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code> into a module the actor and the replica share; the status effect clock and <code>persistentNbt</code> in <code>storeActors.ts</code>; merchant offers in <code>entity/data/trading.ts</code></td><td>Every field a client is shown needs a logged home before the replica can be built from the log alone: the status effect clock, the actor record tags and the offers are not logged today. The replica is folded from a membership entry&#39;s saved form and the entity and slot entries after it, so the projection has to run from plain data. Logging the offers changes no state, so no digest moves</td><td>Proxy test: for every entity in the replay scenarios, the replica folded from the log equals the projection the actor makes from live state, tick by tick. Replica entries and bytes through <code>reportProgress</code>. Replay digests; parity <code>entities.addactor</code>, <code>gameplay.brain-effects</code></td></tr><tr><td>D4c entity replication</td><td>D4b</td><td><code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/storePlayers.ts</code>, <code>world/ShownActor.ts</code>, the entity parts of <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code> and <code>WorldChangeSink.ts</code> (<code>reconcile</code>, <code>entityMoved</code>, <code>flushMovement</code>, <code>entityAdded</code>, <code>entityRemoved</code>, the attribute, metadata, equipment and status effect changes), the tracking phase in <code>World.ts</code>, and <code>PlayerSession.sendAddPlayer</code></td><td><code>StoreActors.sync</code> and its comparisons go: moves come from position entries, health and metadata from the replica, equipment from <code>inventory-slot</code> entries, a removal from a leave entry. A first sight is answered from the replica and encoded once for each group, so <code>spawnTo</code> stops reading the live entity. Breath goes out from <code>vitals.air</code> entries with the same throttle. The tracking phase moves after the seal. Entity writes between ticks are carried into the next tick as D4a carries block writes</td><td>Proxy tests: with N entities and k of them written in a tick, replication visits k; a first sight to V viewers in G groups encodes G times. Replay digests; parity <code>entities.addactor</code>, <code>integration.client-packets</code>; client e2e <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>movement-rotation</code>, <code>look-direction</code>, <code>gait</code>, <code>summon-entity</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>item-drop-pickup</code>, <code>natural-spawn-placement</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code></td></tr><tr><td>D4d occurrences</td><td>D4a</td><td><code>world/changelog/ChangeEntry.ts</code>&#39;s occurrence kinds; the transient members of <code>WorldChangeSink.ts</code> and their parts of <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code> (<code>blockSound</code>, <code>namedSound</code>, <code>namedParticle</code>, <code>worldEffect</code>, <code>actorEffect</code>, <code>actorSound</code>); the methods in <code>World.ts</code> that call them; the relaying handlers (<code>Animate</code>, <code>ActorEvent</code>, <code>Emote</code>, <code>LevelSoundEvent</code>); <code>entity/data/live.ts</code>&#39; impulse send</td><td>Each transient fact becomes an occurrence that replication encodes once for each group, after the seal. An occurrence in a unit that throws is removed with it, so a sound for an undone action no longer plays. Advancement triggers that observers read from the log have to fire at the same point as the inline triggers did</td><td>Proxy test: one broadcast occurrence to V viewers in G groups encodes G times. Parity <code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code>; client e2e <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>player-knockback</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>block-placement</code></td></tr><tr><td>D4e the owner&#39;s view</td><td>D4b, D4d</td><td><code>network/PlayerSession.ts</code>; the three handlers with slice 7 <code>step.defer</code> calls (<code>SetPlayerGameType</code>, <code>InventoryTransaction</code>, <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code>); the inventory and trade screen sends</td><td>The simulation stops awaiting sends to the player&#39;s own client. Slots reach their owner from <code>inventory-slot</code> entries, and a slot whose write was undone is sent again, because the stack&#39;s network id changed. The trader is sent offers from the log. The three deferred pieces run in the handler</td><td>The <code>step.defer</code> count lowered by three, and the awaits reachable from <code>World.update</code> through <code>reportProgress</code>. Replay digests; client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>teleport</code></td></tr><tr><td>D4f the close</td><td>D4c, D4e</td><td><code>WorldChangeSink.ts</code> reduced to the sealed tick and what is left; the replay recorder; a snapshot test beside <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>; the docs the slice invalidates</td><td>A packet replication sends counts toward the sealed tick it read, not the tick the transport framed it in (ADR 0006). A snapshot plus the log reproduces the replay digest only if everything the digest hashes is logged or held in the snapshot: D3 found that scheduled block and fluid ticks, the light queue and the block update queue are hashed and not logged, so this package logs them or puts them in the snapshot and says which</td><td>Everything in the gate for each slice, the full client e2e suite, and the three targets gating</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Rules that keep the packages apart:</p><ul><li>D4a lands first. The others build on its sealed-tick reader, its <code>network/replica/</code> module and its carrying of writes between ticks into the next tick.</li><li><code>NetworkWorldReplicator.ts</code>, <code>WorldChangeSink.ts</code> and <code>World.ts</code> are shared by section: D4c owns the entity members and the tracking phase, D4d the transient members and the methods that call them. Neither moves the other&#39;s members.</li><li>D4c owns <code>storeActors.ts</code> and <code>storePlayers.ts</code>; D4e owns <code>PlayerSession.ts</code>. <code>sendAddPlayer</code> is D4c&#39;s, and D4e does not edit it.</li><li>No D4 package edits the D2 close&#39;s files (<code>PacketHandler.ts</code>, <code>SessionHandler.ts</code>, <code>handlerContexts.ts</code>, <code>NetworkDispatcher.ts</code>, <code>PacketHandlerRegistry.ts</code>, <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code>, <code>Lane.test.ts</code>) until the close has landed.</li><li>Each package adds its own Changeset. The sink is public through Prismarine&#39;s <code>&quot;./*&quot;</code> export, so a package that changes its members adds a <code>minor</code> one.</li></ul><h5 id="d4a-the-block-replica" tabindex="-1">D4a: the block replica <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4a-the-block-replica" aria-label="Permalink to “D4a: the block replica”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Blocks reach clients only from the log. <code>WorldChangeSink.worldChanged</code> and <code>world/WorldChange.ts</code> are gone. A world hands its sink each sealed tick through <code>tickSealed</code>, from <code>EntityStore.onSealed</code>, which <code>World.blockLog</code> installs, and <code>World.update</code> calls it so the sink reads every tick. The sink also hears <code>chunkEntered(x, z, blockEntities)</code> and <code>chunkLeft(x, z)</code> from <code>enterChunk</code> and the unload.</li><li><code>network/replica/BlockReplica.ts</code> holds the logged fields of every block entity in the loaded chunks, as network NBT bytes by field. <code>chunkEntered</code> replaces a chunk&#39;s record with the fields of the block entities it arrived with. <code>read(sealed)</code> notes each block&#39;s last runtime id (layer 0 only, the one the world writes) and writes each <code>block-entity</code> field into the replica, passing over a field in a chunk it does not hold. <code>compound(at)</code> folds <code>data</code> and the <code>items.&lt;slot&gt;</code> fields with <code>withBlockEntityField</code> and passes the result through <code>BlockEntityRegistry.fromNBT(...).toNBT()</code>, so the tags come out in the order the block entity&#39;s own <code>toNBT</code> writes them, which is the order clients were sent before. <code>NetworkWorldReplicator</code> keeps one replica per world and collects the read into the same pending maps the flushes used, so the flushes are unchanged.</li><li>The sink is not handed the chunks loaded before it was attached. The level attaches it straight after it builds the world (<code>Level.ts</code>, <code>onWorldBuilt</code>), before any chunk loads.</li><li>The seal moved. <code>World.update</code> seals after the tracking phase and then runs <code>replicateTick</code>: the block changes, the block entities, the movement, the time, the chunk unloads and the outbound flush, in the order they ran before. A tick that throws is sealed and sends nothing, and the replicator keeps what it read for the next flush, as its pending maps did before.</li><li><code>EntityStore</code> holds <code>block</code> and <code>block-entity</code> entries and <code>block-shown</code> occurrences made between ticks and appends them when the next tick opens, ahead of that tick&#39;s own entries (<code>isBlockEntry</code> in <code>ChangeEntry.ts</code> names them). They are no longer counted as unlogged. Other writes between ticks still are, which D4c changes for entities.</li><li><code>World.resendBlock</code> logs a <code>block-shown</code> occurrence carrying the runtime id it shows, since the replica keeps no blocks.</li><li>What changed on the wire: a unit that throws and is undone no longer sends the blocks it restored, because its entries leave the log. The writes it undid were never sent, since the flush runs after the tick. Nothing else changed: the scripted replay&#39;s digests are unchanged and the eight block scenarios pass.</li><li>Proxy tests. <code>network/replica/BlockReplica.test.ts</code>: holding 1 or 1000 block entities, a tick of three entries (one slot and two blocks) reads 3 entries and folds 1 compound, so the work follows the log and not the replica&#39;s size. <code>world/WorldChangeSink.test.ts</code>, over a real world and store: a block write reaches the sink in the tick that made it, a unit of two block writes that throws hands the sink nothing, a write between ticks arrives with the next tick, a resend is a <code>block-shown</code> occurrence, a chunk&#39;s block entities are handed over as it enters, and a chest folded from its fields gives the bytes of the chunk&#39;s own <code>toNBT</code>. <code>WorldTickPhases.test.ts</code> checks that the seal comes after <code>reconcile</code> and before <code>flushBlockChanges</code>. <code>EntityStore.test.ts</code> checks the carrying and that an entity write between ticks is still counted as unlogged.</li><li><code>testing/sealedBlocks.test.helper.ts</code> builds block entries, sealed ticks of them, a chest&#39;s tags, a sink that reads into a replica (<code>replicaSink</code>) and <code>sealBlockKinds</code> for a store-backed world double. The replicator tests, the cooking, spawner, break, interaction and block entity tests read the log through them. The world doubles built on the prototype now set <code>changes</code>, which the constructor would have.</li><li>Counts: <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.blockReplicaCounts()</code> reports the block entities held, their bytes, the entries read and the compounds folded, and <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> reports them (non-gating). The scripted replay holds 0 block entities, reads 1 block entry and folds none. The entry is the water block its script places from the server&#39;s <code>tick</code> event, between ticks, which D3 counted as unlogged and the next tick now logs.</li><li>For the later packages: the transient sink members (<code>blockSound</code>, <code>namedSound</code>, <code>namedParticle</code>, <code>worldEffect</code>, <code>actorEffect</code>, <code>actorSound</code>) and the entity members are unchanged. <code>replicateTick</code> is where D4c moves the tracking phase. The block entity flush still sends one <code>BlockActorData</code> per position through <code>sendAround</code>; D4d&#39;s encode-once count can take it with the occurrences.</li><li>Checks at D4a&#39;s head, on program head <code>204b9acc66</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The Prismarine, block-behaviors, engine-systems and parity suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> pass together, 525 files and 5272 tests, at load average 9 to 13. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched. Client e2e <code>block-placement</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>furnace</code>, <code>lit-furnace</code>, <code>farmland</code>, <code>edge-chunk-block-change</code> and <code>sub-chunk-block-changes</code> pass.</li></ul><h5 id="d4b-the-entity-replica" tabindex="-1">D4b: the entity replica <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4b-the-entity-replica" aria-label="Permalink to “D4b: the entity replica”">​</a></h5><ul><li><code>network/replica/EntityReplica.ts</code> holds every entity of a world as plain data folded from the log. A membership <code>enter</code> entry starts an entity from the saved form it carries (<code>entity/data/savedForm.ts</code> reads the form as logged fields, named the way field entries address them), each <code>entity</code> and <code>inventory-slot</code> entry after it writes one field or slot over that form, and a <code>leave</code> entry drops it. An entry for an entity the replica does not hold is passed over, which is what happens to an entity that entered between ticks, because its entering entry is not logged. <code>NetworkWorldReplicator.tickSealed</code> reads each sealed tick into one replica per world, after the blocks.</li><li><code>EntityReplica.view</code> answers an <code>EntityView</code> (<code>entity/data/actorView.ts</code>): the pose, velocity, <code>on_ground</code>, the metadata projection, health with its maximum, the synced properties, the status effects and the equipment. <code>entityViewOf</code> builds the same view from a live entity. The metadata projection is split in <code>actorMetadata.ts</code> into what a component set decides (<code>componentMetadataFacts</code>, over a <code>ComponentView</code>) and what the entity&#39;s state decides (<code>ActorMetadataState</code>, plain values), joined by <code>projectActorMetadata</code>. The replica resolves a component set from the entity&#39;s type and its logged <code>groups</code>, base components first and then each group in order, as <code>EntityInstance</code> does, and caches one view per type and group list. <code>@jsprismarine/entity-goals</code>&#39; <code>navigationProfile</code> takes any <code>NavigationSource</code> for this. <code>healthAttributesOf</code> moved out of <code>storeActors.ts</code> into <code>actorView.ts</code>.</li><li>Logged homes. The status effect clock is the body field <code>statusEffectClock</code>, stored as its reading at one update (<code>{ age, tick }</code>) and written at an entity&#39;s first update and after a gap in its updates, so a steady entity logs nothing for it; a new entity starts the clock again, as a new store actor did. The actor record tags are the body field <code>actorTags</code>, network NBT bytes (<code>nbtBytes.ts</code>, shared with the block entity log), and <code>StoreActor.persistentNbt</code> is an accessor over them that answers a new compound on each read. The online owner is the body field <code>owner</code>. The two conditions a client is shown, <code>in_water</code> and <code>on_ground</code>, are logged as <code>condition:&lt;name&gt;</code> as they change (<code>WatchedSet</code>). A trader&#39;s offers are the field <code>merchant</code>, written after a draw, a trade and a restock in the form a save writes. A trade screen trades through <code>EntityRuntime.tradeSource</code>, which reads the merchant on each call, so an undone trade, which builds the merchant again from the logged offers, is what the screen shows next. The saved form also carries the owner, the shown conditions, the record tags and each attribute with its bounds, which the record does not hold.</li><li>The vanilla proxy test found one more gap: a body field and <code>samePlain</code> compared numbers with <code>===</code>, so a pitch written from <code>0</code> to <code>-0</code> was not logged while clients were sent <code>-0</code>. Both compare with <code>Object.is</code> now.</li><li>Nothing changed on the wire, and <code>StoreActors.sync</code> still sends. A unit that throws now also undoes the offers, conditions and owner it changed. The world and outbound digest series of the scripted replay are byte for byte those of program head <code>52f4016c9b</code> over its 19 sampled ticks, checked by writing both series out from the two checkouts, so no baseline moved.</li><li>Proxy tests. <code>network/replica/EntityReplica.test.ts</code>: a fixture entity through thirteen shown writes (name, color, fire, water, owner, leash, a synced property, a group change, health, a status effect, a beam, a fuse and the holder&#39;s removal) and three wanderers, 30 ticks, with the folded view equal to the live one after every tick; holding 3 or 300 entities, a tick that names two of them reads 2 entries and reports 2 changed entities; an entity whose entering entry was never read has no view. With <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code>, 125 entities of the 131 vanilla types (the rest refuse a bare spawn) over 20 ticks match after every tick, and the replica reports 125 entities in 150771 bytes, 2657 entries read and 2618 views built. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> holds every entity&#39;s view against the replica as each sampled tick seals: 38 views over 19 ticks, no divergence, and a replica of 2 entities in 2512 bytes with 17 entity entries read, through <code>reportProgress</code>. <code>PlayerChangeLog.test.ts</code>&#39; warm-up tick now includes the player&#39;s first update, which writes the clock once.</li><li>For D4c: <ol><li><code>EntityReplica.read</code> returns <code>EntityTickChanges</code>: the entities that entered and left, and the fields and slots each held entity had written. <code>tickSealed</code> discards it today; D4c keeps it for its sends. <code>entityView(id)</code> and <code>entityReplicaCounts()</code> are the replicator&#39;s public members.</li><li>An entity that enters between ticks never reaches the replica. Carrying entity entries into the next tick, as D4a carries block entries, fixes it.</li><li><code>EntityView</code> leaves out what a player&#39;s own state decides: breath (<code>vitals.air</code>, <code>vitals.breathing</code>), sprinting, flight from the game mode, the head yaw, and the session&#39;s skin and name. A player&#39;s own fields are logged by <code>PlayerSystems</code> but are not in the saved form, so the replica holds them only once written; D4c has to seed them or put them in the saved form. A player&#39;s equipment comes from its inventory, which the replica holds as <code>inventory-slot</code> entries (<code>EntityReplica.slot</code>).</li><li>The teleport bit of a move (<code>MobBody.teleportPending</code>) is not logged.</li><li>A store actor sends the maximum health it was built with, while the view carries the logged maximum (<a href="https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/issues/2995" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#2995</a>). Sending health from <code>attribute:health</code> entries fixes it.</li><li>A leash held by a player resolves through the world&#39;s sessions (<code>playerIdOf</code>), as <code>EntityRuntime.leashHolderIdOf</code> does through its host.</li></ol></li><li>Checks at D4b&#39;s head, on program head <code>52f4016c9b</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The Prismarine and parity suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> pass together, 491 files and 4744 tests, at load average 12 to 27, including <code>entities.addactor</code> and <code>gameplay.brain-effects</code>; a first run at load average 15 to 27 failed three enchanting table tests that pass alone. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>villager-trade</code> and <code>eating</code> (a golden apple&#39;s effects) pass.</li></ul><h5 id="d4c-entity-replication" tabindex="-1">D4c: entity replication <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4c-entity-replication" aria-label="Permalink to “D4c: entity replication”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Entities reach clients only from the log. <code>StoreActors.sync</code>, its per-tick comparisons and the per-tick equipment comparison are gone, with <code>World.flushEntityAttributes</code> and the sink&#39;s <code>entityAttributesChanged</code>, <code>entityMetadataChanged</code>, <code>entityEquipmentChanged</code>, <code>entityAdded</code> and <code>entityRemoved</code>. <code>network/replica/EntityReplication.ts</code> reads each sealed tick into the replica from <code>tickSealed</code> and sends it from the sink&#39;s new <code>flushEntities</code>, which <code>World.replicateTick</code> runs first after the seal. It shows the entities that entered in the order of the entries: an arriving player is shown the world, then each arrival is offered to everyone else. Then it visits the entities the tick wrote, in the order the replica took them in, which is the store&#39;s, and sends each one&#39;s equipment and metadata, then their health, and queues their moves for <code>flushMovement</code>. A leaving entity waits for the sink&#39;s <code>flushDepartures</code>, which drops it from every client, unless it enters again later in the same flush, when it leaves in order.</li><li>Where each kind comes from: a move from <code>position</code>, <code>yaw</code>, <code>pitch</code> and <code>teleports</code> entries; metadata from the replica&#39;s projection, with a store player&#39;s sprint, flight and breath; synced properties and health (with its maximum) from the replica; a mob&#39;s equipment from its <code>equipment</code> field, and a player&#39;s from the <code>inventory-slot</code> entries of its hotbar, armor and off hand and its <code>inventory.handSlot</code> field. After the seal, <code>World.replicateTick</code> runs <code>flushEntities</code>, D4d&#39;s <code>flushOccurrences</code>, <code>flushDepartures</code>, D4e&#39;s <code>flushOwnerViews</code>, the tracking pass, then the block changes, the block entities and the movement. An occurrence about an entity that entered reaches a client after its AddActor, and one about an entity that left, such as a killing blow&#39;s hurt flash (D4d&#39;s &quot;For D4c&quot; 1), before its RemoveActor. The tracking pass hides whatever the replica no longer holds, so it follows the departures.</li><li>Each send still goes out only when it differs from what the entity&#39;s viewers were last sent, over the visited entities alone. That keeps the packets the old comparison sent: the 1e-4 block move threshold, the breath&#39;s <code>breathWorthSending</code> throttle, a metadata object written over as the actor&#39;s was (so a property that leaves the projection stays as clients hold it), and equipment compared as encoded bytes.</li><li>A first sight is built from the replica (<code>network/replica/firstSight.ts</code>), so <code>spawnTo</code> reads no live entity. Tracking reads <code>ShownEntity</code> values from <code>WorldChangeSink.shownEntities</code>, and each one&#39;s <code>spawnTo</code> encodes its packets once for each entity, codec and compression group in a flush. An entity that entered is shown as it entered, before the entries after its entry, so the AddActor and the updates that follow in that tick are the ones a store actor sent. <code>PlayerSession</code>&#39;s <code>sendAddActor</code>, <code>sendAddItemActor</code>, <code>sendAddPlayer</code>, <code>sendEquipmentFor</code> and <code>sendHeldItemFor</code> are gone, and its armor, status effect and ability sends use the same builders.</li><li>D4b&#39;s five items: <ol><li><code>EntityReplica.read</code> reports the memberships in entry order, each entering entity as it entered, and the fields and slots an entity that entered wrote after its entry.</li><li><code>EntityStore</code> carries every state entry made between ticks into the next tick, and <code>EntityRuntime.remove</code> now records a leaving entry between ticks too. The client e2e <code>summon-entity</code> found that gap: a console <code>/kill</code> runs between ticks, and the pig stayed on screen.</li><li>A store player&#39;s head yaw is the logged field <code>look.headYaw</code>, and its entity enters the log with a shown form beside its saved form (<code>entity/playerShown.ts</code>): head yaw, hand slot, sprinting, game mode, flying, air, breathing, and the hotbar, armor and off-hand slots. Identity (UUID, name, device, platform chat id) comes from the store player and the operator flag from the permission service, as before.</li><li>A teleport writes the logged body field <code>teleports</code>, a count; a tick that writes it sends the move with the teleport bit.</li><li>Leashes resolve players through the world&#39;s sessions, and every leashed entity is projected again in a flush that had a membership.</li></ol></li><li>Two more logged homes: an inert actor&#39;s saved identifier is the saved form&#39;s <code>shownAs</code> (<code>EntityRuntimeOptions.shownAs</code>), and an inert TNT&#39;s fuse is its body&#39;s <code>fuseTicks</code> in place of a hand-set metadata value.</li><li>A store actor&#39;s <code>metadata</code> and <code>attributes</code> are read from the store when asked; what remains reads them: a player&#39;s own client, saves and the replay digest. The live metadata leaves out the leash holder, which only other clients are shown.</li><li>What changed on the wire: health carries the maximum the store holds (<a href="https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/issues/2995" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#2995</a>). AddActor, RemoveActor and the first sights of a spawn leave after the seal instead of mid-tick, in the same tick. A player crossing a chunk mid-tick is shown entities as the last sealed tick left them. Metadata is sent for any entity a tick wrote, where the old pass projected only entities in simulated chunks. A game mode change and a revival send the player&#39;s metadata from the log instead of from <code>PlayerSession</code>. The scripted replay&#39;s world and outbound digest series are byte for byte those of program head <code>70d6c63830</code>, so no baseline moved.</li><li>Proxy tests in <code>network/NetworkWorldReplicatorEntities.test.ts</code>, over a real store&#39;s sealed ticks: holding 3 or 300 entities, a tick that writes 2 of them visits 2; an <code>actor-event</code> and a removal in one tick reach a tracker as the event&#39;s frame and then the drop, and the other way round when the departures run first; a first sight to 5 viewers in 3 codec and compression groups encodes 3 times, and the 3 viewers of one group get the same bytes; a raised maximum health goes out as the new maximum (#2995); an entity removed between ticks is dropped with the next tick. <code>entity/data/storePlayers.test.ts</code> holds a player&#39;s AddPlayer, abilities, armor, moves and breath throttle to the old packet sequence (breath writes of 249, 245, not breathing, sprinting and survival send 1, 0, 1, 1 and 1 SetActorData), and the eye of ender test still sends 2 moves in 3 ticks. <code>EntityReplicationCounts</code> (<code>visited</code>, <code>firstSightEncodes</code>, <code>firstSights</code>) is the replicator&#39;s <code>entityReplicationCounts</code>.</li><li>For D4e: status effects still go out when an actor changes them (<code>StoreActor.announceStatusEffect</code>, <code>statusEffectChanged</code>), not from the <code>statusEffects</code> entries, because the entries carry the whole list each tick and no change kind. <code>entityMoved</code> stays for the moves <code>PlayerSession</code> and <code>MovePlayerHandler</code> make, and a player&#39;s own client still reads the live <code>StoreActor.metadata</code>.</li><li>For D4f: the tracking phase still plans over every entity the replica holds, on its interval. The first-sight cache lives for one flush and is cleared by each read. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> does not report <code>entityReplicationCounts</code> yet.</li><li><code>PlayerSessionOwnSends.test.ts</code> (D4e) no longer finds a <code>SetActorDataPacket</code> among the packets <code>setGamemode</code> queues: the player&#39;s metadata goes to it and its viewers from the logged game mode after the seal. <code>testing/changeTicks.test.helper.ts</code>&#39; <code>replicateEntities</code> runs the four post-seal phases in the world&#39;s order for tests that drive a replicator.</li><li>Checks at D4c&#39;s head, rebased onto program head <code>25cdcbb46d</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code> (49 tasks), <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass; lint&#39;s one warning is the existing unused <code>Tag</code> in <code>packages/leveldb/src/db/VersionEdit.ts</code>. The Prismarine and parity suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> pass together, 495 files and 4793 tests, at load average 11 to 15. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes, and its world and outbound digest series are byte for byte those of <code>25cdcbb46d</code>. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>summon-entity</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>player-knockback</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>item-drop-pickup</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>eating</code> and <code>rejoin</code> pass together, and <code>dimensions</code> passes alone; it fails when it runs soon after <code>damage</code> in a selection, because it asserts full health on arrival (<a href="https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/issues/2997" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#2997</a>). Before the rebase, on <code>70d6c63830</code>, <code>movement-rotation</code>, <code>look-direction</code>, <code>gait</code>, <code>natural-spawn-placement</code>, <code>natural-spawn-under-canopy</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code>, <code>teleport</code> and <code>fall-damage</code> passed as well.</li></ul><h5 id="d4d-occurrences" tabindex="-1">D4d: occurrences <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4d-occurrences" aria-label="Permalink to “D4d: occurrences”">​</a></h5><ul><li>Every transient fact is an occurrence in the change log, and replication sends it after the seal. <code>ChangeEntry.ts</code> has <code>block-sound</code>, <code>named-sound</code>, <code>named-particle</code>, <code>world-effect</code>, <code>actor-sound</code>, <code>relayed-sound</code>, and the existing <code>actor-event</code>, <code>animate</code>, <code>emote</code> and <code>impulse</code> kinds, extended with what replication needs. <code>sound</code> was declared and never written, and is now <code>block-sound</code>.</li><li><code>WorldChangeSink</code> lost <code>blockSound</code>, <code>namedSound</code>, <code>namedParticle</code>, <code>worldEffect</code>, <code>actorEffect</code> and <code>actorSound</code>, and gained the tick phase <code>flushOccurrences</code>. <code>tickSealed</code> keeps the sealed tick&#39;s occurrences in log order, and <code>World.replicateTick</code> runs <code>flushOccurrences</code> first, ahead of the block changes, so a break&#39;s particles still reach a client ahead of its block change, as when they were sent the moment they happened. The world methods keep their names and signatures (<code>sendWorldEvent</code>, <code>sendActorEvent</code>, <code>sendActorSound</code>, <code>hitBlock</code>, <code>playNamedSound*</code>, <code>spawnNamedParticle*</code>) and log through a private <code>World.occurred</code>. A position of another world is refused there with the verbose line <code>getViewers</code> gave, since the log has no world on a position.</li><li>The four relaying handlers log in their own unit and no longer pass a send to <code>step.output</code>: <code>World.relayAnimation</code>, <code>World.relayEmote</code> and <code>World.relaySound</code> are new, and <code>ActorEvent</code> goes through <code>sendActorEvent</code>. The emote&#39;s debug line no longer counts its audience, which the handler no longer knows. The client sound relay rebuilds its packet from the fields the client sent, which are the bytes the relay always carried, since the send path encodes from fields.</li><li>The impulse: <code>host.playerImpulse</code> and its <code>live.ts</code> send are gone, and replication sends <code>SetActorMotion</code> to the owner from the <code>impulse</code> occurrence through <code>PlayerSession.sendMotion</code>.</li><li><code>EntityStore</code> holds an occurrence made between ticks for the next tick, as it holds block entries (<code>isCarriedEntry</code> in <code>ChangeEntry.ts</code>). An <code>action-rejected</code> is a record and not a send, so it is still counted as unlogged.</li><li>What changed on the wire. An occurrence logged in a unit that throws leaves with the unit and is not sent. An occurrence made between ticks, such as a sound from a tick function, goes out with the next tick&#39;s flush. A world with no entity runtime has no log and sends none of them, as D4a already had it for blocks. Inside a tick, the occurrences now follow the packets the simulation still sends inline (attributes, metadata, the spawns and removals, the owner&#39;s own state) rather than sitting between them. One consequence is visible in a capture: an <code>ActorEvent</code> for an entity removed later in the same tick, such as the hurt flash of a killing blow, finds no trackers at the flush, because the removal went out inline and the trackers forgot the entity. The client removes the entity in that tick either way, so nothing on screen changes. <code>animate</code> and <code>emote</code> reach their audience through one shared batch for each codec and compression group instead of one <code>send</code> for each viewer.</li><li>Advancement timing: the advancement triggers and the plugin <code>entityDamage</code> and <code>entityDeath</code> events still fire inline from <code>EntityHostFacts.damageCommitted</code>. No observer reads them from the log, so their timing did not move, and <code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code> passes unchanged.</li><li>Proxy tests. <code>network/NetworkWorldReplicatorOccurrences.test.ts</code>: one occurrence of each of the nine broadcast kinds, sent to six viewers in three codec and compression groups, is encoded 3 times, 2 of them for the first protocol version, and every viewer of a group gets the same bytes (the slice 7 target for occurrences). The same file checks each kind&#39;s audience and packet, that nothing is sent before the flush and each occurrence once, and, through the real <code>LevelSoundEventHandler</code>, that a relayed client sound goes out with the bytes the client sent. <code>world/WorldChangeSink.test.ts</code>, over a real world and store: an occurrence in a unit that throws is not in the sealed tick, one between ticks arrives with the next tick, and one at a position of another world is refused. <code>EntityStore.test.ts</code> checks the carrying, and <code>WorldTickPhases.test.ts</code> that the flush runs after the seal and ahead of the block changes. <code>PlayerChangeLog.test.ts</code> checks that a shove sends no motion until replication reads the sealed tick.</li><li><code>testing/occurrences.test.helper.ts</code> builds a prototype world double over a real store that reads back the occurrences of each tick (<code>occurrenceWorld</code>), and <code>sealOccurrences</code> hands a sink a sealed tick of occurrences. The world doubles in <code>live.test.ts</code>, <code>serverSource.test.ts</code> and <code>interaction.test.ts</code> read sounds and actor events from the sealed tick.</li><li>The scripted replay logs 20 entries and 2350 bytes over its 19 sampled ticks (<code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, non-gating). It holds no occurrence: its session makes no sound the server logs.</li><li>For the later packages: <ol><li>D4c: the actor event flush reads <code>session.tracks</code> at the flush, after an inline removal. Reading a leave entry in log order, after the occurrences before it, sends a killing blow&#39;s <code>ActorEvent</code> to the trackers again. <code>sendToTrackedEntity</code> now takes a <code>Pick&lt;ShownActor, &#39;getRuntimeId&#39;&gt;</code>.</li><li>D4e: <code>meleeAttack</code>&#39;s sounds and particles (<code>Combat.ts</code>) are logged synchronously now. <code>sendActorSound</code>, <code>sendWorldEvent</code> and <code>sendActorEvent</code> are still <code>async</code> and resolve without waiting on anything, so <code>step.defer</code> 8 can drop the awaits and run in the handler. <code>hitBlock</code> still awaits <code>getBlockState</code> before it logs.</li><li>The block entity flush still sends one <code>BlockActorData</code> per position through <code>sendAround</code>, which already encodes once for each group; it has no proxy test of its own.</li></ol></li><li>Checks at D4d&#39;s head, on program head <code>52f4016c9b</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The Prismarine and parity suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> pass together, 491 files and 4752 tests, at load average 20 to 33. <code>gameplay.combat-advancements</code> passes. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched. Client e2e <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>player-knockback</code>, <code>damage</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>movement-fanout</code> and <code>mining-drops</code> pass. The NetherNet loopback tests in <code>Login.test.ts</code>, <code>ticker.test.ts</code>&#39;s timeout test and three <code>enchantingTable.test.ts</code> seed tests each failed in one full run under load and passed alone and in later full runs.</li></ul><h5 id="d4e-the-owner-s-view" tabindex="-1">D4e: the owner&#39;s view <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4e-the-owner-s-view" aria-label="Permalink to “D4e: the owner&#39;s view”">​</a></h5><ul><li>The simulation no longer awaits a send to a player&#39;s own client in the three slice 7 deferred pieces and the methods they reach. A send queues its packet before its first await (<code>MinecraftSession.sendDataPacket</code>), so a method that calls its sends in turn and hands their promises to <code>allSent</code> (<code>PlayerSession.ts</code>) has written everything and queued every packet when it returns. Awaiting each send in turn queued the next packet, and made the writes after it, only once the transport framed the last. Converted: <code>setGamemode</code>, <code>setFlying</code>, <code>stopMining</code>, <code>announceSettings</code>, <code>sendGameTypeChange</code>, <code>sendAbilityChange</code>, <code>completeSpawn</code>, <code>sendInventory</code>, <code>sendArmorFor</code>, <code>sendAttributes</code> (which now clears the dirty attributes as it queues them), <code>sendStatusEffects</code>, <code>setPlayerPosition</code>, <code>openMainInventory</code> and <code>releaseArrivalChunks</code>. They keep their <code>Promise&lt;void&gt;</code> return, which now only says when the transport framed the packets.</li><li>The three <code>step.defer</code> calls run in their handlers. <code>SetPlayerGameType</code> calls <code>setGamemode</code> in its unit and hands the promise to <code>step.output</code>. <code>SetLocalPlayerAsInitialized</code> calls <code>World.addPlayer</code>, now synchronous, and <code>completeSpawn</code> in its unit. <code>InventoryTransaction</code>&#39;s melee calls <code>meleeAttack</code>, which returns its <code>boolean</code> directly: the blow goes through the store&#39;s damage pipeline, and its sounds and particles are occurrences. A target that has left its world&#39;s store takes nothing, which is what the pipeline answered for it before. <code>loose</code> is synchronous the same way, so a bow release puts the arrow in the world in its unit. The <code>step.defer</code> count is 20 calls in 12 handler modules, down from 23 in 14 at program head <code>f8593f7826</code>.</li><li>D2c&#39;s and D2d&#39;s writes that stayed in a unit only because they came before the first await: <code>setFlying</code>, <code>openMainInventory</code>, <code>releaseArrivalChunks</code> and <code>setPlayerPosition</code> have no await now. <code>setSprinting</code> had none. <code>World.dropContents</code>, <code>dropFromBlock</code> and <code>dropFromPlayer</code> drop through a synchronous <code>spill</code>; they make no sends. <code>loose</code> is above.</li><li>Where each owner send comes from now: <ol><li>The game mode change, the join&#39;s spawn state, a move, the inventory window and a released arrival: the input&#39;s handler, queued in its unit, as before. The packets and their order are the same; <code>PlayerSessionOwnSends.test.ts</code> asserts the order, and the same assertions pass against the program head&#39;s <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> with the calls awaited.</li><li>A trader&#39;s offers: replication. <code>tickSealed</code> collects the traders whose <code>merchant</code> field the sealed ticks wrote, and the new sink phase <code>flushOwnerViews</code>, which <code>World.replicateTick</code> runs after <code>flushOccurrences</code>, sends <code>sendTradeOffers</code> to every session with a trade screen open on one. <code>ItemStackRequest</code> no longer sends them after a trade. The prices still come from the screen&#39;s <code>TradeSource</code> at the flush, since the per-player context (cures, the hero effect) is not in the log.</li><li>A slot whose write was undone: replication. <code>EntityStore</code> logs a <code>slot-shown</code> occurrence for each slot an undone span wrote, once and in the order the span first named it, in the span around the undone one, so undoing that span too names the slot again (<code>ChangeLog.revertSpan</code> returns the entries it removed). <code>flushOwnerViews</code> sends each to the player that carries it through <code>PlayerSession.sendOwnSlot</code>, which assigns the restored stack its new network id and sends <code>InventorySlot</code> for the inventory, the armor, the off hand and the cursor. The ender chest and the crafting grid have no window the client always holds, so nothing is sent for them.</li></ol></li><li>What changed on the wire. A trade&#39;s <code>UpdateTrade</code> leaves after the seal in the same tick, not straight after the <code>ItemStackResponse</code>. A level up or a restock while the screen is open now reaches it. A slot written back by an undo is sent to its owner. A failure in <code>setGamemode</code>, <code>completeSpawn</code>, a melee hit or a bow release now undoes the whole input, where it used to undo only the deferred piece. The scripted replay&#39;s digests did not move and no baseline was touched; it logs 21 entries and 2496 bytes, as at the program head.</li><li>Proxy tests. <code>network/PlayerSessionOwnSends.test.ts</code>, over a real session: <code>setGamemode</code> has written the mode and queued its 8 packets before it returns, and awaiting adds none; <code>completeSpawn</code> queues 18 packets in the old order and has cleared the dirty attributes; an undone slot leaves one <code>slot-shown</code> entry and is sent under a network id other than the one it had. <code>entity/data/EntityStore.test.ts</code>: a unit that wrote one slot twice and another once leaves 2 occurrences, a nested undo inside an undone unit leaves 2, and a unit that finishes leaves none. <code>network/NetworkWorldReplicatorOwnerViews.test.ts</code>: 3 <code>merchant</code> entries over two ticks send the offers once to each of the 2 players trading with that trader and 0 times to the others, and slots go to their owner alone in log order. <code>WorldTickPhases.test.ts</code> places <code>flushOwnerViews</code> after <code>flushOccurrences</code>. <code>network/PlayerSessionAwaits.test.ts</code> walks calls on <code>this</code> from <code>World.update</code> and from <code>PlayerSession.updatePlayer</code> and reports the awaits it reaches through <code>reportProgress</code>: 27 in 50 <code>World</code> methods, unchanged, and 53 in 48 <code>PlayerSession</code> methods, down from 64; <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> holds 145 awaits, down from 184. It fails if a method the input handlers run in their unit gains an await.</li><li>For D4f: <ol><li><code>inventory-slot</code> entries reach the owner only for an undo. The client learns the result of its own stack requests from the <code>ItemStackResponse</code>, so sending a slot for every entry would send each request&#39;s slots twice. Sending the rest from the log needs replication to know which slots a response answered. Until then the whole-inventory resends (the commands in <code>command/host/</code>, a pickup in <code>live.ts</code>, the item interactions in <code>item/interaction.ts</code>) still call <code>sendInventory</code> from simulation code, without awaiting it.</li><li><code>World.sendActorSound</code>, <code>sendWorldEvent</code> and <code>sendActorEvent</code> are still <code>async</code> and resolve without waiting, so <code>meleeAttack</code> and <code>loose</code> hand their promises a <code>.catch</code>. Making them synchronous removes that.</li><li><code>PlayerSession.ts</code> still holds 145 awaits; most are chunk streaming, the dimension change and the respawn, which are slices 8 and 9.</li></ol></li><li>Checks at D4e&#39;s head, on program head <code>f8593f7826</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass; lint&#39;s one warning is the unused <code>Tag</code> type in <code>packages/leveldb/src/db/VersionEdit.ts</code>, which this package does not touch. The Prismarine and parity suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> pass together, 495 files and 4775 tests, at load average 36 to 63. <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> passes with no baseline touched. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>eating</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>teleport</code> and <code>mining-drops</code> pass.</li></ul><h5 id="d4f-the-close" tabindex="-1">D4f: the close <a class="header-anchor" href="#d4f-the-close" aria-label="Permalink to “D4f: the close”">​</a></h5><ul><li>The target &quot;a snapshot plus the log reproduces the replay digest&quot; gates. A third run of the scripted session in <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> stops at the tick before the sample, which is the snapshot, and runs no tick after it. It writes each sampled tick of the first replay&#39;s log onto that world through <code>EntityStore.replay</code>, and after each one the digest, taken with the sealed tick&#39;s header, equals the one the replay had as that tick sealed: 20 ticks, the snapshot&#39;s own included. Entity ids are mapped from the replay&#39;s players to the snapshot&#39;s, since runtime ids are process-local. A chunk entry takes the input the replay took: the snapshot asks for the chunk and admits it when the entry comes, and a chunk that left is taken out by the new <code>World.replayChunkLeave</code>, which needs only the position. The sample enters 6 chunks and unloads at least one. Leaving out the entity entries makes 17 of the ticks diverge, so the comparison bites.</li><li>What the digest hashes and the log did not hold, and where each went: <ol><li>The block update queue: logged. Each queue, move to an earlier tick and removal is a <code>block-update</code> field entry holding the due tick (<code>BlockUpdateScheduler.observer</code>). Applying the entries in order keeps the queue&#39;s processing order, which the digest hashes, and the log now undoes a unit&#39;s updates, so <code>BlockUpdateScheduler</code> is no longer a <code>UnitJournal</code>; <code>UnitJournal</code> and <code>EntityStore.addUnitJournal</code> are gone.</li><li>The scheduled block and fluid ticks: logged. Each tick queued and taken off is a <code>scheduled-tick</code> field entry holding its trigger tick, priority and order (<code>TickScheduler.observe</code>, <code>TickList.restore</code> in <code>@jsprismarine/engine-systems</code>). This also closes D3&#39;s &quot;Not undone yet&quot; for scheduled ticks: an undone unit takes back the ticks its writes queued. <code>WorldChangeTick.test.ts</code> covers both queues, since the scripted session&#39;s water reacts through block updates that drain in the tick that queued them and queues no scheduled tick.</li><li>The level clock, the weather, the game rules, the random tick generator, the depth of the light queue and the chunk loads in flight: in the sealed tick, as its <code>header</code> (<code>world/changelog/TickHeader.ts</code>, <code>World.tickHeader</code>). Each moves every tick whether or not anything was written, or belongs to the level that every world shares, or is work derived from block writes, or is an input in flight, so an entry for each would break &quot;an idle world appends no entries&quot;. <code>World.replayStateDigest(header)</code> hashes a given header in place of the world&#39;s own, with the same bytes as before. The light queue is still not undone with a unit; its depth is in the header, so a unit that throws after a write that queued light leaves the depth as the write left it, as D3 found.</li></ol></li><li>Outbound packets count toward the tick that queued them (ADR 0006). <code>MinecraftSession</code> reads the tick when a send queues a packet, not when the transport frames it, so a packet replication sends counts toward the sealed tick it read. The scripted replay frames every such packet inside its tick, so its outbound digests did not move; <code>MinecraftSessionOutboundCodec.test.ts</code> checks a packet framed after its tick ended.</li><li><code>WorldChangeSink</code> carries the sealed tick and its flush phases, and lost <code>statusEffectChanged</code>: a status effect change is a <code>status-effect</code> occurrence, sent after the seal to the trackers with one encode for each group and to the entity&#39;s own client. What it still hears outside the sealed ticks, and why, is in its comment: a chunk entering and leaving with its block entities (the content arrived as an input), <code>entityMoved</code> for a player&#39;s own moves (a head turn alone, the pose resent at a spawn and a respawn&#39;s teleport bit, which the log&#39;s pose comparison does not show), and the time and a game rule (the level&#39;s, which no world&#39;s log holds).</li><li>Counts through <code>reportProgress</code> from <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code>, not gated: replication read 18 entries (1 block, 17 entity), visited 6 entities and encoded 2 first sights for 2 first sights. The change log holds 35 entries and 4260 bytes over the 19 sampled ticks, up from 21 entries at program head <code>847fe2cf32</code>: the block updates the water queues are entries now. The world and outbound digest series are byte for byte those of <code>847fe2cf32</code>, checked by writing both series out from the two source trees, so no baseline moved.</li><li>Leftovers done: D4e&#39;s 2, <code>World.sendActorSound</code>, <code>sendWorldEvent</code> and <code>sendActorEvent</code> return nothing, and <code>World.sendStatusEffect</code> too, so their callers hand no promise a <code>.catch</code>; the actor event relay logs in its handler&#39;s unit with no output. D4c&#39;s status effects go out from the log, as above. <code>PlayerSession.ts</code> holds 132 awaits, down from 133; <code>World</code> keeps 28 reachable from <code>update</code>.</li><li>Not done, with the reason and what each blocks: <ol><li>Every owner slot from <code>inventory-slot</code> entries (D4e&#39;s 1). Replication would have to know which slots an <code>ItemStackResponse</code> already answered, or each request&#39;s slots go out twice. The response is built in the handler from the request, so this needs the response to be an occurrence that names its slots. Until then the whole-inventory resends in <code>command/host/</code>, <code>live.ts</code> and <code>item/interaction.ts</code> call <code>sendInventory</code> from simulation code. It blocks moving replication off the simulation&#39;s thread.</li><li>Trade prices at the flush (D4e). The price depends on the trading player&#39;s cures and hero effect, which are not in the log. A <code>merchant</code> entry says the offers changed, and the screen&#39;s <code>TradeSource</code> prices them when they go out. It blocks a replica that answers a trade screen alone.</li><li>Tracking over every entity the replica holds (D4c). A tracking pass decides what each viewer sees from where the viewers stand, which moves without an entry for the entities; it runs on its interval over the replica, not the simulation. Making it follow the log needs a way to find the entities near a viewer that moved without walking all of them. It blocks nothing in slice 7, since the pass reads only the replica.</li><li>The workstation input slots (<code>OpenBlockContainer.inputs</code>, the D2 close). The digest and the save hold no workstation slots, so the snapshot target does not need them, and logging them would put a window that is session state into the log. It needs the decision the D2 close named: whether an open workstation is player state, with a home on the store player. It blocks undoing a stack request that moved items into a workstation and then threw.</li><li><code>entityMoved</code> and <code>timeChanged</code>, <code>gameRuleChanged</code>, as above.</li></ol></li><li>Changeset <code>change-log-snapshot.md</code>: <code>minor</code> for <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code> (public members removed or changed: <code>WorldChangeSink.statusEffectChanged</code>, <code>UnitJournal</code>, the async return of four <code>World</code> methods) and <code>patch</code> for <code>@jsprismarine/engine-systems</code> (new <code>observe</code> and <code>restore</code>). Both are below <code>1.0.0</code>.</li><li>Checks at D4f&#39;s head, on program head <code>847fe2cf32</code>: <code>pnpm run build</code>, <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass; lint&#39;s one warning is the unused <code>Tag</code> in <code>packages/leveldb/src/db/VersionEdit.ts</code>. The Prismarine, parity, client, block-behaviors and engine-systems suites with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passed 543 of 545 files (5421 tests) at load average 13 to 30; the two failures were port binds under load, <code>Cli.test.ts</code> (UDP port 19280 in use) and a timeout in <code>modules/network.test.ts</code>, and both files pass alone. Client e2e <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>movement-fanout</code>, <code>block-placement</code>, <code>mining-drops</code>, <code>lever-lamp</code>, <code>lit-furnace</code>, <code>attack-entity</code>, <code>item-drop-pickup</code>, <code>villager-trade</code>, <code>recipe-book</code>, <code>eating</code> and <code>death-respawn</code> pass together, and <code>dimensions</code> passes alone.</li></ul><h3 id="phase-e-the-synchronous-tick" tabindex="-1">Phase E: the synchronous tick <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-e-the-synchronous-tick" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase E: the synchronous tick”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>E1</td><td>8</td><td>D3, B4</td><td>Saves at a tick boundary, if ADR 0018 is accepted</td></tr><tr><td>E2</td><td>9</td><td>D4</td><td>Dimension travel as messages, and worlds built outside ticks</td></tr><tr><td>E3</td><td>9</td><td>D4</td><td>Chunk tickets and waiting states for the dragon fight</td></tr><tr><td>E4</td><td>9</td><td>E2, E3</td><td>Tick functions as inputs, and the test that a tick completes without yielding</td></tr></tbody></table><p>E1, E2 and E3 run side by side.</p><h5 id="e2" tabindex="-1">E2 <a class="header-anchor" href="#e2" aria-label="Permalink to “E2”">​</a></h5><p>E2 is too large for one pass, so it splits in two. E2a goes first.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Work</th><th>Main files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>E2a portal travel as messages</td><td>A trip through a portal leaves its world&#39;s tick as a message. The destination world is built and the arrival chunks are loaded outside every tick, and the destination admits the arrival in its input step once they are loaded. The source side leaves its world in the same input step, so a trip is one leave entry in the source world and one enter entry in the destination. Removes the portal travel entry of <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code></td><td><code>world/portal/DimensionTravel.ts</code>, <code>entity/data/storeActors.ts</code>, <code>world/Level.ts</code>, <code>world/TickContext.ts</code>, <code>Server.ts</code></td></tr><tr><td>E2b the other world changes</td><td><code>/tp</code> and <code>changeWorld</code> into another dimension, the queued change that <code>completeDimensionChange</code> starts, the credits, and <code>SetDefaultGameType</code> as messages, which removes their <code>step.defer</code> calls</td><td><code>command/host/entities.ts</code>, <code>command/jsprismarineCommands.ts</code>, <code>network/PlayerSession.ts</code>, the handlers that defer a world change</td></tr></tbody></table><p>E2a, and the credits from E2b, left these notes.</p><ul><li>How a trip runs. In a source world&#39;s tick, <code>StoreActor.update</code> finds the contact ready and calls <code>DimensionTravel.depart</code>, which plans the trip from the source world alone (the route, the scaled target, the traveler&#39;s place in the portal, its size and facing), files it and returns. It starts the destination&#39;s preparation through <code>outsideTick</code> (<code>world/TickContext.ts</code>): <code>Level.prepareWorld</code> builds the world if nobody has entered it, then the chunks the arrival reads are loaded, and those chunks enter at an input step like any other. Nothing in the destination changes in that tick, and the traveler stays in its world. <code>Server</code>&#39;s input step runs <code>DimensionTravel.admit</code> after the chunk completions and the respawn answers. A trip lands once its world is ready (<code>Level.readyWorld</code>) and either a portal stands within the search radius in loaded chunks or every chunk of the creation area is loaded. The arrival is then resolved from loaded chunks only, a portal or the End platform is written, and <code>transfer</code> takes the traveler out of the source store and into the destination&#39;s. Both writes land in the tick the input step opened, one membership <code>leave</code> in the source world and one <code>enter</code> in the destination. A trip that is not ready stays filed, and a trip whose traveler left its world first is dropped.</li><li>Readiness is decided in the input step from loaded chunks, so a replay that admits the same chunk completions at the same ticks lands each trip at the same tick. Preparation runs while ticks run and is never awaited by one.</li><li>Timing: a trip lands at the input step of a later tick, not inside the update that started it. The traveler spends the rest of that tick in the source world, and the data-driven half of its tick runs there once more. No replay digest moved, because <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> has no portal trip, so nothing was re-baselined.</li><li>While a trip is filed, <code>DimensionTravel.touch</code> skips the traveler, so standing in the portal starts no second trip.</li><li>The credits. <code>PlayerSession.completeCredits</code> returns a <code>boolean</code> and files the change with <code>DimensionTravel.move</code>, which the same input step share carries out before the trips: <code>changeWorld</code>, then the respawn answer. <code>ShowCreditsHandler</code> no longer defers. The change runs one input step later than before; the client sees the same packets in the same order (client e2e <code>ender-dragon</code>).</li><li>Removed: <code>DimensionTravel.resolve</code>, and the <code>IN_TICK_CHUNK_LOADS</code> entry &quot;portal travel loading the arrival chunks&quot;. Three entries are left: the respawn search, the dragon fight (E3) and <code>World.setBlockRuntimeId</code>.</li><li><code>step.defer</code> calls, as <code>noServerAccess.test.ts</code> reports them: 20 in 12 handler modules before, 19 in 11 after.</li><li>Proxy tests, in <code>DimensionTravel.test.ts</code> under &quot;DimensionTravel as messages&quot;: a mob&#39;s trip appends no membership entry in its source tick and no entry at all in the destination, then exactly one <code>leave</code> in the source and one <code>enter</code> in the destination at the input step; the same for a creative player driven through <code>World.update</code> with the completion queue active, counted over every input step until it lands; and a trip into an End nobody entered asks for the build exactly once, from outside the tick (<code>insideTick()</code> is false at the call), and the traveler is still in its world when the tick ends.</li><li>Left for E2b: <code>/tp</code> and <code>changeWorld</code> into another dimension, which await <code>worldFor</code> and <code>changeWorld</code> inside a command; the queued change <code>completeDimensionChange</code> starts, and its <code>step.defer</code>, which needs the advancement trigger kept after the sends; <code>releaseChangeHeldOverDeath</code>; <code>SetDefaultGameType</code>.</li><li>Left for E4: <code>transfer</code> still awaits inside the input step: the <code>playerChangeDimension</code> event, <code>startCredits</code>&#39; send and <code>changeWorld</code>, which saves the player record and awaits its sends. A trip out of the End awaits the respawn search for a player and <code>getSpawnPosition</code> for anything else, also in the input step. <code>touch</code> still awaits <code>entityPortalEnter</code>. <code>outsideTick</code> is an <code>AsyncLocalStorage.exit</code>; once the tick is synchronous and the store is a flag, it is a call that clears the flag.</li><li>Risk for chunk tickets (E3): the chunks a trip loads hold no ticket. The unload pass can take them from a destination that has players before the trip lands, and the trip then loads them again.</li><li>Checks at this head, on program head <code>9c09572f42</code>: <code>pnpm run typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm run lint:custom</code> and <code>pnpm run lint</code> pass. The Prismarine suite with <code>JSP_CONTENT_VANILLA=1</code> passes, 442 files and 4493 tests, with 2 files and 10 tests skipped; the run before the last fix failed only <code>blockContainer.test.ts</code>, whose travel double had no <code>isDeparting</code>. The parity and client suites passed 68 of 69 files: <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> failed under the suite at load average 20 to 25 and passes alone with no baseline touched. Client e2e <code>dimensions</code> alone, then <code>spawn</code>, <code>rejoin</code>, <code>death-respawn</code>, <code>spawnpoint-respawn</code>, <code>ender-dragon</code>, <code>item-drop-pickup</code> and <code>teleport</code> pass. NetherNet tests needed <code>pnpm --filter @jsprismarine/native-network run build:native</code> in this worktree first.</li></ul><h3 id="phase-f-cleanup" tabindex="-1">Phase F: cleanup <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-f-cleanup" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase F: cleanup”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Slice</th><th>Depends on</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F1</td><td>10</td><td>E4</td><td>One lifecycle event bus</td></tr><tr><td>F2</td><td>11</td><td>F1</td><td>Explicit exports for Prismarine, split by directory</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-gate-for-each-slice" tabindex="-1">The gate for each slice <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gate-for-each-slice" aria-label="Permalink to “The gate for each slice”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Typecheck, lint and tests pass.</li><li>The parity families the slice touches pass.</li><li>Replay digests are unchanged, or are re-baselined in a separate commit that states the intended change. Until slice 9 this compares one replay with another under the guarantees listed for the slices already landed.</li><li>A client e2e scenario covers any client-visible change.</li><li>The proxy tests that gate at this slice pass.</li><li>A Changeset for each affected package follows <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/guide/versioning.html">the versioning policy</a>.</li><li>The docs the slice invalidates are updated, such as <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/data-driven/entities.html">entities</a>, <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/data-driven/engine-systems.html">engine systems</a> and the status in <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/plans/server-rearchitecture.html">the server re-architecture plan</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="performance-targets" tabindex="-1">Performance targets <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-targets" aria-label="Permalink to “Performance targets”">​</a></h2><p>Each target is a count or a structure asserted in a test. None is a duration.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Gates from slice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every handler declares a lane, checked against the handler registry</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>No wall-clock read inside the world systems phase, with <code>performance.now</code> stubbed to throw</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>A replay applies each recorded input at the tick the capture stamped</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>An index query examines at most the candidates in the cells it touches, on a fixed scenario</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Applying a span and then its inverse leaves the replay digest unchanged, for every entry kind</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>An idle world appends no entries, and N walking mobs append at most N position entries in a tick</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>No mirror class and no health comparison</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>No <code>Entity</code>, <code>GenericEntity</code> or <code>Human</code> class, and no array copy through <code>all()</code> or <code>getEntities()</code></td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>No <code>Server</code> import in a handler</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Encode calls for one broadcast occurrence equal the number of codec and compression groups</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Replication visits entities in proportion to log entries, not to the number of entities</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>A snapshot plus the log reproduces the replay digest</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>A tick makes no wall-clock read and completes without yielding</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>No barrel in Prismarine</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three counts are reported and do not gate: awaits reachable from <code>World.update</code>, change log entries and bytes per tick, and replica entries and bytes. They go through <code>reportProgress</code>.</p><p>The slice 7 targets gate from D4f. Encode calls for one broadcast occurrence: <code>NetworkWorldReplicatorOccurrences.test.ts</code>, for every broadcast kind, and <code>NetworkWorldReplicatorEntities.test.ts</code> for a first sight. Visits in proportion to log entries: <code>BlockReplica.test.ts</code> for blocks and block entities and <code>NetworkWorldReplicatorEntities.test.ts</code> for entities. A snapshot plus the log: <code>SessionReplay.test.ts</code> over the scripted session, and <code>WorldChangeTick.test.ts</code> for the scheduled ticks that session does not queue.</p><h2 id="scope-notes" tabindex="-1">Scope notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-notes" aria-label="Permalink to “Scope notes”">​</a></h2><p>Handlers take a narrow context and not <code>Server</code>. Other files that take <code>Server</code> stay out of the program, unless a codemod or delegated agents can convert them mechanically with no real risk. Then they go in.</p><h2 id="deferred" tabindex="-1">Deferred <a class="header-anchor" href="#deferred" aria-label="Permalink to “Deferred”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The Script API host. Its before-hooks are guards under ADR 0013. A wall-clock watchdog for long scripts would conflict with <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0009-external-inputs-enter-once-per-tick.html">ADR 0009</a>, and the host design has to answer that and pick the point in a tick where <code>system.run</code> callbacks run.</li><li>The low-level plugin form (&quot;mods&quot;). Keep the store&#39;s plain-data edge and the explicit exports in mind, and build nothing for it now.</li><li>Moving replication, a world or pathfinding to another thread. <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/adr/0011-a-world-exchanges-only-plain-data.html">ADR 0011</a> lists the candidates. A world on its own thread also moves the read lane onto the replica.</li><li>Retention of the change log, crash recovery from it, and rollback tooling.</li></ul><h2 id="checks-to-run-not-assumed" tabindex="-1">Checks to run, not assumed <a class="header-anchor" href="#checks-to-run-not-assumed" aria-label="Permalink to “Checks to run, not assumed”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Advancement triggers fire inline from the legacy damage and death methods. Reading them from the log changes when they fire, so slices 4 and 7 need a parity check on advancement timing.</li><li>Rejecting an interaction with an unloaded chunk should match Java. The parity suite has to confirm it in slice 6. Confirmed by D2b&#39;s parity family <code>gameplay.block-targets</code>.</li><li>Counts in place of the 20 ms and 25 ms budgets mean an overloaded host ticks slower and never simulates differently. Slice 1 needs a scenario that shows the tick counter lagging with identical digests.</li><li><code>SubChunkRequest</code> can load an evicted chunk that was already announced (<code>PlayerSession.ts:578</code>). The read lane allows the wait, and slice 1 has to confirm the load reaches the world as an input.</li><li><code>World.replayStateDigest</code> calls <code>dataEntities().persist()</code> before it hashes, so taking a digest has a side effect. Slice 2 has to remove it or show it changes nothing.</li><li>The replay test asserts only that one tick has a digest. Slice 1 should make it compare two runs tick by tick before anything depends on it.</li><li>How the path finder treats a read from an unloaded chunk was not traced.</li><li>Whether a saved player&#39;s inventory record is a deep copy. Confirmed by B4 and again by C4e, which also made the record copy the spawn point; see the C4e notes.</li><li>Tick functions run from the ticker after the worlds, and a function can act in another dimension. Slice 9 treats a function as an input to the world it runs in and a cross-world command as a message. Confirm against Bedrock when a function runs relative to the tick.</li><li>The handler lanes were assigned from each handler&#39;s direct calls and not from a full trace. Slice 1 reviews the 15 readers and the <code>Disconnect</code> handler one by one. Done by D2a to D2g, which each traced their handlers through what the handlers call; <code>Disconnect</code> moved to the connection lane in D2c.</li></ul></div>`,1)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};